\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  -Yen weakens vs dollar on talk of more policy easing\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2016\par 21:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Yen could see major reversal -analyst\par \par \uc2 * Fed officials in focus in thin trading (Adds comments, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 12 (Reuters) - The yen fell to a two-week low against the dollar on Thursday, pressured by speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could expand its monetary stimulus as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has surged more than 9 percent against the dollar in 2016 despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's series of monetary easing measures. That has fuelled \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk from Japanese officials, resulting in a 1.7 percent drop in the value of the yen so far this week.\par \par \uc2 Talk of more action increased after prominent Japanese academic Takatoshi Ito said on Thursday Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may expand monetary stimulus either in June or July. Ito has close ties to BOJ Governor Haruhiko Kuroda.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Kuroda said the BOJ won't hesitate to take further easing steps if necessary, adding that there were still large downside risks to Japan's economy. The BOJ introduced negative interest rates earlier this year, but that has had little impact on the yen or economic data.\par \par \uc2 "I foresee a major reversal on the yen," said Juan Perez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Tempus Consulting in Washington, adding that he believes the BOJ will act aggressively at its next policy meeting in June.\par \par \uc2 "In the second half of the year, we could see dollar/yen go back to between 120-125 yen."\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.6 percent to 109.06 yen in late trading, after earlier hitting a two-week peak of 109.39 yen and recovering from a 18-month low of 105.55 yen on May 3. That low was struck after the BOJ held off from expanding its monetary stimulus in late April.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials, meanwhile, took center stage on Thursday, more or less voicing willingness to continue on a rate-hiking path. Cleveland Fed President \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2 , for instance, said even though there is uncertainty in the Fed's economic forecasting, that should not stop it from taking monetary policy decisions. She added that inflation measures have moved higher.\par \par \uc2 In another event, Boston Fed President \uc2 Eric Rosengren\uc2  said the Fed should raise rates again if second-quarter data confirms that the U.S. labor market is near full strength and inflation is on track to accelerate.\par \par \uc2 Despite these Fed officials' less cautious stance on rates, the market remained skeptical, with just one rate hike expected this year.\par \par \uc2 "The (U.S.) data has simply not supported an aggressive approach to hiking rates this year," said Marvin Loh, market strategist, at BNY Mellon in Boston.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index rose 0.4 percent to 94.171. (Reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Editing by Paul Simao and Nick Zieminski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-12T20:49:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160512ec5c01y7u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
