\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  -Dollar rises vs yen on position squaring, BOJ easing talk\b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2016\par 08:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar/yen regains some ground after Wednesday's fall\par \par \uc2 * Short-covering supports dollar/yen -traders\par \par \uc2 * Japanese academic says BOJ likely to ease in June or July (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 12 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against the yen on Thursday due to position squaring, getting an added lift after a Japanese academic said the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  was likely to expand its monetary stimulus as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up 0.5 percent to 108.97 yen. On Wednesday, the dollar had lost steam after touching a two-week high of 109.38 yen and ended up falling 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Some traders who sold the dollar in its recent bounce seemed to be buying it back now, said a trader for a Japanese bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 The dollar added to its gains versus the yen after Takatoshi Ito said the BOJ is likely to expand monetary stimulus either in June or July. Ito is a prominent academic with close ties to Bank of Japan Governor Haruhiko Kuroda.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Kuroda said the BOJ won't hesitate to take further easing steps if necessary, adding that there were still large downside risks to Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had set an 18-month low of 105.55 yen on May 3, having slid after the BOJ held off from expanding its monetary stimulus at its policy meeting in late April.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has since regained some footing as traders cut their bullish bets on the yen following a series of warnings from Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  that the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any excessive one-sided gains.\par \par \uc2 But analysts believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before it hosts a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  meeting this month, even though Tokyo is clearly unhappy with the yen's rise of more than 10 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "For dollar/yen it would appear that it is now caught in nervous range trade around 105 to 110," said Heng Koon How, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Credit Suisse Private Banking Asia Pacific.\par \par \uc2 "It is likely that Tokyo is still trying to build consensus and agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  both internationally and domestically," Heng said.\par \par \uc2 Against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar edged up 0.1 percent to 93.898, but remained below Tuesday's high at 94.356, which represented a 2.7 percent recovery from its 16-month trough hit earlier in the month.\par \par \uc2 The euro held steady at $1.1424.\par \par \uc2 While the dollar has been supported by a recovery in risk appetite in financial markets, doubts linger over the strength of the global economy with political risks in many places seen as hampering companies' investment plans.\par \par \uc2 "It is not clear how strong the U.S. economy will be in the April-June quarter. It will be better than the first quarter but it doesn't look so strong," said Makoto Noji, senior strategist at \uc2 SMBC Nikko Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. economic growth slowed to 0.5 percent in the first quarter, with the strength of the dollar late last year seen as a factor weighing on the economy. That has also supported expectations that the Fed may delay rate increases this year, further hampering dollar bulls.\par \par \uc2 Sterling\uc2  remains on the defensive as the latest surveys show the "Brexit" referendum on Britain's continued membership in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  on June 23 is still too close to call.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's monetary policy committee releases updated growth and inflation forecasts in a quarterly report on Thursday but uncertainty over Brexit is likely to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extra cautious.\par \par \uc2 The pound held steady at $1.4443, not far from its two-week low of $1.4375 touched on Monday. (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano in Tokyo; Editing by Eric Meijer and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-12T07:30:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160512ec5c00j34\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
