\par\par \b \uc2 What  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Needs to Make Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Work -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos   \par \uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2016\par 04:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Decisiveness does not equal effectiveness. This is the conundrum facing Japan over what to do with its stubbornly strong yen.\par \par \uc2 For the second time this week, Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, after the yen has strengthened more than 10% against the dollar this year. He made the comments to be "decisive and prevent it from advancing further in that direction."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aso has managed to nudge the yen a bit weaker, recently trading at 109 per dollar. But this is likely temporary. Ironically, the best course isn't Mr. Aso following through on his threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's history of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  going back to the 1980s show success isn't easy to assess. There is an unknowable counterfactual nature to the exercise. If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would the yen have been that much stronger (or weaker in some cases)?\par \par \uc2 But there are lessons. Uncoordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes it alone without the help of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and other big central banks supporting the trades, tends to provide a fleeting effect. The U.S. seems in no mood to help this time around. And even with other central banks' help, as in the post-2011 earthquake and tsunami \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , turning the yen's direction around, rather than just slowing its ascent, is a tough task.\par \par \uc2 The one weapon that has been proven to weaken the yen is BOJ easing in the form of asset purchases. (Negative rates, introduced in January, seem not to have had the same effect.) It was the lack of a BOJ move at its last meeting in April, in fact, that caused the most recent leg up for the yen. In a way, Mr. Aso, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officially controls yen policy, is acting out because the BOJ elected not to.\par \par \uc2 Obsession about the yen isn't unwarranted. With massive monetary easing having already flattened borrowing costs below zero even for long term debt, a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be one of the few things left to support growth.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Aso, and his boss, Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , do have another tool at their disposal: fiscal policy. Most specifically, abandoning a planned 2017 increase in Japan's value-added tax would probably do the most to lift sentiment, weakening the yen again.\par \par \uc2 The best case scenario for those betting on a weak yen, and its twin sister, a rising stock market, is that Mr. Aso's threats are really just a delaying tactic. The tax decision could come by the end of the month. And the BOJ meets again in June and July, when a further slug of easing may yet be in store. In 2014, a rapid fire BOJ easing move followed by a delay in the same tax sent the yen weaker for the better part of the following 14 months.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only dish on the menu, however, then a weaker yen is off the table.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Frangos at alex.frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 11-05-16 0247GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160511ec5b00030\par