\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if yen firms to 90-95/dlr - PM's adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * U.S. is worried about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Hamada\par \par \uc2 * BOJ can wait and see impact from negative rate policy\par \par \uc2 * Sales tax increase next April will entail risks (Adds further comments, details)\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko and Sumio Ito\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the yen strengthens to 90-95 per dollar, even if that upsets the United States, a key economic adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said on Tuesday, asserting the right to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Koichi Hamada, an emeritus professor of economics at Yale University and special adviser to the Cabinet, told Reuters that he had recently heard "many concerns" from economists and officials in the United States about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - something it last did five years ago when it acted to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "In case the yen happens to be so firm that it becomes between 90-95 yen per dollar, then \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it angers the United States," Hamada said. "I don't think Japan would be able to wait."\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed to an 18-month high at 105.55 yen against the dollar last week after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  kept monetary policy unchanged. The dollar was around 109.20 yen on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hamada said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not need to undertake further monetary easing for a while and can wait to gauge the effects from negative interest rates it adopted in February.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in 2011, when they sold yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Tokyo got the consent of G7 countries to stem a spike in the yen driven by speculation that a devastating earthquake would force Japanese insurers to repatriate overseas funds to pay for damage claims.\par \par \uc2 "If verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone works, that is the best. But if it is repeated many times, it will become ineffective," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 "Japan can warn markets that the nation has a right to stem volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ held off from expanding monetary stimulus last month, defying market expectations for action even as soft global demand, an unwelcome rise in the yen and weak consumption threatened to derail a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ's monetary policy is working fairly well and the job market hasn't deteriorated, so I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can continue the current monetary policy," Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 Asked about "helicopter money" - underwriting government debt so the money can go directly to citizens - Hamada said such policy would lack safeguards against inflation. However, he would not oppose postponing a sales tax hike or cutting the tax and keeping the current monetary policy, which would be equivalent to the government distributing cash to consumers.\par \par \uc2 He also said Japan's economic fundamentals were in good shape and the logic behind "Abenomics" remains sound, but it was risky to raise the sales tax as planned next April to 10 percent when conditions in the economy are rocky.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Chang-Ran Kim; Editing by William Mallard and Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-13T11:10:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160510ec5a010kp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
