\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Japan's line in sand over yen is a short-term fix\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 08:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \par \par \uc2 By Quentin Webb\par \par \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 10 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Japan is smarting as the yen rebounds. So finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  has stepped up the rhetoric about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, explicitly mentioning "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " two days running. If it comes to it, actually stepping in to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy dollars would be a firmer way to get speculators to back off. But even that would be just a short-term fix.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Graphic: Japanese yen against the dollar: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/1qapawM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/1qapawM}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in Tokyo on May 10, the yen stood at about 108.7 against the dollar - roughly midway between the uncomfortably strong 85, where it traded when Premier \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  took office in December 2012, and last year's weak point above 125.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The rebound is bad for exporters and unhelpful for a country that would like to import inflation. Genuine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, is probably some way off - the yen would probably have to break through 100 before \uc2 Japan's finance ministry\uc2  started dumping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The average Japanese company becomes loss-making if the rate falls below 103.2, official data cited by \uc2 UBS\uc2  shows. Nor has the complaining reached maximum volume. Aso talks about "one-sided" movements. He has noted the Group of Twenty nations agrees that overly choppy or disorderly moves are bad. But he is not - yet - describing the yen's move as "disorderly".\par \par \par \par \uc2 The United States is not onboard either: it says the yen moves are orderly, and has added Japan to a list of countries whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies it monitors. Tensions are evidently mounting between the two sides. But for Japan, a G7 member, to move unilaterally, would mark a big breakdown in economic diplomacy - especially as it gears up to host a meeting of the seven developed nations later this month.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Suppose, though, that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did follow a further spike. That would show markets the limits to Japan's tolerance. But such a line in the sand would only be temporary. That is because yen strength is partly about a flight to safety - Japan is not flirting with electing Donald Trump as president or leaving the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , nor does it suffer when commodities are cheap. The rally also reflects the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's recent inaction, in defiance of market expectations. That fed the suspicion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is running short of effective ways to make monetary policy even easier, thus tamping down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Neither problem is fixed simply by buying dollars.\par \par \par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/qtwebb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/qtwebb}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \par \par \uc2 - Japan is determined to prevent recent "one-sided" yen rises from accelerating, Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said on May 10, reiterating his resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if yen gains last long enough to hurt a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \par \par \uc2 - Aso told parliament that Japan has not and has no plans to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves on a long-term basis to give its exports a competitive trade advantage. But he added that it was a shared understanding among G7 and G20 nations that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility was undesirable.\par \par \par \par \uc2 - The yen was bid at 108.72 per dollar by 12.55 p.m. Tokyo time (GMT 0355). It hit an 18-month low of 105.55 last week.\par \par \par \par \uc2 - Reuters: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if 'one-sided' yen moves persist - Aso\par \par \par \par \uc2 - Graphic: Japanese yen against the dollar: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/1qapawM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/1qapawM}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 RELATED COLUMNS\par \par \par \par \uc2 Rest easy\par \par \par \par \uc2 Heated exchange\par \par \par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-13T06:13:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160510ec5a00ct5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
