\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-World stock markets rise while yen falls back\b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 13:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European shares up, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  climbs after earnings\par \par \uc2 * Japan reiterates readiness to weaken yen\par \par \uc2 * Greek market rises on prospect of debt relief\par \par \uc2 By Sudip Kar-Gupta\par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 10 (Reuters) - World stock markets rose on Tuesday, helped by solid corporate earnings in Europe, progress on Greek debt talks, and a new pledge by Japan that it was prepared to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MSCI All-Country World index climbed 0.4 percent, the pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 index advanced 1.3 percent, while the \uc2 MSCI\uc2  Emerging Market index also edged higher.\par \par \uc2 U.S. equity index futures rose around 0.5 percent and oil prices also climbed, driven by supply disruptions in Canada and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 European stock markets built on positive momentum from earlier on in Japan, where the Nikkei rose 2.2 percent after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  reiterated his resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen's gains last long enough to hurt Japan's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Aso's comments sent the yen to its lowest level in almost two weeks against the dollar, and reinforced the backdrop of central banks around the world looking for ways to boost the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Hampstead Capital hedge fund manager Lex Van Dam said record low interest rates from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant equities still offered more attractive returns than cash or bonds, while Clairinvest fund manager Ion-Marc Valahu added that he had bought up European equity positions over the last week.\par \par \uc2 "Rates are not going anywhere, so buying any dips on the stock market might still be the best strategy," said Van Dam.\par \par \uc2 European equities were also helped by some decent corporate results.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  rose after the Swiss bank reported a smaller-than-expected first quarter loss, while jewellery maker Pandora surged after posting higher profits and raising its financial outlook.\par \par \uc2 Greek shares hit their highest level in 2016 after euro zone finance ministers offered to grant Greece some debt relief, with the move causing Greek 10-year bond yields to fall below 8 percent for the first time in more than six months.\par \par \uc2 The offer appears to be a compromise between Germany, which does not believe Greece needs additional debt relief, and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , which insists it is necessary, and will be fleshed out by deputy finance ministers by May 24.\par \par \uc2 "At the very least it appears the gap between the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and the Germans appears to be narrowing and that has been very well received by investors," said Nick Stamenkovic, bond strategist at RIA Capital Markets. (Additional reporting by Anirban Nag and John Geddie in London, and Saikat Chatterjee in Hong Kong; Editing by Robin Pomeroy) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-10T12:11:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160510ec5a010i8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
