\par\par \b \uc2 China Outlook for Weak Growth, Little Stimulus Hurt HK Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 03:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares in Hong Kong look to be headed lower as investors await the release of China inflation data Tuesday morning. Investors are watching this batch of data as the latest indicator of the economy and how Beijing might react with stimulus; yesterday China shares fell sharply amid gloom about the country's growth prospects. Nevertheless, investors are dialling back their expectations for more stimulus. "We are reviewing our forecast of two 25 basis point \uc2 PBOC\uc2  policy rate cuts by year-end for downward revision," wrote Tim Condon, ING strategist, in a morning note. Falling oil prices overnight could pressure Hong Kong's energy shares, as they are already weighing on the energy-heavy Australian S&P \uc2 ASX\uc2  200. Futures for the Hang Seng Index were last down 0.5%. China's April money and credit data are also due, sometime from today to Sunday. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Oil prices edge lower in early Asian trade Tuesday on expectation that threats on oil output from Canada's wildfire are subsiding, putting crude back under pressure, analysts say. "Data also weighed on sentiment after market intelligence firm Genscape issued its weekly report on inventories at delivery hub Cushing, Oklahoma that stated a build of 1.4 million barrels," says Stuart Ive, a client manager at OM Financial. Weekly U.S. crude inventory and production data is scheduled for release on Wednesday. \uc2 Nymex\uc2  is down 27 cents at $43.17 a barrel, Brent is down 11 cents at $43.52 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippine peso opened weaker to the U.S. dollar at 47.20 Tuesday but then clawed its way back up, as results of the presidential election streamed in, pointing to a clear victory for candidate Rodrigo Duterte. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had slipped in the offshore market Monday while the local dealers were closed for the polling session, partly due to broad U.S. dollar strength. The election of the controversy-prone Rodrigo Duterte frets economists, due to his unclear policy stance and economic agenda. The possibility that the previous administration's economic plans will be upended is likely unnerving to foreign investors, and could lead to short-term capital flight that would weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The peso is now at 47.10, slightly stronger than the implied spot price of 47.23 last traded in the offshore market, and nearly unchanged from its Friday closing of 47.09. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0111 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's latest data on retail sales point to a steady improvement in consumer sentiment following a turnaround in March. Sales at the country's top department stores rose 8% in April from a year earlier versus the previous month's 4.8% gain, according to preliminary data in the finance ministry's green book report. Sales at Korea's leading discount-store chains increased 0.2% in April, turning around from a 0.7% contraction in March. The report also says passenger-car sales at home rose 5.8% on year in April. The trade ministry releases April's confirmed retail-sales data later this month. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0059 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks are up slightly on the back of the dollar's modest recovery against the yen, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. Healthy U.S. wage inflation data on Friday and the threat of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Monday triggered some recovery in the dollar against the yen. Mr. Akino says he isn't convinced that stocks will rise further based merely on the yen moves. Among individual stocks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7011.TO) is down 3.6% at 388.0 yen after the company said Monday it was demanding a payment of Y379 billion (about $3.5 billion) from Hitachi (6501.TO), its partner in a power-systems joint venture, in a dispute over a project in South Africa. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hitachi\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 4.1% at Y464.0. The Nikkei is up 0.5% at 16294.85. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TRL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0053 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares in Asia were mixed Tuesday morning after energy shares fell overnight in U.S. stocks following a drop in oil prices. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was up 0.4%, Korea's Kospi was up 0.1%, and Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.4%. Unstable oil prices and worries about the glut of crude could weigh on Asian markets. "Commodities are set for a tough time in a stronger U.S. dollar environment, but news that China may be pulling back on its aggressive first-quarter stimulus is also driving prices lower," says brokerage firm IG in a morning note. Brent was recently trading at $43.37 per barrel. Traders are also expecting fresh China credit and inflation data before mainland markets open.(dominique.fong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0054 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (7011.TO) are down 3.6% at 388.0 Japanese yen after the company said Monday it was demanding a payment of Y379 billion (about $3.5 billion) from Hitachi (6501.TO), its partner in a power-systems joint venture, in a dispute over a project in South Africa. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hitachi\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it wouldn't accept \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's demand. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy\f28 \uc2 \f2  said part of the payment it expects to receive from Hitachi is recorded on its balance sheet as a liquid asset. "That could lead to a loss. You don't know if they'll get the amount fully," says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. Mr. Akino says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's earnings projections announced during Monday trading initially made a good impression, but the forecast might not be as conservative as it should be. In addition to the dispute with Hitachi, progress at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Heavy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's regional jet business is another source of concern, Mr. Akino says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hitachi\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 4.1% at Y464.0. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TRL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0050 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit, now at 4.0500 to the U.S. dollar, has fallen to its weakest level in six weeks. Tuesday's significant rally of the U.S. dollar across Asia has whittled the ringgit's value by 1.0% within the first hour of trading. The catalyst for the dollar's rise was unclear, say traders, but the yen's sharp drop may have something to do with it. The minutes of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's last policy meeting, released yesterday, revealed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen is still being considered, in spite of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  report placing Japan on a shortlist of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulators. The rise of the U.S. dollar in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets has impacted commodities as well. Oil and gold prices, both denominated in U.S. dollars, fell. Lower oil prices act as a double whammy for oil-exporting Malaysia's ringgit, due to its adverse effect on the nation's revenues and trade balance. USD/MYR is now 4.0500 from its Monday closing of 4.0100. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0046 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY may find it difficult to break above the 110-mark, even with Japan Finance Minister's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2 's jawboning yesterday to stem the JPY strength, says Osamu Takashima, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citigroup Global Markets Japan in a morning note. Aso's comments--"prepared to undertake \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," if the yen rises further and sharply--prompted JPY selling to some extent. But due to a lack of a virtuous cycle in which higher Tokyo stocks and JPY weakness benefit each other, and given Japanese exporters' USD selling expected around Y110, the USD/JPY can only extend its gains to the Y109-level, says Takashima. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0034 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] In a fresh sign of trouble for Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 , whose growth-revival plan has centered on weakening the yen against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Donald Trump has made it clear that a strong dollar is not in his interest. "If the value of the dollar goes up, it would be at this point not a very good thing," Trump said in an interview Monday with The Wall Street Journal. The remark comes just as Tokyo escalates its efforts to arrest the yen's rise versus the greenback. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Japan's finance minister and Abe's deputy, managed to lift USD/JPY Monday by scaring away speculators by hinting strongly at the possibility of directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. All this means that if the presumptive Republican presidential nominee does make his way into the White House, tension between the U.S. and Japan over exchange rates would only further grow.(takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0027 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has upgraded its earnings forecasts for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fletcher Building\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FBU.NZ) on the back of New Zealand's strong economic outlook. "New Zealand economic indicators remain robust: employment levels are high, migration flows are high, the level of housing underbuild remains high," the broker says. "We believe these factors and more should underpin continued strong construction demand in New Zealand." Deutsche increased its FY16 underlying net profit forecast by 0.8% to NZ$421 million, and by 0.7% for FY17 to NZ$476 million. It has increased its underlying ebit forecasts for FY16 and FY17 by 0.9% and 1.1% to NZ$691 million and NZ$766 million, respectively. FBU last traded at NZ$8.48, well below Deutsche's revised NZ$10.31/share price target. (kate.geenty@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9 May 2016 21:26 ET \par China Outlook for Weak Growth, Little Stimulus -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0026 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's won tumbled sharply Tuesday, following the weakening of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen on rehashed expectations of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The USD/KRW pair jumped to 1,175.7, a nearly two-month high, from a Monday closing of 1,165.8, representing a 0.8% drop for the won against the dollar. The move has taken USD/KRW just above the 200-day moving average resistance line, a long-term chart barrier, at 1,174.4. If the pair ends the day above that line, the likelihood of won weakness against the dollar in the medium term will increase. The won has a strong trade-linked correlation with Japan's yen, which fell as the market revisited bets on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The minutes of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's last policy meeting revealed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still being considered, in spite of a \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  report placing Japan on a shortlist of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulators. USD/KRW is now 1,175.0 from its Monday closing of 1,165.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 09, 2016 21:26 ET (01:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160510ec5a00071\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
