\par\par \b \uc2 Possible IAG Capital Return Supports Macquarie View -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Macquarie says that among Australia's general insurers, relative valuation supports its preference for Suncorp (SUN.AU) over \uc2 Insurance Australia Group\uc2  (IAG.AU). Still, the potential for a materials return of capital to shareholders supports a neutral call on IAG's shares. The brokerages target on IAG rises to A$5.60 from A$5.30 to reflect medium-term modeling changes for the insurance margin. IAG was last at A$6. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar remain under pressure, as resurging worries over China's economic health continued to rattle equity investors and led to a global selloff in resources overnight. The AUD/USD fell 0.8% overnight to as low as US$0.7308, from US$0.7370 in the London morning, reflecting hefty falls in the prices for gold, iron ore, copper and oil. "There was little data of note but an overall nervous global mood," says Sean Callow, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Sydney. AUD/USD was last trading at 0.7320 Tuesday morning in Asia. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Bell Potter is backing the management team who turned Sirius Resources from a mining minnow into a more than A$1 billion takeover target to do it all again with their newly established venture S2 Resources (S2R.AU). S2 Resources, which was spun out of Independence Group (IGO.AU) following its takeover of Sirius, is focused on gold and nickel deposits in Western Australia and Scandinavia. It recently established a maiden mineral resource at its Baloo gold prospect in Western Australia, and Bell Potter thinks it could be rapidly developed at a modest cost to provide cash flow for further exploration. The broker starts S2 Resources at buy with a A$0.52/share price target. S2R last traded at A$0.29. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Expectations that the Australian dollar could fall to fresh lows between US$0.71 and US$0.72 this week are great news for companies that typically benefit from a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --such as tourism-related stocks, exporters and firms generating a significant chunk of earnings in U.S. dollars, says IG. Shares of \uc2 Sydney Airport (SYD.AU) \uc2 have already risen 5.7% since last Monday in anticipation of further growth in international visitor numbers. Hotel operator Mantra Group (MTR.AU) has rebounded 6.7% over the same period, after steep falls in April. Sydney Airport was trading at A$7.20 before markets opened Tuesday. Mantra was at A$3.95. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] It's time to switch off electronics circuit-board design software company Altium (ALU.AU) temporarily after its share jumped by more than 75% since late August, says Bell Potter. "We like the company and the story a lot but, as with all stocks, valuation is the key driver of our recommendation," the broker says. It drops Altium to sell from hold, even as its price target moves up 9.1% to A$6.00/share. ALU last traded at A$6.57. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The New South Wales government's plan to introduce a container deposit scheme, whereby drink bottles and cans can be redeemed for 10 cents each at depots and reverse vending machines, will reduce litter but increase costs for beverage companies such as \uc2 Coca-Cola Amatil\uc2  (CCL.AU), says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "If Amatil passes through all costs, there is likely to be a volume response," says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "The one consolation for Amatil, is this scheme would have a greater impact on lower priced products, especially private label, compared to Amatil's premium priced products." (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japanese stocks may be supported by the yen's weakening against the dollar. Healthy U.S. wage inflation data on Friday and the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  on Monday appear to be driving the yen's latest decline. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 16275 on Monday, compared to an earlier close in Osaka at 16220. The Nikkei ended up 0.7% at 16216.03. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Bell Potter shifts to a buy recommendation on \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  (CBA.AU) from hold previously following what it says was a solid third-quarter trading update. It trims its target on the shares by A$2 to A$81.50, still ahead of the A$75.78 where they last traded. The brokerage says the rise in net profit and cash earnings for the latest quarter marked a good outcome given a higher bad-debt charge, with strong 6% operating income growth, a stable net interest margin and maintained lending momentum. Bell Potter's earnings estimates are largely unchanged. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Australian dollar is headed for fresh three-month lows after breaking through a key support level at US$0.7350 overnight, says IG. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 5.4% since the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates by 25 basis points to 1.75% last Tuesday. It was last trading at US$0.7315 Tuesday morning in Asia. IG's technical charts suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has entered a downtrend, which would take it to between US$0.71 and US$0.72 this week. "On a technical basis, the Aussie looks like it has plenty of room to fall further," says IG. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says it understands why \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  (AIR.NZ) decided to settle a class action claim for alleged cargo price fixing in the U.S., which has been ongoing since 2006. \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  settled for US$35 million but didn't admit any wrongdoing. Deutsche says under U.S. law the anti-trust element of the case could have seen \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  liable for up to three times the entire US$2.66 billion damages claimed in total. "This amount, or any meaningful proportion of it, represents a staggering amount (\uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2 's market capitalization is about NZ$2.6 billion)," says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "We understand why \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2 's board would elect for a settlement, irrespective of the probability of the claim being successful." Deutsche has a hold rating on the airline and a target price of NZ$2.89. The stock last traded at NZ$2.285. (kate.geenty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY (now at 108.40) will likely move in a 107.80-108.80 range during Asia trade, amid short covering of the USD, says Akira Moroga, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  products group at \uc2 Aozora Bank\uc2 . The USD is bought after Japan Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2 's jawboning to stem the JPY strength. And with buying from Japanese corporate players kicking in from earlier this week, "I think the dollar is now creating a new Y105-Y110 range," Mr. Moroga says. But due to USD selling expected from Japanese exporters around Y110, the USD/JPY may struggle to go upside. China data such as April CPI (due at 0130 GMT) are in focus. The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.1350-1.1420 range and is now at 1.1380. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2251 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australian shares look set for a soft start to the day, with falling commodity prices weighing on resources stocks. IG forecasts the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 will open 0.1% lower, after rising 0.5% on Monday to 5320.7 thanks to gains by bank stocks. "Energy stocks and the materials sectors are clearly going to weigh down the index so the banking and financial sectors are going to have to do some heavy lifting today to halt a major decline in the index," says IG market analyst Angus Nicholson. Overnight, iron-ore prices fell sharply, copper futures declined in London, and U.S. and global oil benchmarks pulled back. Ahead of the open, explosives and mining services company \uc2 Incitec Pivot\uc2  (IPL.AU) reported a 79% fall in half-year profit. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 09, 2016 19:59 ET (23:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160509ec59004j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
