\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Yen tumbles after Tokyo warning; oil prices fall\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2016\par 17:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Yen falls after finance minister says could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * U.S. stock indexes mixed, dragged down by energy sector\par \par \uc2 * European shares led by Germany's DAX after strong orders\par \par \uc2 * U.S. yields fall as rate hike seen more distant (Updates with open of U.S. markets)\par \par \uc2 By Lewis Krauskopf\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 9 (Reuters) - The yen tumbled against the U.S. dollar on Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled it was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, while a drop in oil prices undercut stocks.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock indexes were little changed, as the energy sector dragged. Europe's broad stock index gained as data showed German industrial orders rose more than expected in March.\par \par \uc2 Most \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields fell as investors lowered estimates that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates in June, after a weaker-than-expected jobs report.\par \par \uc2 "Today, really you're seeing just that back-and-forth that has been here for months: very tight ranges, modest movements and just a mix between uncertainty and having such low interest rates that there isn't somewhere else to go," said Rick Meckler, president of LibertyView Capital Management in Jersey City, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 "There isn't a lot of conviction on the part of global investors for any of the asset classes," Meckler said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average was down 69.3 points, or 0.39 percent, at 17,671.33, the S&P 500 was losing 2.51 points, or 0.12 percent, at 2,054.63 and the Nasdaq Composite was adding 6.48 points, or 0.14 percent, at 4,742.63.\par \par \uc2 Europe's broad FTSEurofirst 300 index gained 0.4 percent. Germany's DAX climbed 1.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's world equity index slipped 0.2 percent after posting its worst weekly performance since mid-February last week.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar gained 1.2 percent against the yen, after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week hit a 1-1/2 year high against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said Tokyo is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if yen moves are volatile enough to hurt the country's trade and economy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a risk of either \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or BOJ (\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) monetary policy easing in the months ahead, a risk that is, at least for now, keeping the yen's upside limited," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar gained 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell as traders took in their stride the impact of wildfires on Canada's oil output and after another inventory build at the U.S. hub for crude futures delivery.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude dropped 2.3 percent to $43.62 a barrel, while benchmark Brent dropped 3.3 percent to $43.88 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields fell as investors evaluated when the Fed is likely to raise rates.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year notes were last up 5/32 in price to yield 1.7613 percent, down from 1.777 percent late on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's top banks have all but abandoned any expectation that the Fed will raise interest rates in June, and most now see the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next rate hike coming in September, according to a Reuters survey conducted on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Copper prices fell 2.1 percent as weak trade data from China highlighted poor demand growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss, Karen Brettell and Barani Krishnan in New York, Patrick Graham, Pratima Desai and Sudip Kar Gupta in London; Editing by Nick Zieminski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-9T16:40:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160509ec5901ler\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
