\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Financial Services News of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2016\par 16:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FINANCIAL SERVICES TOP STORIES \par  \par LENDINGCLUB CEO RESIGNS FOLLOWING  LOAN SALES REVIEW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The CEO of the leading company in the burgeoning "peer-to-peer" lending industry resigned Monday following a board investigation into what it called improper practices in the lending process. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FED SHOULD AIM FOR INFLATION OVERSHOOT, SAYS EVANS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Top Federal Reserve official \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Evans\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should consider allowing inflation to temporarily rise above its 2% annual target. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER MAKES FIRST DIRECT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREAT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said he is "prepared to undertake \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the yen rises further and sharply, spurning speculation that U.S. opposition would constrain Tokyo's actions. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOJ MINUTES UNDERSCORE SPLIT VIEWS ON NEGATIVE RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials expressed worries over signs of "adverse effects" of negative interest rates at their March policy meeting, underlining a sharp split among central bankers over the policy measure. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO OFFER 'NEUTRAL, APPROPRIATE' MONETARY SUPPORT FOR REFORM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would create a monetary environment that is "neutral and appropriate" for the country's structural reform. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WINDFALL FOR CENTRAL BANKS FUELS POLITICAL PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks are turning record profits, bolstered by income from their bulging asset and loan portfolios and, more recently, from charging banks for making deposits. But as their balance sheets grow, political pressures on central banks are intensifying. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SPOTLIGHT SWINGS TO MALAYSIA'S EXPOSURE TO 1MDB BOND DEFAULTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As investors brace for another potential default on debt issued by a troubled Malaysian government investment fund, attention is turning to the possible cost to the Malaysian government if it has to bail the fund out. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LENDINGCLUB DRAWS SKEPTICISM FROM INVESTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LendingClub\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports earnings Monday, but investors remain skeptical, with many arguing that the online lender will hit a wall once credit-market conditions deteriorate, competition grows stronger or legal barriers are put in place. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SAUDI ARABIA EYES IPOS TO RAISE CAPITAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As cheap oil constricts Saudi Arabia's capacity to spend at home, the Arab world's largest economy is pressing ahead with the selling of state assets to raise new capital and boost the private sector. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TEAMSTERS\f28 \uc2 \f2  PENSION BAILOUT REJECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department rejected a proposal to cut retirement payouts for hundreds of thousands of truck drivers, construction workers and other service personnel. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. CONSUMER CREDIT BALLOONED AT FASTEST PACE SINCE 2001 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Borrowing by U.S. consumers ballooned in March at the fastest pace in more than a decade, as credit-card debt expanded particularly quickly. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SEC SAYS FINANCIAL ADVISER DEFRAUDED PRO ATHLETES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Pittsburgh-based financial adviser who targeted professional athletes and entertainers as clients was charged with fraudulently taking roughly $2.35 million from client accounts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 09, 2016 10:04 ET (14:04 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160509ec5900253\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
