\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2016\par 12:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Threatens \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said he is "prepared to undertake \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the yen rises more and sharply, countering speculation that U.S. opposition would constrain Tokyo's actions. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ Minutes Underscore Split Views on Negative Rates \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials expressed worries over signs of "adverse effects" of negative interest rates at their March policy meeting, underlining a sharp split among central bankers over the policy measure. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Greek Lawmakers Pass Austerity Measures \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece's government hopes the approval of pension changes and tax increases will unlock bailout funds, but its key creditors remain deadlocked over the terms of the country's bailout plan. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par German Leadership Differs on Greek Debt Relief \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Vice Chancellor Sigmar Gabriel said that Greece's debt burden needed to be reduced, signaling a clash inside the \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  as key decisions loom for Athens's creditors. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Thousands Rally in Poland to Protest Government \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's main opposition parties rallied thousands of people in a protest against the country's socially conservative government and its policies they say antagonize key European partners. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Italy to Appoint Carlo Calenda as Industry Minister \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Carlo Calenda, Rome's top European Union representative, will be appointed later this week to replace Federica Guidi, who resigned two months ago over allegations of conflict of interest. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Iran Test-Fires Another Ballistic Missile \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Iran has apparently test-fired a missile with a range of 1,250 miles, the latest in a spate of tests following the implementation of the nuclear deal with world powers. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Offer 'Neutral, Appropriate' Monetary Support For Reform \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would create a monetary environment that is "neutral and appropriate" for the country's structural reform. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Japan Wage Growth Picks Up Speed in March \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall cash wages for Japanese workers rose at the fastest pace in nearly two years in March, driven by a jump in bonus payments. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Twitter Bars U.S. Intelligence Agencies From Data Service \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut off U.S. intelligence agencies from access to a service that sifts the entire output of its social-media postings, in the latest sign of tensions between Silicon Valley and the government over terrorism and privacy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  Affiliate Claims Cairo Drive-By Killings \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  Egypt claimed responsibility for a drive-by shooting that killed eight policemen in a Cairo suburb, the first attack on the capital's police forces in months. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Baghdad Attacks Kill at Least 15 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Multiple attacks in Baghdad killed at least 15 people and wounded scores more, a stark reminder of Iraq's continuing instability amid a political crisis that is heaping pressure on Prime Minister Haider al-Abadi. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Landslide in Southern China Leaves 33 Missing \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rescuers searched for 33 construction workers missing in a landslide at the site of a hydropower project following days of heavy rain in southern China. Eight other workers were pulled out alive, officials and state-run media reported. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Australia to Hold National Election in July \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malcolm Turnbull\f28 \uc2 \f2  called a July 2 poll, seeking a fresh mandate for the conservative government he took the helm of nearly eight months ago in an economy roiled by voter angst at the end of a long resources boom. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  May Cut Rates Further \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is on track to lower interest rates again in the coming months as it tries to ward off the risk of a period of prolonged deflation, economists said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 09, 2016 06:02 ET (10:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160509ec59000zb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
