\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds vs yen, slips against other major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2016\par 17:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar recovers after touching 18-month low of 106.14 yen\par \par \uc2 * Japanese officials reported frustrated with yen's strength\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Sterling\uc2  reaches 5-month highs as Brexit fears recede\par \par \uc2 * Euro hits 8-month high above $1.1500 (Updates market action, changes dateline; previous LONDON)\par \par \uc2 By Richard Leong\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 2 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Monday from its biggest weekly fall in more than seven years against the yen but softened against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in particular the euro, which was helped by stronger German manufacturing data.\par \par \uc2 Worries over Japanese policymakers' inability to stem the yen's rise pushed the dollar to an 18-month low of 106.14 yen in the first hours of Asian trade. It later bounced to 106.78 yen, up 0.4 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Taro Aso was quoted in Japanese media over the weekend as saying he viewed the yen's strength as "extremely concerning," stoking speculation the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 "It contributed to the performance of the yen, while we are seeing the dollar softer against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 Eric Viloria\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo Securities\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index dipped 0.35 percent to 92.747 after hitting its lowest since January 2015 at 92.568.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has fallen since mid-March after Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  signaled the Fed would proceed with further rate increases with "caution."\par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness intensified following the BOJ's surprise inaction on further stimulus at its policy meeting last week.\par \par \uc2 "One can only imagine that they did not want a stronger yen, but the authorities there caused it by taking no action last week," Thu Lan Nguyen, a strategist with \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Frankfurt, said of Aso's comments.\par \par \uc2 Last week's nearly 5 percent gain for the yen was its biggest since the 2008 crisis and pushed "long" bets on more gains to the highest on record. This raised the risk the dollar could weaken to 100 yen, some analysts said.\par \par \uc2 With British markets closed for a holiday on Monday, sterling reached a five-month peak versus the dollar on receding worries about the "Brexit" referendum on June 23. The pound was last up 0.3 percent at $1.4653.\par \par \uc2 Monday's data on global manufacturing supported the notion of sluggish global growth. U.S. factory growth slowed more than expected in April, while Chinese manufacturing activity expanded only marginally.\par \par \uc2 German factory data, while marginally below forecast, showed the sector grew solidly. That lifted the euro to $1.1532, the highest since late August, according to Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 An Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting will be a focus on Tuesday. The Aussie dollar was up 0.1 percent at $0.7615 on Monday, adding to an 11 percent surge since hitting multi-year lows in January. (Additional reporting by Patrick Graham in London, Hideyuki San in Tokyo; Ian Chua in Sydney; Editing by John Stonestreet and Dan Grebler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-2T16:10:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160502ec5200lzp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\