\par\par \b \uc2 IFR Markets ForexWatch Asia Regional Daily Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 3,561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2016\par 01:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SYDNEY, May 2 (IFR) - North America news and data\par  \par * US Mar Personal Income 0.4% vs. f/c 0.3% & prior 0.1% (rev from 0.2%)\par * US Mar Personal Consumption 0.1% vs. f/c 0.2% & prior 0.2% (rev from 0.1%)\par * US Mar  Core PCE price index m/m 0.1% vs/ f/c 0.1% & prior 0.2% (rev from\par 0.1%)\par * US Q1 Employment Costs as f/c at 0.6% vs. prior 0.5%\par * US Apr Chicago PMI vs. f/c 53.00 & prior 53.6\par * US Apr U. of Mich. Sentiment final 89.0 vs. f/c 90.0 & prior 89.7\par * US Apr Chicago PMI 50.4 vs. f/c 53.0 & prior 53.6\par * CA Feb GDP m/m as f/c at -0.1% vs. prior +0.6%\par * CA Feb Producer Prices m/m -0.6% vs. f/c +0.1% & prior -1.0% (rev from -1.1%)\par * CA Mar Raw Materials Prices 4.5% vs. f/c 3.7% & prior 0.7% (rev from -2.6%)\par * DBRS confirms Republic of Portugal at BBB (Low), stable trend\par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 German Federal Statistics Office\f28 \uc2 \f2  corrects Apr HICP to -0.5%m/m from previously\par reported -0.3%y/y; Corrects April HICP to -0.3% y/y from previously reported\par -0.1% y/y\par * EZ April flash inflation excluding unprocessed food & energy revised down to\par 0.7% from 0.8% after German correction\par * Fed's Kaplan: 'Will advocate' raising interest rates\par * Kaplan: Holding interest rates too low for too long could lead to bubble\par * Fed's Kaplan: Expects GDP rebound in Q2; Brexit will be a factor in Fed's\par decision in June- BBG TV\par * NY Fed Nowcast: US GDP seen growing 0.8% in Q2 vs. 1.2% April 15 estimate\par * China Politburo: To keep basic stable yuan exchange rate; Econ still faces\par relatively large downside pressure-MNSI\par * Russian Cen Bk Press Office tells Reuters that rate cut possible within next\par three setting meetings\par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2 : Hungary inflation expectations appear to be anchored around 2%, the lower\par end of CB's target- Country \par     \par News from the weekend\par  \par China Apr Manufacturing PMI 50.1 down from 50.2 in March\par * China Manufacturing PMI came in 50.1 versus expectations of 50.4\par * Despite missing expectations the PMI data still indicates manufacturing\par expanding\par * China non-Manufacturing PMI came in at 53.5 down from 53.8 in March\par  \par China PMI data released on the weekend indicated the stronger activity in March\par has stabilized rather than maintaining upward momentum. The markets haven't been\par reacting to China data as much over the past couple of months and while the\par manufacturing PMI missed expectations, it is still expanding - albeit at a\par slower pace. The non-manufacturing PMI slipped slightly, but still remains\par robust at 53.5. It is unlikely the data will spark a sustained negative reaction\par when the markets open on Monday morning.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par  \par South Korea trade slump deepens in April - exports to China worst in 3 months\par * South Korea Trade data released on Sunday came in a bit worse than expected\par * April exports fell 11.2% versus minus 11% expected and 8.1% in March\par * April imports fell 14.9% versus expectations of minus 12.5% and minus 13.9% in\par March\par * Exports to China fell 18.4% - the worst in 3 months\par  \par South Korean trade data came in a bit worse than expected as exports to China,\par the US and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  slumped. Improvement isn't expected anytime soon as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 South\par Korean Trade Ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a statement saying that May export conditions "are\par not optimistic". Data from around the globe in recent weeks has pointed to\par global growth expanding at a slower pace. If the trend continues it will not\par bode well for economies that are dependent on exports. John.Noonan@tr.com\par  \par Japan FinMin Aso says US monitoring will not stop Japan from acting on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kyodo\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  \par According to a Reuters report - Japanese Finance Minister Aso told \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kyodo\f28 \uc2 \f2  news\par that Japan will act if needed if "one sided \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speculation" continues. On\par Friday Japan along with China, South Korea and Taiwan were placed on a US\par "monitoring" list under provisions of a trade bill passed in Congress this year.\par Local Japanese media speculated that the US monitoring list could be a factor\par behind further JPY strength - as it may limit what Japanese authorities could do\par and say if and when the JPY strengthens to uncomfortable levels. The Japanese\par Finance Minister may have felt compelled to offset that contention. The MOF will\par have a difficult time justifying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  based on high speculation alone.\par The short-term market was caught short the JPY when the BOJ surprised by taking\par no further easing action on Thursday. The latest IMM data shows speculative JPY\par longs at 66.5 K contracts. While on the high side - it was nowhere the size of\par net JPY shorts that were held from the huge run higher in the USD/JPY from 2012\par through 2015. The USD/JPY will be vulnerable next week due to Golden Week\par holidays in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - so verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might start to increase from Japanese\par officials.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par The week ahead - RBA in focus before key US non-farm payrolls\par  \par Central banks\par The key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  event in the week ahead will be the RBA decision on\par Tuesday. According to market pricing there is better than a 50% chance the RBA\par will cut the cash rate 25 BPs to 1.75% and it is likely most in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par believe a cut is the most likely outcome. Economists still favour the RBA\par remaining on hold with the latest Reuters polls showing only 11 out of 48\par economists predicting a 25 BP cut on Tuesday. Only \f28 \uc2 \f2 NAB\f28 \uc2 \f2  of the four Australian\par majors sees the RBA cutting on Tuesday. See the "Wrap-up" for more RBA\par commentary and opinions. The RBA Statement on Monetary Policy (SOMP) will be\par released on Friday and the RBA will surely lower their inflation forecasts.\par  \par Key data in the week ahead\par  \par It will be a very busy week for global data with the US jobs report on Friday\par the most important. Other US data released in the week ahead includes ISM\par Manufacturing and Construction Spending on Monday, ADP US jobs, Durable Goods,\par Factory Orders, non-manufacturing ISM and US Trade on Wednesday. The key events\par in the Euro zone will be EZ MFG PMI out on Monday followed on Wednesday by\par services PMI and Retail Sales. UK manufacturing PMI is out on Tuesday followed\par on Wednesday by services PMI. China will release Caixin MFG PMI on Monday;\par Caixin non-MFG PMI will be released on Thursday while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves will be\par released on Friday. In Australia the TD/MI Inflation gauge will be released on\par Monday; Building Approvals will be out Tuesday along with the Australian Federal\par Budget; Retail Sales and Australian trades will be released Thursday.\par  John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par Best to worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week\par CCY   APR022       APR29      %Change\par JPY   111.80      106.45      4.79%\par EUR   1.1222      1.1455      2.08%\par CHF   0.9782      0.9595      1.91%\par NZD   0.6852      0.6976      1.81%\par GBP   1.4399      1.4608      1.45%\par CAD   1.2671      1.2555      0.92%\par AUD   0.7715      0.7603      -1.45%\par     \par Themes for Friday's trading\par  \par * The main themes from Friday's trading were:\par  \par 1) Increased US and global growth concerns following another batch of weak US\par data and disappointing earnings results from US companies\par  \par 2) Broad USD weakness (led by USD/JPY once again) as expectations of a Fed hike\par further faded following the weak US data\par  \par 3) The weak US dollar continues to underpin key commodities despite the US\par growth concerns. This was especially true with gold, as USD weakness encourages\par the gold buyers to come out of the woodwork\par  \par 4) Big falls in Euro zone equities due to deflation concerns caused by the\par strengthening EUR/USD - that added to the concerns following the weak EZ\par inflation data released on Friday\par  \par * US data was disappointing on Friday and added to US growth concerns that\par intensified in the wake of the weak US GDP release on Thursday.\par  \par * Univ of Mich consumer sentiment came in at 89 versus expectations of 90 while\par the Fed's favorite inflation gauge, core-PCE price index eased to plus 1.6% Y/Y\par from 1.7% in February.\par  \par * Wall Street closed lower after very weak leads from Europe and Japan where the\par Nikkei was crushed 3.61% lower following the BOJ disappointment.\par  \par * European share markets directly reacted to the Nikkei fall with the German DAX\par falling 2.73% and giving back all of the previous two days gains. The German DAX\par fell 3.22% for the week.\par  \par * Wall Street earnings weighed on the market. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gilead Sciences\f28 \uc2 \f2  weak report led to\par their shares falling over 9% to weigh on the S&P and \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  in particular.\par  \par * The past week included weak results from Apple, Gilead, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xerox\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Goodyear\par that saw shares in all four fall double-digits. The weak company investment\par component in Thursday's US GDP added to the gloomy mood on Wall Street.\par  \par * On Friday the Dow fell 57.12 points or 0.32% to 17,773.64; the S&P fell 10.51\par points or 0.51% lower at 2,065.30 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 29.93 points or 0.62% to\par 4,775.36.\par  \par * It was the worse week for Wall Street in around two months with the Dow and\par S&P falling 1.3% and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  sliding 2.7%\par  \par * Fed Fund Futures are pricing in less than a 15% chance of a June Fed hike; a\par 28% chance of a July hike; a 41% chance of a hike by September and a 60% chance\par of one hike by the end of 2016.\par  \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields eased slightly on Friday with the 2-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield\par easing 1 BP to 0.77% while the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield eased 2 BPs to 1.81%. For\par the week the 2-year yield fell 5 BPs and the 10-year yield fell 7 BPs.\par  \par * Fading Fed hike expectations; sluggish US data and the continued fallout from\par the shock BOJ decision to refrain from easing sent the US dollar lower against\par most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par * The USD/JPY plunged another 1.52% to close at 106.45. The weaker USD and\par fading Fed hike expectations provided the excuse for heavy technical selling\par that triggered stops below some critical support levels.\par  \par * USD/JPY stops were triggered below 107.50 and 106.50 to a low at 106.27 before\par settling around 106.45 into the close. The 106.55 level was the 38.2 fibo of the\par entire 75.31/125.86 move (Abenomics rally) and the weekly close below was a\par bearish event.\par  \par * The EUR/USD also made strong gains on Friday, as the heavy fall in the German\par DAX added to the strong EUR/USD/weak DAX spiral that is taking turns moving both\par markets. The EUR/USD closed at 1.1455 to post a 0.91% gain from Thursday's\par close.\par  \par * It was a mixed performance for commodity/risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday. The\par USD/CAD closed the day flat at 1.2555 - while the NZD/USD managed to post a\par small 0.22% gain on the day.\par  \par * The AUD was the worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday - falling 0.25% to close\par at 0.7605 despite strong gains in some of the key commodities that usually\par impact AUD sentiment. The AUD is getting hit across the board as the market\par continues to price in a 25 BP cut in the cash rate by the RBA when they meet on\par Tuesday.\par  \par * The GBP/USD closed the day flat, as a 1.4600 option expiry drew the price\par action towards 1.4600 after hitting an intra-day high and near three month high\par at 1.4672. The GBP/USD has now made a perfect double top at 1.4672 - as that was\par the Feb 4, 2016 trend high before the pair fell to 1.3836.\par  \par * It was mostly a very good day for key commodities - despite global growth\par concerns weighing on equity markets and EM assets.\par  \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude traded as high as 46.78 (fresh 2016 high) on Friday before selling\par off late in the session to close at 45.92 - down 0.24% on the day. It still\par managed a 5% gain for the week and for the month of April it gained 20% - the\par biggest monthly gain in a year.\par  \par * London Copper jumped 2.19% on Friday to close at 5,050.15. For the week Lon\par Copper managed a small 0.38% gain and for the month it gained around 4.20%.\par  \par * It was a wild week for iron ore prices, as there were big selloffs early in\par the week, but Friday it soared 3.55% after gaining close to 4.0% on Thursday.\par Despite the big swings - iron ore was about flat for the week. For the month\par iron ore surged 22.5%.\par  \par * Gold was perhaps the biggest story in the commodity world - posting a 2.15%\par gain on Friday, as the falling US dollar is forcing out shorts. Gold closed at\par 1,293 - the highest daily close in 15 months. For the week gold gained close to\par 5%, which also happened to be the monthly gain.\par  \par * The emerging market equity ETF eased 0.43% on Friday, which also happened to\par be the weekly loss. For the month of April the EM equity ETF gained 0.41%.\par  \par Wrap-up  The weaker than expected China PMI data could see some risk selling\par first up on Monday - especially as markets closed the week/month on a sluggish\par note Friday following weaker US data and disappointing US company earnings.\par China data wasn't poor enough to prompt sustained weakness in EM, the AUD and\par other risk assets, but wasn't good enough to get May off to a positive start.\par  \par If the Asian markets do get off to a weak start - look for AUD/JPY to move\par lower, as there were whispers on Friday that hedge funds could take advantage of\par Japan Golden Week  holidays to push the USD/JPY lower. With Japan on the US list\par of countries monitored for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation - it is viewed as highly unlikely the\par MOF will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the JPY this side of 100.00. The market continues\par to price in a RBA easing and the AUD will remain vulnerable until the RBA\par announces their decision on Tuesday.\par  \par The RBA decision on Tuesday will be the most interesting event early in the\par week. The majority of economists believe the RBA will remain on hold while the\par market is busily pricing in a 25 BP cut from 2.0% to 1.75%. It is very clear\par that the RBA is reluctant to cut interest rates further and all of their\par messaging until now suggested they were comfortable with the present settings.\par However the surprisingly soft Aus CPI data is likely the game-changer to force\par the RBA to finally act on their long held easing bias. I believe RBA Governor\par Stevens will hold his nose and announce a 25 BP rate cut on Tuesday and the RBA\par will use Friday's Quarterly SOMP to explain in great detail the reasons behind\par their decision. For if the RBA does hold rates at 2.0% they run the risk of the\par AUD/USD resuming its march towards 0.8000. The AUD underperformed on\par Thursday/Friday when key commodities broke higher and the USD broadly weakened.\par If the RBA stays on hold - the AUD/USD will quickly play "catch-up". Because\par there is a great deal of doubt regarding a RBA rate cut - the AUD/USD could slip\par to support around 0.7450, which would be welcomed by the RBA.  \par  John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par Technical view\par  \par Equities\par  \par S&P   The S&P stopped trending higher last week, as the 5-day MA fell below the\par 10-day MA and on Friday the price closed below the 20-day MA at 2,075.67. It\par isn't trending in either direction, but the price action was bearish. The S&P\par ended a sequence of 10 consecutive weeks of making a higher weekly low. The next\par level of support is at 2,030/2,035 where the 50-day MA and the April 7 trend low\par converge. A close below that level targets the 38.2 of the year's 1.810/2,111\par low/high at 1,996.  A close above the 20-day MA (2,075 on Friday) would ease the\par downward pressure. \{Last 2,065.30\}\par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  The trend higher in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  is showing signs of exhaustion, as the 5-day MA\par have started to point lower. A close below the ascending 10-day MA (5,209 on\par Friday) would be another warning that momentum is waning and a short-term top\par may have formed around 5,280. A break back above 5,280 would reignite the trend\par higher and target the Jan 4 and year's high at 5,331.   \{Last 5,252.22\}\par  \par Commodities\par  \par Gold The price action last week was very bullish and now both the short-term and\par medium-term moving average studies indicate gold in trending higher. The 5, 10\par and 20-day moving averages are aligned in a bullish formation and all are\par pointing higher. The same can be said for the 50, 100 and 200-day moving\par averages. The next level of resistance is found at the Jan, 2015 trend high at\par 1,306 with a break targeting the 38.2 of the 1,921 all-time high/1,045\par multi-year low at 1,380. A fall below the ascending 20-day MA (1,242 on Friday)\par would indicate the trend higher is ending and a short-term  top is forming.\par \{Last 1,294\}\par  \par Iron Ore The short-term trend higher ended last week when the 5-day MA turned\par lower and crossed below the 10-day MA. The ascending 20-day MA (59.86 on Friday)\par held the pullback - so the trend higher could easily resume with constructive\par price action. Resistance is at the recent trend high at 68.70 and a break above\par that level should see the trend higher resume. A fall below 59.50 would suggest\par a short-term top is in place.  \{Last 65.20\}\par  \par Lon Copper The 5, 10 and 20-day moving averages remain aligned in a bullish\par formation, but the 5-day MA is pointing lower - negating the trend higher for\par now. Key resistance is found in the 5,130/5,145 window where the March 18 and\par the 38.2 fibo of the 6,481/4,318 move converge. A break above that window would\par be bullish and target 5,350/5,370. Support has formed ahead of 4,870 and a break\par below that level would suggest a short-term top is in place. \{Last 5,050.15\}\par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude is in a short-term trend higher and it is developing\par well. The next level of resistance is found in the 48.25/48.60 window where a\par double top and the 61.8 fibo of the 62.58/26.05 move converge. A fall below the\par ascending 10-day MA (43.44 on Friday) would warn upward momentum is waning.\par  \{Last 45.92\}\par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUR/USD   The EUR/USD isn't trending in either direction, but a break above the\par April 12 high at 1.1465 would be bullish and initially target the Oct 15, 2015\par trend high at 1.1495. A fall below the 10-day MA at 1.1318 would suggest a\par significant double top is in place at 1.1460/65.   \{Last 1.1455\}\par  \par USD/JPY  The USD/JPY broke and closed below the 38.2 fibo of the entire\par 75.31/125.86 move at 106.55 on Friday, which was a bearish event. The next level\par of support is at former resistance at 105.45 and the psychological 105.00 level.\par The USD/JPY also completed a bearish outside week to add to the negative\par outlook. A break above 108.75 would warn there might be a bottom forming and\par more whippy range trading ahead.  \{Last 106.45\}\par  \par AUD/USD The short-term trend higher ended last week, as the 5-day MA crossed\par below the 10-day MA and the price closed below the 20-day MA (0.7662 on Friday).\par The medium-term trend higher remains valid with the 59, 100 and 200-day moving\par averages aligned in a bullish formation and pointing higher. A break below the\par ascending 50-day MA (0.7535 on Friday) would warn the medium-trend higher is\par breaking down. A break below the 38.2 fibo of the 0.6827/0.7836 move at 0.7450\par would suggest a key top is in place at 0.7836 and likely signal a trend lower is\par underway. A break back above 0.7765 would renew upward pressure and initially\par target the trend high at 0.7836. \{Last 0.7603\}  John.noonan@tr.com\par \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-4T23:38:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160502ec51009n9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
