\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Markets Cautious Ahead of BOJ and Fed Meetings -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 3,759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2016\par 02:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Markets in Asia started weaker Monday, as the Japanese yen strengthened and investors stayed cautious ahead of meetings by both the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  this week. Japan's Nikkei slipped 0.6% and South Korea's Kospi was off 0.1%. The Australian and New Zealand markets are closed for holiday. The Fed isn't expected to raise interest rates when it announces its April monetary decision later this week, while in Japan, traders have been speculating about some form of easing from the BOA. The Japanese yen reached as weak as 111.90 yen to one U.S. dollar earlier but was last strengthening to 111.35 yen to one U.S. dollar. A stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decreases the competitiveness of Japanese exporters. Tokyo-listed Mitsubishi Motors Corp. (7211.TO) was up 0.4% after losing more than 40% in the past three trading sessions after admitting to using a non-compliant check for fuel efficiency. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 The above snippet was corrected at 09:03 p.m. Original incorrectly identified the two indexes as Japan's Nikko and South Korea's Kops. Snippet corrected at 9:03 p.m. Original incorrectly identified the two indexes as Japan's Nikko and South Korea's Kops. Additional correction at 10:54 p.m. because the initial BOA was used instead of BOJ (\uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Oil prices are lower in early Asian trade Monday as traders take profit after the strong rally last week on signs that the global glut might be easing. "Although oil markets remain oversupplied through the first half of 2016, we expect supply and demand to come back into balance in the second half of the year. As inventories start to drop, this should push prices back towards the marginal cost of production," says Neil Beveridge, senior analyst at Bernstein. \uc2 Nymex\uc2  is down 40 cents at $43.33 a barrel, Brent is down 35 cents at $44.76 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0022 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia pairs have recently been beholden to external risk factors, and that seems unlikely to change. The upcoming \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  monetary policy meeting, followed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate decision, also on Wednesday, will dictate sentiment for regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week. The yen fell sharply Friday and remains weak on bets that the BOJ will announce some form of additional monetary policy easing, to stoke economic growth and raise inflation levels, with a secondary goal of weakening its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for an additional growth stimulus. The South Korea won and the Singapore dollar might be most reactive to yen-weakening. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  isn't expected to move on rates yet, but there might be a bullish tone from the rate-setting committee that would alter expectations for interest rates in the near future. An upbeat outlook for the economy would raise the chance of another rate-increase, which would boost the greenback. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2304 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely maintain an upward trend but remain vulnerable to selling pressure during Asia trade, says Mizuho Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Kengo Suzuki. The pair (now at 111.62) is on a short-term upward trend to test a technical resistance point of 112.70. But the pair "may gradually lose momentum," after surging more than 2% on Friday on expectations for an extra monetary easing when the BOJ policy setters meet April 27-28, says Suzuki, who tips the pair to trade in a 111.00-112.20 range. The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.1190-1.1290 range and is now at 1.1234. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Apr 2016 20:59 ET \par Correction to Asia Markets Cautious Market Talk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei slipped 0.6% and South Korea's KOSPI was off 0.1% earlier today. "Asia Markets Cautious Ahead of BOJ and Fed Meetings -- Market Talk," at 0036 GMT, incorrectly identified the two indexes as Japan's Nikko and South Korea's Kops.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0051 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW is on the rise a second day, magnetized by USD/JPY which has spiked on bets that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  may announce further easing of monetary policy meeting on Wednesday. The yen tanked 2.1% against the U.S. dollar on Friday, weighing on most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , particularly the South Korea won and the Singapore dollar, which have a stronger correlation to the yen. If the yen does another nosedive, the won will depreciate sharply as well, because the market is aware that \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  keeps close tabs on the yen-won exchange rate, and will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if needed to ensure the won stays relatively weaker for export competitiveness. Aside from the effect of yen-weakening on USD/KRW, the chance of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  setting the stage for another rate-increase after its Wednesday monetary policy meeting, is also supportive for the greenback. USD/KRW is now 1,149.0 from its Friday close of 1,143.1. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0050 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks are little changed as market participants await further information on what, if any, additional easing steps the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will take at its policy meeting later this week, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. Akino anticipates the BOJ will increase its exchange traded fund purchases and also introduce negative interest rates in some bank loan programs. "I believe they'll do both. I'm sure they'll do them, but I'm not sure, for instance, how much more ETF they are going to buy," he says. Financial stocks, especially insurers, are higher, driven by the prospect of more BOJ easing and lessened concerns about Kumamoto earthquake ripples. \f28 \uc2 \f2 MS&AD Insurance Group Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (8725.TO) is up 4.7% at 3,254 yen. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dai-ichi Life Insurance\f28 \uc2 \f2  (8750.TO) is up 4.6% at Y1,535.5. Nikkei is up 0.01% at 17574.88. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0036 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Markets in Asia started weaker Monday, as the Japanese yen strengthened and investors stayed cautious ahead of meetings by both the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  this week. Japan's Nikko slipped 0.6% and South Korea's Kops was off 0.1%. The Australian and New Zealand markets are closed for holiday. The Fed isn't expected to raise interest rates when it announces its April monetary decision later this week, while in Japan, traders have been speculating about some form of easing from the BOA. The Japanese yen reached as weak as 111.90 yen to one U.S. dollar earlier but was last strengthening to 111.35 yen to one U.S. dollar. A stronger local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decreases the competitiveness of Japanese exporters. Tokyo-listed Mitsubishi Motors Corp. (7211.TO) was up 0.4% after losing more than 40% in the past three trading sessions after admitting to using a non-compliant check for fuel efficiency. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0025 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Oil prices are lower in early Asian trade Monday as traders take profit after the strong rally last week on signs that the global glut might be easing. "Although oil markets remain oversupplied through the first half of 2016, we expect supply and demand to come back into balance in the second half of the year. As inventories start to drop, this should push prices back towards the marginal cost of production," says Neil Beveridge, senior analyst at Bernstein. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 40 cents at $43.33 a barrel, Brent is down 35 cents at $44.76 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0022 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/Asia pairs have recently been beholden to external risk factors, and that seems unlikely to change. The upcoming \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  monetary policy meeting, followed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate decision, also on Wednesday, will dictate sentiment for regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week. The yen fell sharply Friday and remains weak on bets that the BOJ will announce some form of additional monetary policy easing, to stoke economic growth and raise inflation levels, with a secondary goal of weakening its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for an additional growth stimulus. The South Korea won and the Singapore dollar might be most reactive to yen-weakening. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  isn't expected to move on rates yet, but there might be a bullish tone from the rate-setting committee that would alter expectations for interest rates in the near future. An upbeat outlook for the economy would raise the chance of another rate-increase, which would boost the greenback. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2304 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely maintain an upward trend but remain vulnerable to selling pressure during Asia trade, says Mizuho Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Kengo Suzuki. The pair (now at 111.62) is on a short-term upward trend to test a technical resistance point of 112.70. But the pair "may gradually lose momentum," after surging more than 2% on Friday on expectations for an extra monetary easing when the BOJ policy setters meet April 27-28, says Suzuki, who tips the pair to trade in a 111.00-112.20 range. The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.1190-1.1290 range and is now at 1.1234. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Apr 2016 22:50 ET \par Correction to Asia Markets Cautious Market Talk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed isn't expected to raise interest rates when it announces its April monetary decision later this week, while in Japan, traders have been speculating about some form of easing from the BOJ. "Asia Markets Cautious Ahead of BOJ and Fed Meetings -- Market Talk," at 0036 GMT, incorrectly used the initials BOA, instead of BOJ in referring to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0247 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's stock markets are down as defaults in corporate bonds strain risk appetite on equities. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index dips 0.8% to 2935.45, while the Shenzhen Composite Index falls 0.9% to 1850.50. Tourism and brokerage stocks are among the biggest decliners, with Dongxin Securities (601198.SH) falling 2.4% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China International Travel Service\f28 \uc2 \f2  (601888.SH) losing 2.9%. Bond defaults among state-owned enterprises, including Baoding Tianwei Group, are putting stress on the equities market, driving more investors to cut positions. "The rising rates in corporate bonds are a warning sign to the equity market. If investors are less interested in bonds that guarantee yields, they will be much less interested in stocks given a stronger need for risk aversion," says Shen Zhengyang, an analyst at Northeast Securities. In addition, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely keep interest rates steady this week, it has nonetheless hinted at a hike in June. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adjusted the yuan weaker against the U.S. dollar by 0.34% Monday, sending a signal to the market that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't immune from depreciation. Outstanding margin debts dropped to roughly 872.2 billion yuan, the lowest level in over a month. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2 -style ChiNext board is down 1.4% at 2106.24. (Yifan.xie@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0243 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/CAD is turning down and also remains under pressure below its key resistance at 1.2720. The relative strength index is bearish, capped by a negative trend line. Besides, a bearish cross has been identified between the 20-period and 50-period moving averages. The pair is likely to challenge its nearest support at 1.2625 in sight. The risk is now a slide below this threshold, which would call for further downsides toward 1.2585. Alternatively, only a break above 1.2720 would trigger further upside with 1.2760 and 1.2780 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0241 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/JPY remains bullish above its nearest support at 124.25. The process of higher highs and lows remains intact, which should confirm a positive outlook. In addition, the rising 50-period moving average still acts as a support role. Hence, as long as 124.25 is not broken, likely advance to 125.85 & 126.40 in extension. Alternatively, below 124.25 look for further downside with 123.80 & 123.35 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/GBP accelerated on the downside after the recent bearish penetration of the key level at 0.7860. The relative strength index has broken down its 30 level, meaning it's in the oversold area, but has not yet displayed any reversal signals. Furthermore, the 20-period and 50-period moving averages are heading downward, and should continue to push the prices lower. As long as 0.7830 is not surpassed, look for further decline to 0.7735 and 0.7695 in extension. Alternatively, above 0.7830 expect further upside with 0.7860 & 0.7895 as targets.[This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0227 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/USD has bounced slightly Monday, after notching a one-month low of 1.1213 and reaching a key technical chart support, but may still be biased lower. The euro rebounded after touching the base of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, but as long as it closes Monday below 1.1269, keeping the bearish chart pattern in effect, there is still a good chance that the euro will weaken against the dollar. The EUR/USD technical chart only turned bearish Friday, when the market reacted to a sharp rally in USD/JPY which was sparked by speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  could announce fresh easing measures at its monetary policy meeting Wednesday. The U.S. FOMC rate meeting, on Wednesday as well, may also be a factor keeping the dollar strong. While the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  is highly unlikely to adjust rates this week, it could paint an upbeat picture for the economy, setting the stage for its next interest rate-rise. EUR/USD is now 1.1237 from its Friday close of 1.1230. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0213 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/PHP leapt higher Monday, opening significantly higher than its last closing for a third consecutive day. Bullishness for the U.S. dollar, rather than bearishness for the Philippine peso, has been the main reason for the sharp move in USD/PHP. On a weekly basis, the peso has fallen 1.3% against the U.S. unit. The dollar has rallied broadly in Asia since the rapid descent of the Japanese yen triggered by bets on further monetary policy easing, or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Ahead of the BOJ meeting on Wednesday, the yen is likely to stay depressed. USD/PHP has now crossed a long-term chart resistance, the 200-day moving average line at 46.64, which may encourage bets on even more peso weakness ahead. USD/PHP is now 46.74 from its Friday close of 46.65. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0145 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/JPY Intraday: Bias remains bullish. The pair rose up to 111.80 before entering a consolidation last Friday. Currently it is trading below the 20-period (30-minute chart) moving average, which still stands above the 50-period one. And the intraday relative strength index has crossed below the neutrality level of 50, indicating downward momentum and the possibility of a further consolidation. However, as long as the level of 110.75 (a key resistance seen last Friday, the breaking of which led to the latest upleg) holds as the key support, the intraday outlook remains bullish and the pair is expected to challenge the first upside target at 112.05 (a key support seen in March 30 - April 1). Alternatively, a break below 110.75 would trigger a further decline toward the next support at 110.25. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0143 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/USD Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair remains on the downside while trading around the 20-period moving average, which stands below the 50-period one. Meanwhile the intraday relative strength index stays within the selling area between 50 and 30. As long as the level of 1.1270 (a level of over-lapping support and resistance seen in April 14-18, and also on last Friday) holds as the key resistance, the pair is expected to post choppy price action with a bearish bias toward the immediate support at 1.1200. Alternatively, a break above 1.1270 would call for a bounce toward the next resistance at 1.1310. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Apr 2016 22:50 ET \par Correction to Asia Markets Cautious Market Talk -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0142 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AUD/USD Intraday: 0.7675 in sight. The pair is being capped by the descending 20-period moving average, which stands below the 50-period one. At the same time, the relative strength index remains below the neutrality level of 50 lacking upward momentum. The intraday outlook remains very bearish and the pair should sink toward the immediate support at 0.7675 (an important level of over-lapping support and resistance seen in April 12-18), and in extension toward 0.7640. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0127 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the daily USD/CNY benchmark rate at 6.5120 from Friday's 6.4898, representing a 0.34% weakening of the yuan against the U.S. dollar. The raising of the USD/CNY mid-point comes on the back of Friday's surge of the U.S. dollar index. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  dollar index jumped back above the 95.0 psychological level after the yen fell 2.1% against the greenback. The yen's weakening was mostly due to hints that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  might announce further policy easing measures on Wednesday. But the dollar also rallied across the other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , due to the possibility that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may, at its Wednesday FOMC meeting, set the stage for its next interest rate-rise. The yuan's fixing is now more correlated to broader \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves, making it a more market-driven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The new characteristic of the fixing mechanism may have discouraged bets on a sharp yuan devaluation ahead, but rather a slow and steady crawl lower. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0127 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The NZD/USD stays below its key resistance at 0.6900 and is also capped by a negative trend line. Meanwhile the relative strength index remains below its neutrality area at 50. Even though a technical rebound is possible, its extent should be limited. A first target to the downside is therefore set at 0.6820. A break below this level would open the way to further weaknesses toward the horizontal support at 0.6790. Alternatively, only a break above the key resistance at 0.6900 would call for further upside toward 0.6935 at first. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 24, 2016 22:50 ET (02:50 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160425ec4p00077\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
