\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Notch Another Weekly Gain\b0\par\par\uc2 2,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2016\par 23:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET Friday) \par  \par                          LAST    CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 18003.75     21.23    0.12% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2                  4906.23    -39.66   -0.80% \par S&P 500               2091.58      0.10    0.00% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    17572.49    208.87    1.20% \par Hang Seng            21467.04   -155.21   -0.72% \par Shanghai Composite    2959.24      6.35    0.22% \par S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2   Sensex       25838.14    -42.24   -0.16% \par Australia: S&P/ASX    5236.40    -36.30   -0.69% \par UK: FTSE 100          6310.44    -71.00   -1.11% \par  \par                  PRICE CHG  YIELD \par U.S. 2 Year        -1/32    0.822 \par U.S. 5 Year        -3/32    1.357 \par U.S. 10 Year       -7/32    1.889 \par Australia 10 Year -11/32    2.642 \par China 10 Year      -6/32    2.945 \par India 10 Year       2/32    7.454 \par Japan 10 Year       2/32   -0.126 \par German 10 Year      2/32    0.234 \par  \par                        LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)    0.7709  -0.0030 \par Yen (USD/JPY)            111.78     2.32 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1149.83     8.66 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)   6.5068   0.0248 \par Euro (EUR/USD)           1.1227  -0.0062 \par WSJ Dollar Index          86.75     0.54 \par  \par                LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     43.74    0.56    1.30% \par Brent Crude   45.11    0.72    1.62% \par Gold        1234.0   -16.3    -1.30% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended mostly higher Friday, with the Dow industrials and S&P 500 gaining for the week despite weak earnings from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Treasurys continued to sell off on an improving global economic outlook. The dollar gained on the prospect that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise rates sooner than expected. Gold fell as the dollar strengthened. Oil settled higher as investors bet that oversupplied markets will ease. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reports of potential new stimulus from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  pushed down the yen on Friday, and that could put a bigger onus on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to deliver when it concludes its two-day policy meeting later this week. Any disappointment could send the yen rising again. "The main risk is that the full impact of such a move has already played out, and so the BoJ will have to surprise with an even-bigger package to sustain more weakness in the yen," says Nomura's Bilal Hafeez. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology shares slumped Friday, but the Dow Jones Industrial Average and the S&P 500 still posted weekly gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are debating whether U.S. stocks can push toward highs reached last May following a rally that began in mid-February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow is hovering near 18000, up 3.3% in 2016, but investors are struggling to find fresh impetus during a lackluster earnings season.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not going to be a one-way street where the market is going to continue to go up," said Mike LaBella, a portfolio manager at QS Investors, a unit of asset manager Legg Mason.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, the Dow added 0.1% to 18003.75, while the S&P 500 ended virtually unchanged at 2091.58. For the week, the Dow gained 0.6% and the S&P 500 rose 0.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite fell 0.8% Friday and technology companies in the S&P 500 were the worst performers in that index, down 1.9% after disappointing earnings from the sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares tumbled 7.2%, weighing on the Dow, while shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  parent Alphabet dropped 5.3% after both companies released earnings late Thursday that fell short of analyst expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy companies, whose declines helped pull major indexes lower earlier this year, rose Friday, along with the price of crude.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 McDonald's\f28 \uc2 \f2  slipped 0.2% after the company's first-quarter profit came in above expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined 0.7% after the company reported growth in revenue and core earnings for its first quarter, though profit in its industrials business slid on declines in its transportation, power and oil-and-gas segments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 American Airlines Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 4.5% after it reported lower quarterly profits compared with a year ago. Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 United Continental Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  lost 3.8% after it said its quarterly profit dropped.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While fears of a recession that had helped drag stocks down earlier this year have eased, tepid economic growth continues to be a concern.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A read on U.S. first-quarter economic growth is scheduled to be released on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think there is growing unease," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michael Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  , chief global strategist at Investec Asset Management. "We're not far away from a quite material slowdown in the U.S. economy. Whether it is going to translate into softening markets, it's hard to tell."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also be focused next week on guidance from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  , whose next meeting is April 26-27.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asian trading Friday, Japan's Nikkei Stock Average gained as the yen weakened and hopes built among traders that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will provide more economic stimulus. Meanwhile, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell and China's Shanghai Composite edged higher. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen fell to a three-week low against the dollar on Friday amid speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will introduce new stimulus at its meeting next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen had rallied this month to its strongest point against the dollar since October 2014, driven in part by speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wouldn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Speculative investors pushed bullish bets on the yen to a record high in the week ended April 12, according to regulatory data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But reports Friday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could expand the negative interest-rate policy it put in place in January sparked profit-taking among some investors, according to Win Thin, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is scheduled to meet April 27 and 28.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are nervous already going into the BOJ meeting next week," Mr. Thin said. "These reports that they may have something cooking...[and] have spooked some people."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the dollar rose against many of its peers as investors grapple with the prospect that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise interest rates sooner than expected. While few expect the Fed to change interest rates at its meeting next week, some investors think the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could take a more upbeat outlook on the economy, which could fuel expectations for a June rate increase. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys suffered their biggest weekly selloff of 2016, sending benchmark bond yields to a multiweek peak as improving sentiment toward the global economic outlook sapped demand for haven debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark U.S. 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note posted the biggest weekly increase since early November. Yields rise as bond prices fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is a moderate setback for government bonds that had chalked up big price gains during the first quarter, driven by worries over a global recession and a deflation scare. Over the past few weeks, investors have been easing up on government bonds and buying riskier assets: stocks, oil, junk bonds and emerging-market equity and bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil prices have soared from multiyear lows. U.S. stocks are flirting with their all-time highs set in May 2015. Capital outflow in such large developing countries as China has been easing thanks to a weaker dollar. Recent data suggest China isn't headed toward a sharp slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bond market is "likely to face continued pressure" heading into next week's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy meeting, said Aaron Kohli, interest-rate strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BMO Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "It's likely that the backdrop of improving risk sentiment will remove some of the rationale for rates to remain so comparatively rich."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's next meeting is April 26 and 27. Investors widely expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stand pat. Fed Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  made it clear in late March that she would be cautious in raising interest rates due to an uncertain global growth outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some analysts say the Fed may give a nod to improving financial conditions over the past few weeks. Bond prices could fall if the Fed's interest-rate statement increases the chance of a rate increase at the June policy meeting, traders say. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil kick-started its rally during a week many expected it to fall apart, the product of a clear change of heart among investors: The panic is gone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil made gains for the eighth time in 10 weeks, a rally that has pushed it up 67% from a nearly 13-year low in February. The gains come as many analysts expected crude to retreat toward that low, citing stockpiles lingering near record highs, global production that is still strong and the failure of the world's biggest exporters to complete a deal last weekend to cap their output.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, small and even temporary changes to the oil market were enough to keep the rally going, traders said. Pipeline outages and strikes disrupted supply around the world, China reported rising imports, employment grew in the U.S. and the storage glut didn't grow. With fears of a recession now firmly on the back burner, these changes are enough to halt another massive selloff for oil, said Quincy Krosby, market strategist at Prudential Financial, which manages $1.2 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All the market needs to see is that at the margin something is changing, " she said. "There's stability globally, and that helps oil."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In other commodity markets, gold prices extended losses on Friday, hurt by a stronger U.S. dollar and a shift in investor expectations for potential interest-rate increases this year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par  \par U.S. TO SEND 250 ADDITIONAL MILITARY PERSONNEL TO SYRIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama\f28 \uc2 \f2  plans to announce Monday he is sending up to 250 additional military personnel to Syria to help local forces fighting \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  , significantly expanding the small American footprint inside the war-ravaged country, according to U.S. officials. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par APPLE'S WATCH OUTPACED THE IPHONE IN FIRST YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apple Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  . sold twice as many Watches as iPhones in each device's debut year. Yet the smartwatch is dogged by a perception that seems premature given the history of Apple's most popular devices: disappointment. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OBAMA EXPRESSES SKEPTICISM OVER NORTH KOREAN OFFER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Apr 2016 17:36 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Notch Another -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barack Obama\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Sunday the U.S. is analyzing North Korea's most recent provocative actions, but gave little credence to a new overture from Pyongyang. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DONALD TRUMP, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HILLARY CLINTON\f28 \uc2 \f2  LOOK SET TO BUILD ON LEAD IN PENNSYLVANIA PRIMARY, POLL SHOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hillary Clinton\f28 \uc2 \f2  looked poised to build on their momentum-shifting wins in New York with victories in the Pennsylvania primaries Tuesday, a new Wall Street Journal/NBC News/Marist poll shows. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NORTH KOREA LAUNCHES MISSILE FROM SUBMARINE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 North Korea appeared to have launched a missile from a submarine during a weekend test launch off its east coast, \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korea's defense ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  said, the latest attempt by Pyongyang to bolster its strategic military capabilities against the U.S. and its Asian allies. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FIAT CHRYSLER\f28 \uc2 \f2   RECALLS MORE THAN 1.1 MILLION CARS, SUVS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiat Chrysler\f28 \uc2 \f2  is recalling more than 1.1 million cars and SUVs world-wide because their gear shifters may confuse drivers and allow them to exit their vehicles without placing them in 'park.' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par VW SETTING ASIDE $18 BILLION FOR EMISSIONS SCANDAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volkswagen\f28 \uc2 \f2  took a $18.28 billion charge related to the diesel emissions cheating scandal, forcing it to slash its 2015 dividends and post a deep loss. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GM CEO BARRA RECEIVES 77% JUMP IN COMPENSATION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Motors Chief Executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mary Barra\f28 \uc2 \f2  was awarded $28.6 million in total compensation in 2015, a 77% increase coming as the company notched record sales and profits on strong demand for light trucks. Separately, \f28 \uc2 \f2 GM\f28 \uc2 \f2  announced that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steve Girsky\f28 \uc2 \f2  would leave its board of directors. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MCDONALD'S\f28 \uc2 \f2   PROFIT CLIMBS ABOVE EXPECTATIONS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All-day breakfast and a new value menu helped \f28 \uc2 \f2 McDonald's\f28 \uc2 \f2  beat profit and sales expectations for the third straight quarter, signaling that a turnaround that began last year is sustainable. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL GLOOM HURTS \f28 \uc2 \f2 GE\f28 \uc2 \f2   RESULTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global oil rout continued to weigh on \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  , which posted a quarterly decline in operating income and orders for its core industrials business. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SEVERAL BIG \f28 \uc2 \f2 COMERICA\f28 \uc2 \f2   SHAREHOLDERS URGING BANK TO EXPLORE SALE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some of the biggest shareholders at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Comerica\f28 \uc2 \f2  are frustrated with the Texas bank's results and urging it to explore a sale. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MICROSOFT\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 GOOGLE\f28 \uc2 \f2   END REGULATORY DISPUTES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  parent Alphabet agreed to end their yearslong regulatory battles around the world, reflecting the changing relationship between the tech giants. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par VALEANT FINALIZING CONTRACT WITH \f28 \uc2 \f2 PERRIGO\f28 \uc2 \f2  'S CHIEF AS CEO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Valeant Pharmaceuticals\f28 \uc2 \f2  is finalizing a contract to name Perrigo Chief Executive Joseph Papa as its next CEO, hoping a fresh face and an experienced pharmaceutical-industry boss will calm nervous investors. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par Libya's Eastern Government Poised to Send Its First Crude Exports \par Siemens to Showcase Its U.S. Presence During Obama Visit \par Several Big Comerica Shareholders Urging Bank to Explore Sale \par Valeant Finalizing Contract With \f28 \uc2 \f2 Perrigo\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Joseph Papa as Next CEO \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par 2350  JPN  Mar Services Producer Price Index \par 0300  SKA  Mar Net barter terms of trade \par 0400  THA  Mar Trade data \par 0401  MAL  Feb Labour Force Statistics \par 0500  SIN  Mar CPI \par 0500  JPN  Feb Indexes of Business Conditions - Revision \par 0800  GER  Apr Ifo Business Climate Index \par 0820  TAI  Mar Money Supply \par 1000  UK   Apr CBI Quarterly Industrial Trends Survey \par 1400  US   Mar New Residential Sales \par 1430  US   Apr Texas Manufacturing Outlook Survey \par  N/A  AUS  Australia: Anzac Day. Financial markets closed. \par  N/A  VIE  Apr Industrial Output \par  N/A  VIE  Apr Trade Balance \par  N/A  NZ   New Zealand: Anzac Day. Financial markets closed. \par  N/A  ITA  Italy: Liberation Day \par  N/A  GER  Cameron meets Merkel and Obama in Germany \par             to discuss IS,  Libya and migration \par  N/A  