\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Falls on Bank of Japan Stimulus Hopes -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2016\par 15:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:21 ET - The yen has given back most of this month's gains against the dollar amid speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will ramp up stimulus at its meeting next week. The yen had rallied in recent weeks, touching its highest point since 2014, as investors bet that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. But reports Friday that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could expand its negative rate program have pushed the yen lower. The dollar is up 1.7% against the yen at Y111.275. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com; @chelseydulaney)\par \par \uc2 9:21 ET - We all know markets tend to buy the rumor and sell the fact. Reports of potential new stimulus from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  next week are pushing down the yen today, but that could put a bigger onus on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to deliver. Any disappointment could send the yen rising again. "The main risk is that the full impact of such a move has already played out, and so the BoJ will have to surprise with an even-bigger package to sustain more weakness in the yen," says Nomura's Bilal Hafeez. But today's move is just a minor setback for the a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has been roaring this year, surprising traders and investors given the BoJ introduced negative interest rates earlier this year as it stepped up monetary stimulus. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:14 ET - The inflation trade rolls on amid resurgent oil, a patient Fed and diminishing fears about deflation. The 10-year breakeven rate, the yield spread between 10-year TIPS and Treasurys, hit 165.3 basis points--the highest since August. The move reflects hedge funds and money managers buying TIPS and selling nominal Treasurys, and at this level it suggests investors expect US inflation running at an annualized 1.653% on average the next decade. While still lower than the Fed's 2% target, policy makers should find some solace in that the rate has climbed after falling below 120bp in mid-February. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:11 ET - While rising levels of commercial and residential construction in North America should drive higher demand for \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  (CAT) machinery, 1Q sales of construction equipment in fell 18% to $2B. CAT says a glut of used equipment coming out of oil and natural gas fields is being redeployed for general construction work, reducing the need for contractors to buy new machinery. (robert.tita@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:03 ET - Yen bulls are taking a beating on reports the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could roll out new stimulus at next week's meeting. The yen is selling off broadly, and Brown Brothers' global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -strategy team notes speculators had amassed record gross and net long yen positions. It adds the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upward momentum has stalled with the dollar successfully holding the Y107.75 area 3 times. "The interest-rate differential makes it expensive to hold a long-yen position with it trending higher," the firm adds. "Without momentum, the late yen longs were in weak hands." As such, longs may be vulnerable to more unwinding. The dollar is up 1.5% at Y111.13 while the euro gains 1.3% to Y125.14. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:56 ET - It's been a rough week for major bond markets. The US 10-year yield was slightly higher, extending this week's large upswing. While it is a moderate setback from a big drop of the yield in 2016, investors are concerned whether just like last year, bonds might have been overbought and could be hit by rout this Spring. Haven allure has also been pulling back as fear of a global recession tapers off, commodities have been rising, US stocks are flirting with record highs and Chinese yuan has been stabilizing. Some analysts say bond yields need to rise due to this reflation trade. Fed and BOJ meetings are due next week. For now a patient Fed holds yields at historically very low level, so any shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rattle the bond market. The 10-year US yield was 1.881% vs 1.87% Thursday. The yield was 1.753% at end of last week. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:55 ET - \uc2 BB&T\uc2  (BBT) and \uc2 M&T\uc2  (MTB) are acquisitive big regional lenders with similar solid-looking results. But MTB's stock gains have been double BBT's rebound the past 3 months, putting BBT shares as a 15% discount to MTB. "We believe BBT is better positioned strategically near and medium term," says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , so it upgrades the stock to buy and drops MTB to hold while trimming its price target $4 to $124. Regarding BBT, Deutsche sees "more opportunity for revenue upside...given cross-sell potential into and from acquired franchises." Both, inactive premarket, have gained 3% this week. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 8:43 ET - Messy 1Q results from \uc2 GE\uc2  aren't scaring off some Wall Street analysts despite continuing pain in its oil-and-gas unit. \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  notes performance was weaker than expected in the power turbine, locomotive and oil-and-gas-equipment businesses. But the investment thinks "\uc2 GE\uc2  remains anchor to windward in what we think will be an increasing move from a greed to fear market" after the past 2 month's stock rebound. It's still Stifel's "preferred defensive quality name in a pullback." Shares are off 1.1% premarket at $30.65. (ted.mann@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:41 ET - Pension funds and other big investors seeking diversification and better yields than bonds are back at it: scooping up bonds that pay hurricane and other insurance claims from catastrophes. Willis Capital Markets & Advisory said that 1Q saw new issuance of $2B of non-life-insurance-linked securities, "the most of any first quarter in history." Capacity was up 35% from $1.5B in year-earlier period. The quarter ended with $23.2B of the so-called insurance-linked securities outstanding, "also a record for any quarter end." In another sign of the market's expected continued growth, advisory firm TigerRisk Partners this week announced hiring an exec from \uc2 Swiss Re\uc2 , an industry powerhouse, to "develop next-generation ILS and other insurance risk-to-capital solutions." (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:39 ET - As \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 's (CAT) revenue has been shrinking, so has the workforce. Its payroll is down 12% from a year earlier at 114K, and more than half of 15K positions cut last quarter were in the US as the equipment maker sheds workers in response to reduced production of machinery and engines. (robert.tita@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:31 ET - Amid its 1Q EPS beat, American (AAL) is adding $2B to stock-buyback efforts, which that money targeted to be spent by the end of 2017. The airline returned more than $1.6B to shareholders last quarter through dividends and buybacks, putting the total since mid-2014 at $6.6B. Shares are up 0.7% premarket at $40.27 after having retreated 2.2% this week.(susan.carey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:26 ET - \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  (CAT) says prices for mined commodities are off their recent lows, but the company says it's "not clear at this time that the current prices are either sustainable or sufficient to drive increased demand for equipment." CAT says mining companies remain focused on cutting spending on capital equipment, keeping demand for new equipment weak. Sales of CAT's mining equipment dropped 26% in 1Q to $1.4B and the mining business lost $96M. CAT slips 4.5% in pre-market trading. (robert.tita@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2016 09:21 ET (13:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160422ec4m002na\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
