\par\par \b \uc2 Energy Shares Lead Hong Kong 0.6% Higher -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2016\par 06:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Stocks in Hong Kong are up 0.6% as energy shares get a lift from rebounding oil prices. The Hang Seng Index's energy sector was last up 1.1%. Though the failure to agree on an output freeze at a weekend meeting of oil producers will "probably continue to weigh on crude-oil prices" until Saudi Arabia and Iran can reach a consensus, says Margaret Yang, market analyst at brokerage CMC Markets, expectations will build of an agreement at June's \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting, "as low oil price are hurting all the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  members." Crude-oil futures rebounded from a loss of more than 6% Monday to trade down just 1.4% in the U.S. Brent was last down just 0.5% at $46.45 a barrel. One explanation offered for the bounce is that investors believe the oil-price problem is more one of overproduction than of insufficient demand -- lessening worries about the pace of global economic growth. Energy stocks were also the biggest gainers on the S&P 500 overnight. (chao.deng@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: \f28 \uc2 \f2 WWE\f28 \par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells 2.185 trillion yen ($20.2 billion) of 0.1% five-year bonds with the lowest price at 101.60 (yielding minus 0.222%), which was slightly stronger than street forecasts. The bid-to-offer ratio slightly improved from the last auction to 4.36 from 3.59, while the tail was at 0.02 versus 0.05 at the last tender. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0404 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors ought to put their money into Asia ex-Japan and emerging market equities, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 , reversing its five-year bearish view on the region. What changed? Valuations are cheap and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are now more competitive, with the U.S. dollar reaching a peak or stabilizing, says the bank. Capital expenditure in the region as a percentage of GDP has declined, which may push up margins and thus price-to-earnings ratios. Monetary policy in China, the region's giant, is gaining traction and property prices in the country are on the rise, potentially pulling up equities in the region. Lastly, BofA sounds a note of optimism on expected reform: "We think that a renewed public backlash against perceived corruption, more expensive and less available capital, and deteriorating fiscal positions are likely to drive an inflection point in emerging market (state-owned enterprises) reform." (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0349 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysian sovereign five-year credit default swaps stabilized Tuesday morning, trading unchanged at around 160 basis points, suggesting negative sentiment arising from the default news about the Malaysian development fund 1MDB has been digested, a trader said. The CDS, which investors buy as insurance against debt default, rose around 10 basis points to a spread of 162 basis points on Monday, on news that an Abu Dhabi sovereign fund said 1MDB was in default of its obligations. To put that into context, Malaysian five-year CDS traded at a spread of about 220 basis points in January when oil prices tumbled to below US$30 a barrel and global stocks were plunging. Yield on Malaysian five-year government bonds, which rose as high as 3.415% Monday, was bidding at around 3.412% Tuesday. "Whether Malaysian CDS will widen further will depends on the development of the [1MDB] incident, but more, I think it will be more driven by commodity prices," the trader said. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0347 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (MS) first-quarter revenue from Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was down 25% from a year earlier to $911 million, the company said. Chief Financial Officer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jonathan Pruzan\f28 \uc2 \f2  told The Wall Street Journal the sharp drop was attributable to the spike in volatility the region experienced earlier this year. Asia now ranks third in contribution to revenue, behind after the Americas and Europe, Middle East and Africa. (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @jbsteins) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: \f28 \uc2 \f2 WWE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0336 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The lowest accepted price for today's 5-year JGB auction will likely come in around 101.58-101.59, traders say. The result is due at 0345 GMT. The latest issue (amounting to 2.4 trillion yen), carries a coupon of 0.1%, the same as the previous issue. Bond traders say that while investors are unlikely to aggressively buy negative-yielding JGBs, demand still remains given expectations that the BOJ will buy them via its asset-purchase program, thus making the auction uneventful. Ahead of the auction result, the benchmark 5-year JGB is yielding minus 0.225%. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0330 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Inflation in Malaysia likely eased in March from a seven-year high the previous month as lower fuel prices offset higher prices for food and consumer goods. The consumer-price index, the country's main inflation gauge, likely rose 3.5% in March from a year earlier, according to the median forecast of eight economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. Inflation in January was 4.2% on year, the biggest on-year rise since December 2008. Such an outcome in March would signify that the inflation rate has peaked and will likely moderate ahead, assuming no major changes to prices of key items such as fuel, transport, food and utilities, said Dr. Mohd Afzanizam Abdul Rashid, chief economist at Bank Islam Malaysia Bhd. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0314 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A failure to reach a production cap agreement in \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's biannual June 2 meeting could push oil prices back to $30 a barrel, says Hamza Khan, the head of commodity strategy at ING. However, there are other factors that could whittle down \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  output, such as the oil industry strike in Kuwait, which has cut the country's oil output by more than half to around 1.1 million barrels a day. "As other countries (including Saudi Arabia) tighten their purse strings, we could see similar disruptions in the region," the analyst writes. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  is down 11 cents at $39.67 a barrel, Brent is down 19 cents at $42.74 a barrel. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0300 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's markets slid Tuesday as investors choose to stand on the sidelines given uncertainty hovering around oil market. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index trades up 0.3% at 3042.75 with half of the stocks retreating and the Shenzhen Composite Index edges up 0.4% at 1959.90. Shares in non-ferrous metal, communications and automobile sectors are the biggest winners so far. Confidence in the oil market was shaken as major oil producers failed to reach production freeze. "Given the lack of additional new fund inflow, the market is expected to continue range-bound trading in the short-term," wrote Tian Weidong, an analyst at Kaiyuan Securities. The ChiNext Price Index retreats into the negative territory during the first trading hour, currently down 0.3% at 2264.95. (Yifan.xie@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A clash Friday between the U.S. and Japanese financial chiefs over yen policy has raised the Japanese public's interest--and concern--about the coming biannual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Japan's Finance Minister Taro Aso on Friday threatened "necessary" action against a rising yen despite U.S. Treasury Secretary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacob Lew\f28 \uc2 \f2  arguing there was no justification for Tokyo to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This raises that possibility that the upcoming \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  report will criticize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against recent yen appreciation. Responding to a question from a domestic TV reporter, on Tuesday Finance Minister Taro Aso counters that he will take "various measures" if there are rapid yen rises. Among many Japanese, memories are still fresh of a December 2011 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  report. It described exchange rate movement during that year as "orderly"--contradicting the views of Japanese officials that the rate moves had been disorderly--and harshly criticized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  implemented by Tokyo in 2011. That report sent shock waves through the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japanese finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  then, resulting in intense internal debate over whether Tokyo should stay out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, according to people with direct knowledge of the matter. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0250 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR slipped Tuesday, giving the Indonesia rupiah an intraday peak gain of 0.3% against the dollar, due to a generally upbeat mood in Asian financial markets. Stocks in the region are mostly higher, led by Japan's Nikkei which has jumped 3.5% on hopes that the authorities will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. The overnight rebound of oil prices, which bucked the bearish view that was propagated after the failed Doha meeting, has also helped general investor sentiment. USD/IDR could take on a bearish technical skew, implying further rupiah strength against the dollar ahead, if it closes Tuesday below 13,130 and inside the Bollinger downtrend channel. USD/IDR is now 13,155 from its Monday close of 13,170.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0234 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/JPY Intraday: Target 109.45. The pair continues on its rebound initiated yesterday from the low of 107.82. With support provided by the 20-period (30-minute chart) moving average, it is currently trading around the upper Bollinger band, calling for an acceleration to the upside. Also, the intraday relative strength index is well directed near the over-bought level of 70 lacking downward momentum. The intraday outlook is very bullish and the pair should approach the immediate resistance at 109.45 before re-testing 109.75 (around the high of April 15). [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 19, 2016 00:14 ET (04:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160419ec4j00085\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
