\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Flirts With 18-Month High\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ewen Chew and Gregor Stuart Hunter   \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2016\par 08:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen climbed to a near 18-month high against the U.S. dollar on Monday, as traders resumed bets that authorities won't be allowed to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid pressure from the nation's key allies.\par \par \uc2 The yen strengthened as much as 1.4% against the dollar, sending other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly lower on renewed risk-aversion concerns, given the yen's safe-haven status, while a sharp fall in crude-oil prices also hurt demand for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen, last at Yen108.02, will notch a new 18-month high against the dollar if it surpasses the Yen107.61 mark recorded on April 11.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the weekend meeting of \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  leaders, the yen fell substantially and gave Japan's domestic stock market a 6.5% weekly gain. The yen's weakening was fueled by hopes that Japan might win approval from its peers to use brute force to reverse what it has termed a one-sided and excessive appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  poured cold water on that idea late Friday, saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets remained orderly despite the yen's recent appreciation, hinting that the U.S. won't support \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Despite talks of "excessive" moves in the yen from Bank of Japan Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda, pushback from the U.S. may have diminished the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Tom Kenny, a senior economist at ANZ Bank, which holds one of the best records on forecasting the yen.\par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to get in there and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it needs to get international support for that," he said. "If they go in unilaterally, history has shown they haven't had much success in stopping the yen from rising."\par \par \uc2 In addition, the slump in oil prices after producer nations failed to reach an accord in Doha to freeze production will provide further impetus for the yen to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 "Lower oil is helping their current-account balance and terms of trade, and that's contributing to a stronger yen," Mr. Kenny said.\par \par \uc2 The yen's recovery has taken stocks down in Japan. The benchmark Nikkei index was down around 3%, weighing on other Asian equities, which are mostly in the red.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the rest of Asia were all slightly weaker, most prominently the Malaysian ringgit, which was battered by both yen-related risk aversion and plummeting crude-oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Lower oil prices hurt oil export-related revenues for Malaysia, as well as the country's trade balance, which in turn adversely affects credit ratings. The ringgit was down more than 1.0% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 After major oil producing countries failed to agree on a production freeze Sunday, Brent crude prices fell 4% to $41.37 a barrel in Asian trading hours. The decline in oil prices has triggered a sharp drop in investor confidence that is also hitting other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar, weakened by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing monetary policy on Thursday, hovered near S$1.3583, just under its previous closing level against the dollar. The Singapore dollar had strengthened slightly Friday, pulled along by the yen's surge.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, China's yuan rose marginally against the dollar to 6.4805 yuan, compared with its official Friday close of 6.4816 yuan, clinging on to the strong start engineered by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , which set its daily benchmark 0.2% higher against the U.S. unit.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com and Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 18, 2016 02:28 ET (06:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160418ec4i000az\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
