\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's pitch for  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action meets cool G20, U.S. response\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2016\par 23:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Japan tells U.S. concerned about yen rises\par \par \uc2 * U.S. Lew doesn't see disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves\par \par \uc2 * Japan lobbies to keep G20 warning vs excess volatility\par \par \uc2 * Onus to keep yen rises in check may fall on BOJ (Adds analyst quote)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, April 16 (Reuters) - Japan's efforts to seek informal consent to act against an unwelcome yen rise bore little fruit, with the United States offering a cool response to concerns voiced by Tokyo that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains are too sharp and may justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A lack of G20 sympathy for Tokyo's appeal may embolden yen bulls to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 17-month highs against the dollar hit earlier this month, keeping Japanese policymakers on edge to contain the damage on a fragile, export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew said on Friday he did not see any disorderly moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a day after his Japanese counterpart Taro Aso conveyed to him Tokyo's "strong concern" over what it saw as "one-sided" yen rises.\par \par \uc2 "At a time of slow and uneven global growth, avoiding beggar-thy-neighbor exchange rate policies is particularly important," Lew said in a statement to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's steering committee on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "Surplus countries especially have a responsibility to adopt stronger adjustment measures to avoid reliance on the exchange rate to support demand," he said, signaling that Washington was in no mood to allow Tokyo to conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a communique issued on Friday, the G20 finance leaders maintained a warning on countries to refrain from competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and signaled that markets have calmed from the past few months of turbulence.\par \par \uc2 The G20 also reiterated that excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility was undesirable, but only after heavy lobbying by Japanese delegates who want to use the language to justify stepping into the market if they see yen gains as excessive.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo won't be engaging in competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation as long as any yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is brief and aimed at smoothing abrupt yen rises, a senior \uc2 Japanese finance ministry\uc2  official told reporters after the G20 gatherings.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE SYMPATHY\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials share Japan's unhappiness over the dollar's weakening, though they accept it as a natural consequence of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's cautious economic outlook and see no reason to act to weaken the euro.\par \par \uc2 Others also did not see broad dollar falls as a concern.\par \par \uc2 "We are in a situation where the world is working its way through a lower commodity price scenario and that's where we'll have countries that are exporters have (their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) tend to decline, and those that import ... tend to rise," Bank of Canada Governor \uc2 Stephen Poloz\uc2  said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  sees a high bar to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It is hard to call the current market a dysfunctional one that requires the BOJ to provide entry into yen positions at reasonable prices via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 With its hands tied on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the government may lean on the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to deploy another blow of monetary stimulus as early as its next rate review on April 27-28.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Haruhiko Kuroda waded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate to describe past yen rises as "excessive," and reiterated his pledge to take additional monetary easing steps if yen moves hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 While many BOJ officials are wary of acting again so soon after having deployed negative interest rates in January, Kuroda may be ready to pull the trigger, some analysts say.\par \par \uc2 U.S. economist \uc2 Nouriel Roubini\uc2 , who claims to have spoken to Japanese central bankers, signaled on Friday the BOJ may be nervous enough about the yen's rise to ease even before the April meeting.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it's going to happen, but if the dollar were to keep on weakening and the euro and yen keep appreciating... they may have to act earlier because this is becoming a serious issue." (Additional reporting by Andrea Hopkins; Editing by Andrea Ricci) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-16T22:24:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160416ec4g00ueh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
