\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-IMF's Furusawa warns of limits to further BOJ rate cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2016\par 02:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Yen moves broadly in line with fundamentals-Furusawa\par \par \par \par \uc2 * Adds monetary policy alone can't cure economic woes\par \par \par \par \uc2 * G20 warning vs competitive devaluation won't bind Japan   (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, April 12 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  still has tools to further expand monetary stimulus but must bear in mind that there are limits to how far it can deepen negative interest rates, a senior \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  official said on Tuesday.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Mitsuhiro Furusawa, the IMF's deputy managing director, said   he did not see recent yen rises as deviating sharply from the fund's existing assessment that yen moves were "broadly in line with fundamentals."\par \par \par \par \uc2 When asked whether recent yen rises were sharp enough to justify unilateral yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, he told Reuters: "All I can say is that there's a consensus among nations that authorities can take necessary action against rapid and disorderly exchange-rate moves."\par \par \par \par \uc2 The BOJ stunned markets in January by deciding to add negative interest rates to its massive asset-buying programme in a fresh attempt to achieve its 2 percent inflation target.\par \par \par \par \uc2 But the move has failed to arrest an unwelcome yen rise that hurts already weak exports and a fragile economy, prompting government officials to warn of their readiness to step into the market to stem yen gains.\par \par \par \par \uc2 With the G20 major economies warning against competitive devaluations, however, many market players expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of being criticised as adopting a "beggar-thy-neighbour" policy.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Furusawa said the communique from the G20 finance leaders meeting in Shanghai in February will not constrain additional monetary stimulus. The BOJ still had tools it could use to reflate the economy, he noted.\par \par \par \par \uc2 But he warned that monetary policy alone could not solve  all of Japan's economic problems, stressing that easy monetary conditions must be accompanied by fiscal stimulus and structural reforms to achieve sustained, balanced growth.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "Negative rates have positive effects on the economy," Furusawa said. "But the BOJ must be mindful that there are limits to how long such a policy can continue and how deep rates can go negative."\par \par \par \par \uc2 BOJ policymakers will likely debate the possibility of easing policy at a rate review this month, as a raft of gloomy data threatens their scenario that a moderate economic recovery will accelerate inflation toward their target, sources familiar with their thinking said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The BOJ charges a 0.1 percent interest on a small portion of excess reserves that financial institutions park with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \par \par \uc2 Ballpark estimates by \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff showed central banks could cut interest rates between minus 75 basis points to minus 200 points, before they reach the tipping point at which depositors will shift to cash.   (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Eric Meijer)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-16T00:24:30.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160413ec4d000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
