\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS: Nikkei Lifted By Weaker Yen, As Shanghai Stocks Slip\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dominique Fong   \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2016\par 09:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Most Asian markets move higher\par \par \uc2 Japanese shares rose as the yen weakened slightly Tuesday, delivering relief to investors who are worried that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hampers efforts to boost the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average jumped 1.1% as the yen pulled back from its recent gains versus the U.S. dollar. Stocks were on track to post the highest one-day gain since March 10, when the Nikkei Stock Average rose 1.3%. The yen was recently trading at 108.22 a dollar, falling from as strong as 107 a dollar.\par \par \uc2 In China, stocks slipped after the country's securities regulator said late Monday that it approved seven initial public offerings that are expected to raise a total 2.8 billion yuan ($433 million). Investors were selling stocks ahead of the offerings, on worries that an impending batch of new stock-sales would divert money from other shares. The Shanghai Composite Index fell 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Read:China's premier says economy is showing positive signs ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/chinas-premier-says-economy-showing-positive-signs-2016-04-11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/chinas-premier-says-economy-showing-positive-signs-2016-04-11}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, shares in other Asia markets edged higher. Investors took on risk as they became more optimistic that a slightly weaker yen against the U.S. dollar and an overnight uptick in oil and commodities prices could help revive growth in the region.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi was up 0.6%, and Australia's S&P/ASX 200 rose 0.9%. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index gained 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 The weaker yen ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-rises-after-japans-finance-minister-fires-a-warning-shot-over-yen-2016-04-12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-rises-after-japans-finance-minister-fires-a-warning-shot-over-yen-2016-04-12}}}\uc2 ) gave relief to investors in Japanese stocks. Many had been betting that a weaker yen would boost exporters' earnings, in turn lifting wages and consumption to support the world's third-largest economy. The yen's rise in recent months has confounded analysts' previous forecasts and defied the wishes of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials who are trying to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in part by easing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Read: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be gentle with yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/japan-cant-be-gentle-with-yen-intervention-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/\b  \highlight22japan\b0 \highlight22 -cant-be-gentle-with-yen-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 -2016-04-08}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 A mix of factors has made the yen stronger recently. Investors have rushed to the traditional haven asset while global growth softens. The U.S. dollar has weakened relative to major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  slowed the pace of interest-rate increases this year, and some speculators who had earlier bet on the yen's weakness bought back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cover short positions.\par \par \uc2 Still, a stock rebound in Japan might only have limited momentum as investors assume the yen will gradually strengthen again to as much as 105 per dollar, said Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.\par \par \uc2 "A slight rebound [in the yen] is giving the Japan market a little bit of strength," said Margaret Yang, market analyst for CMC Markets, based in Singapore. "I'm not sure how long this rebound is going to last, given the uncertain outlook of the yen."\par \par \uc2 Shares of Japanese steelmakers also gained as investors bet that the recent uptick in commodities prices would sustain a rebound. \uc2 Nippon Steel & Sumitomo Metal Corp\uc2 . (5401.TO) shares jumped 6.5%.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 12, 2016 03:18 ET (07:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160412ec4c000l7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
