\par\par \b \uc2 EURO CREDIT-Slightly wider start to a busy week\b0\par\par\uc2 959 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 09:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 11 (IFR) - European markets are beginning the new week with some slight negativity after a quiet overnight session in Asia.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, yet more yen strength has again weighed on the Nikkei. The index opened a tad lower and almost immediately headed south to be down nearly 1.5% after a couple of hours. There was a small recovery into the mid-session break and that recovery continued in the afternoon, with the index closing down just 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 In China, some mixed inflation data had no real effect on the Shanghai Comp, which gap-opened up nearly 1% and back above 3,000. That rally extended further into half-time with the index up 2%. After the break, though, some sellers emerged and it is currently up 1.6%. The Hang Seng is also slightly higher.\par \par \uc2 Over in Europe, the weekend saw plenty of \uc2 ECB\uc2  speak and the interminable will they/won't they reach an agreement in Greece also occupied plenty of column inches.\par \par \uc2 None of that has made a major difference though. Bullet-proof Bunds are up around 20 ticks with 10yr yields still in single figures. BTPs are flat. Equity futures are a touch lower with Brent back below $42 after Friday's major rally. Synthetic credit is a bit wider, with the Main out 1.5bp at 81bp, Crossover up 2bp at 338bp and Sen Fin up 2bp at 103bp.\par \par \uc2 Last week did not have a great deal on the agenda but there was still plenty to talk about. First some major fluctuations in the oil price. Then the dollar/yen tumble which led to rumours of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BoJ. And just to top it all off a peripheral sell-off akin to the dark days of the sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 This week, however, sees a touch more on the agenda. On the data front, the inflation picture on both sides of the Atlantic will be under the microscope to see if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures are taking hold. We also have plenty of other big numbers to focus on. And it scarcely seems believable but the US Q1 earnings season is also upon us. \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  - as is tradition - kicks-off tonight after the bell has tolled, but the key will be the investment banks' numbers which start at the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Today, however, there is very little on the agenda. And that might well present us with an opportunity to catch-up on a bit of admin before those events unfold. And, of course, to fully digest the events at the Augusta National last night, where Danny Willett won his first green jacket in remarkable circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Weekend news:\par \par \uc2 Europe: \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Mersch says \uc2 ECB\uc2  aware of complexity of negative rates, warns on competitive devaluation and diminishing returns in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  instruments; Villeroy said \uc2 ECB\uc2  not out of ammo, wants appointment of euro fin min; Visco says rates could stay very low for long time but not forever.\par \par \uc2 Spiegel reports German govt would consider legal steps if \uc2 ECB\uc2  implemented helicopter money policy, would ask courts to clarify the limits of \uc2 ECB\uc2  mandate; govt denies considering legal action, \uc2 ECB\uc2  only independent within the framework of mandate.\par \par \uc2 Germany's Schaeuble says should encourage Fed, \uc2 ECB\uc2 , BoE to slowly exit easy mon pol, excessive liquidity has become more a cause of problem than solution.\par \par \uc2 Austria's FMA announced 54% haircut on senior Heta bonds, extends maturities to 2023, coupons cancelled as of March last year, sub debt haircut 100%.\par \par \uc2 Greece talks with lenders edge closer to compromise, Reuters reports, divergence remains over pension reforms and regulating NPLs; Schaeuble optimistic deal can be found, but no debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Italy govt calls meeting for today to finalise state-backed bank fund plan; fund's mandate still be decided, maybe buying NPLs, helping recap of weaker banks; fund cap max 5bn, CdP contributing max 300m.\par \par \uc2 Obama will meet Yellen this afternoon to discuss economy, Wall Street reform; VP Biden will also attend.\par \par \uc2 Asia: China Mar CPI prints at 2.3% vs 2.4% consensus, PPI -4.3% vs -4.6%; Mar auto sales +7.8% y/y.\par \par \uc2 Japan Feb machine orders print at -9.2% m/m vs -12%.\par \par \uc2 Japan Chief Cab Sec Suga says seeing speculative moves in markets; will take necessary action on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed; G20 agreement does not mean \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in response to one-sided moves.\par \par \uc2 Oil: Ecuador Energy Min says producers will not discuss prod cuts at Doha; Venezuela oil min says 18 counties confirmed for Doha meeting, first step to freeze output; if that doesn't work, second step is to reduce volumes; fair price $50-60.\par \par \uc2 Other: StanChart looking to sell at least $4.4bn of assets in Asia, including $1.4bn of stressed loans extended to Indian companies, according to sources.\par \par \uc2 On the agenda:\par \par \uc2 Data: Europe: Italian industrial output at 9am.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC CALENDAR:\par \par \uc2 See Reuters TGM2417, Bloomberg TG2417, or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ifrmarkets.com/rates/global/economics/global-economic-calend"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.ifrmarkets.com/rates/global/economics/global-economic-calend}}}\uc2  ar\par \par \uc2 PRIMARY MARKET MANDATES and NEWS:\par \par \uc2 COVERED:\par \par \uc2 MunHyp is marketing a 10yr hypo pfand benchmark at MS flat area. Pricing today via DZ, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , LBBW (B&D) and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . Aaa.\par \par \uc2 DNB Boligkreditt A/S has mandated \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 , \uc2 NordLB\uc2  and \uc2 RBS\uc2  for a 7year EUR prime Norwegian residential mortgage covered bond benchmark. Expected Aaa/AAA.\par \par \uc2 For the full pipeline of upcoming deals see Reuters TGM17546, Bloomberg TG17546, or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ifrmarkets.com/credit/europe/deal-pipeline/international-eur"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.ifrmarkets.com/credit/europe/deal-pipeline/international-eur}}}\uc2  ope-asia\par \par \uc2 All of the day's pricings on Reuters TGM17522, Bloomberg TG17522, or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ifrmarkets.com/credit/europe/deal-summary/international-euro"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.ifrmarkets.com/credit/europe/deal-summary/international-euro}}}\uc2  pe-asia (Reporting by Adam Parry, editing by Julian Baker)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-14T07:03:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160411ec4b00hft\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
