\par\par \b \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 : Yen's Long-Term Fair Value Is At Near 90 -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 02:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Why is the yen so strong?\par \par \uc2 A dovish \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  aside, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be ineffective because the yen is already way overvalued, according t o \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Koon How Heng:\par \par \uc2 There is increasing concern in the market that the BoJ may find it difficult to stop JPY strength. This is because the JPY has already depreciated significantly from its level three years There is increasing concern in the market that the BoJ may find it difficult to stop JPY strength. This is because the JPY has already depreciated significantly from its level three years.\par \par \uc2 In fact, former IMF deputy chief Naoyuki Shinohara acknowledged this point about the JPY's cheap valuation when he was quoted as saying, "it is clear that the JPY is not extremely strong on a real effective basis." Shinohara added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market to stem JPY rises" and that "conducting solo JPY selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now would be ineffective and unlikely to gain G7 consent." Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was also recently quoted by the Wall Street Journal as saying, "we must definitely avoid competitive devaluation in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market" and "should refrain from arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Both Shinohara and Abe's comments were widely seen as the trigger for pushing USD/JPY below 110. Needless to say, \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) has protested JPY strength and warned that such "one sided appreciation" of the JPY is undesirable.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been strong lately also because foreign investors are losing faith in Abenomics. Foreigners tend to conduct a pair trade of buying Japanese stocks and selling yen, taking advantage of the inverse relationship between the two to limit losses. So when they unwind the long stock trade, they also close the short yen hedge. Indeed, \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  has joined the Japan pullout, Bloomberg reported this morning. So did hedge fund LGT. This is why \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  suggested the BoJ buys stock ETFs big time to pause the yen's rise. See also my last Friday's blog " UPDATE: How Far Will The Yen Go? BoJ Stock ETF Purchases Seen".\par \par \uc2 So what's next for the yen? Heng has more:\par \par \uc2 Given the balance of policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, our view for USD/JPY is currently neutral for both 3M and 12M. But the immediate bias is likely that of JPY strength. Technical indicators also point to downside in USD/JPY. Now that the key 111/110.50 support is broken, the next potential downside targets are 108/105, followed by 100.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the yen edged higher again, up 0.2% to 107.92, while the Nikkei 225 widened losses, down 1.4% recently. Financials led the losses. \uc2 Mizuho Financial\uc2  fell 1.9%, \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial\uc2  dropped 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 iShares MSCI Japan ETF\uc2  (EWJ) gained 2.9%, the \uc2 iShares \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hedged MSCI Japan ETF\uc2  (HEWJ) was up 2.7%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\p