\par\par \b \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  Buys Stake in Founders Entertainment -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 17:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:29 ET - \uc2 Live Nation Entertainment\uc2  (LYV) says it acquired a majority stake in Founders Entertainment, the concert promoter behind Governors Ball Music Festival, long held in June on Randall's Island in New York. The move pits \uc2 Live Nation\uc2  against Anschutz Entertainment, which is launching its own New York festival, Panorama, on Randall's Island in July. Both companies are aiming to secure more artist tours and sponsors by broadening their festival portfolios. (Hannah.karp@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:18 ET - The dollar has fallen below Y108 again this morning as investors continue to brush off Japan's verbal efforts to halt its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. The greenback was up as much as 0.3% earlier after a Japanese official reiterated the BoJ's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary. The official added that a pact among world leaders to not competitively devalue \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't explicitly prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening the yen. But \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's Shahab Jalinoos says investors aren't buying it--due in part to the political hurdles \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would face to a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar is now down 0.2% today at Y107.88. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:15 ET - \uc2 Southwestern Energy\uc2  (SWN) CEO Bill Way says it's "possible" company would again draw down its revolver and immediately repay amount to boost liquidity. Last month SWN borrowed $1.55B from its revolver for two days before repaying it in order to boost the cash on its books at quarter's end, which in turn increased the amount of secured debt it could take on by $232M under its credit agreement. SWN up 3.2% at $8.96. (ryan.dezember@wsj.com; @RyanDezember)\par \par \uc2 11:10 ET - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  calls the 20% recovery in \uc2 United Natural Foods\uc2 ' (UNFI) shares since plunging on a 2Q earnings warnings "unwarranted" as it cuts the stock to underweight. Analysts worry about slowing growth at it's largest customer, \uc2 Whole Foods (WFM)\uc2 , and pressure faced by its core packaged food business from a shift to a preference for fresh foods. While UNFI has been beefing-up fresh offerings through acquisitions, the roll-out will likely take time. Morgan expects that to create "margin pressure, we believe is likely to persist." UNFI falls 5.5% to $34.99, down 53% in the past year. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:09 ET - Recently trading through February's raised price target of $38, RBC makes a much-bigger $10 boost on Restaurant Brands (QSR) as the investment bank sees continued strength for both \uc2 Burger King\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Hortons\uc2 . While boosting companywide forecasts through 2017, RBC doubles its 1Q same-store-sales-growth view on \uc2 Burger King\uc2 's domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 4%. "BK has weathered the storm of competitive discounting with its own value platforms" while "hot dogs also contributed to 1Q growth." This as the bank thinks \uc2 Tim Hortons\uc2  will continue to wring costs out of its supply chain, creating a 1.5-percentage-point margin boost following a 2-point benefit last year. QSR rises 4.9% to $39.89, returning to late-October levels. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 11:05 ET - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's Fear Barometer, which measures the cost of bearish put options relative to bullish calls on the S&P 500, rose to a record 44.7% on Friday, signaling muted expectations for stock-market gains the next 3 months. Friday's increase for the gauge, which goes back to 1994, was entirely driven by lower demand for call options, the investment bank notes. Typically, an increase in its fear index is driven by both higher put and lower call demand. The metric's new high come as the \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Volatility Index fell on Friday and remains well below its long-term average. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's Mandy Xu says, "The derivatives market is assigning less than 1% probability the market will rise by 10% in the next 3 months versus 17% probability it will fall by 10%." (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 10:52 ET - After concluding the acquisition of a minority stakes in its peers in Mexico and Chile, Brazil stock exchange and futures-market operator BM&F Bovespa SA (BVMF3.BR) is planning to acquire minority stakes in bourses in Peru, Colombia and Argentina, says Edemir Pinto, the head of \uc2 BM&F Bovespa\uc2 . The Brazilian exchange is planning to assume stakes of between 5% to 15% in those bourses during 2016 as part of its plans to expand across the region, Pinto says at a press conference. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:51 ET - \uc2 Comcast\uc2 's (CMCSA) online-ticketing service Fandango is taking the first steps of capitalizing on its recent deal with \uc2 Time Warner\uc2  (TWX)'s \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . The company will begin selling movie tickets on Flixster and Rotten Tomatoes, two sites it acquired from \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . earlier this month. The acquisition deal, which awarded Warner an equity stake in the company, continues Fandango's efforts to grow from just a ticket utility site to a hub of entertainment content. Fandango will also roll out a ticketing technology at several theater chains that eliminates the need for ushers to scan moviegoers' phones to process a mobile ticket. (Erich.schwartzel@wsj.com; @erichschwartzel)\par \par \uc2 10:46 ET - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  turned bearish on \uc2 Under Armour\uc2  (\uc2 UA\uc2 ) 3 months ago, then highlighting data showing the company's apparel market share was declining and falling average selling prices were accelerating. Fresh numbers show a slight drop for \uc2 UA\uc2  on wholesale apparel in 1Q, notably on the women's side, to extend what was seen in 4Q. But while \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  today says "\uc2 UA\uc2  has near-term revenue drivers to meet or beat Street 1Q expectations," the apparel weakness and "soft running-footwear trends [will] ultimately cause a future" sales miss and P/E contraction. So it's remaining at underweight as the bank's price target is now a split-adjusted $32, a level nearly reached in January. \uc2 UA\uc2  is down 3.2% today at $42.14, putting it near the bottom of the S&P 500. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 10:42 ET - Sandler O'Neill cuts \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  (WFC) to hold amid broader concerns about the banking industry. The firm had upgraded the SF-based bank to buy late last summer after market turbulence but "since that time, much about the environment for bank stocks has changed" such as the flattening yield curve, energy exposure and the expectation of fewer interest-rate increases. The bank has still maintained many of its strengths though, like management and performance metrics. "While none of these company-specific concerns is necessarily critical, they do give us some pause," the firm says. Sandler cuts WFC price target to $51, down from $55 in January and $59 over the summer; the bank reports 1Q earnings Thursday. WFC up 0.7% to $47.42 as broad market gains. (emily.glazer@wsj.com; @EmilyGlazerWSJ)\par \par \uc2 10:35 ET - The anti-fossil-fuel trend seems to be snowballing as drivers eye Tesla (TSLA) electric cars, renewables like wind and solar gain ground and low oil prices pressure the industry. So, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  today asks the burning question: "Can the world run without oil?" It answers with a quick and emphatic no. "The world would literally grind to a halt if oil was not available." Bjorn Eberhardt, head of global macro research, says even while advanced economies become less-dependent on oil, emerging economies like China are using more than ever. We can't "get around oil even if we would like to." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:31 ET - Mexico's above-estimate 2.6% rise in February industrial production from a year before was encouraging, but it's too early to suggest the sector has turned a corner," says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . "Against a backdrop of a looming fiscal squeeze and continued softness in US industry, we don't expect Mexican industrial-production growth to accelerate much from here." February's performance was helped by Leap Day. Seasonally adjusted, production eased 0.1% from January. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2016 11:29 ET (15:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160411ec4b002f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
