\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Aren't Buying Japan's Warnings on Yen -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 142 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 17:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:18 ET - The dollar has fallen below Y108 again this morning as investors continue to brush off Japan's verbal efforts to halt its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. The greenback was up as much as 0.3% earlier after a Japanese official reiterated the BoJ's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary. The official added that a pact among world leaders to not competitively devalue \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't explicitly prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening the yen. But \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's Shahab Jalinoos says investors aren't buying it--due in part to the political hurdles \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would face to a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar is now down 0.2% today at Y107.88. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2016 11:18 ET (15:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160411ec4b002el\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
