\par\par \b \uc2 Shanghai Market Rebounds After Lower-Than-Expected Inflation Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 04:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Chinese shares rally Monday morning, as the country's inflation appears more benign, and while the market recovers from the over 2% declines in the last two sessions. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index jumps 1.7% to 3036.48, led by gains in steel, coal and brokerage sectors. China's CPI rose 2.3% from a year earlier in March, lower than the expected 2.5%. Investors got a further confidence boost after Ren Zeping, an analyst at Guotai Junan Securities, projected late Sunday that there's potential for a further 20% gain in the A-share market and urged investors to add positions. \uc2 Dongxing Securities\uc2  (601198.SH) jumps 5.9% while \uc2 Huatai Securities\uc2  rallies 4.9%. The Shenzhen Composite Index advances 1.9% to 1949.72 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2 -style ChiNext board jumps 2.3% to 2280.43. (Yifan.xie@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The number of Australian housing finance approvals ticked up in February (+1.5% on month vs the 2.2% rise forecast by economists). The figures confirm other indicators suggesting markets have had a better start to 2016 after a weak finish to 2015, \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's Matthew Hassan says. The value of loans to owner-occupiers rose 1.7%, recovering about half of January's 4.8% fall. Hassan says the holiday low period tends to make monthly housing data choppier and less reliable at the start of the year. Overall, the figures point to a "relatively steady start to the new year, something also apparent from auction market activity." The housing market has been one of the few stand-out sectors of the economy as a long mining investment boom ends, though many economists think housing construction may already have peaked. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com; Twitter: @RachelPannett) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0212 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/USD remains in a bullish chart pattern suggesting that the euro will keep gaining versus the U.S. dollar in the near term. The daily Bollinger uptrend channel will be reaffirmed if EUR/USD ends Monday above 1.1386. There is a risk that the euro will fall out of the uptrend channel. The continued appreciation of Japan's yen is exerting pressure on EUR/JPY, a commonly-traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair that has been declining since the start of April due to the yen's seemingly unstoppable appreciation. The yen has just printed a new 18-month peak versus the U.S. dollar, and could keep going, as the political obstacles for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appear too big for now. If USD/JPY keeps sliding, EUR/JPY will follow, depressing the euro generally. EUR/USD is now 1.1408 from its Friday close of 1.1399. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0209 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Investors shouldn't misinterpret the USD/JPY's declines as Tokyo's inability to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and launch extra monetary easing, says Nomura Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Yunosuke Ikeda. In the G20 meetings this week, "we are going to see the same old things" such as an agreement to refrain from competitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  devaluation. But Ikeda contends that Japan finance minister Taro Aso's jawboning on Friday, with the comment that the yen movement is one-sided, is crucial. Whether Aso would change his rhetoric to something more subjective, such as saying that the yen's level doesn't reflect fundamentals, for example, may provide clues as to how close Tokyo is to actually stepping into the market. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's March consumer inflation came in lower than market expectations thanks to falling prices in food and service sectors, says Yan Ling, an economist with China Merchants Securities. But the inflationary rate is expected to continue to rise to 2.6% in the second quarter of the year given the shortage of pork supply, Yan predicts. The rising CPI together with recovering producer prices, which saw the first on-month increase since January 2014, will likely curb \f28 \uc2 \f2 China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ability to further ease its monetary policy and thus will bring a negative impact to the economy, Yan says. (grace.zhu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0206 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's pork prices will likely remain elevated in the coming months but won't be high enough to affect the country's monetary policy, says Zhang Fan, an economist of RHB. "Inflation expectations rose as people worry that we may see another round of surging inflation due to rapidly rising pork prices like what happened in 2008," Zhang says. "But I don't think it's the case this year." The statistics bureau reduced the weightings of food prices in its basket of goods and services in the consumer-price index, which could partly explain why the headline figure came in lower than market expectations, he says. Pork prices will likely slow within three quarters and the CPI will likely climb 2.5% for the full year, he says. China's CPI rose 2.3% in March from a year earlier, the same as February's tally, and was lower than economists' median projection of 2.5%. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0206 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/MYR is lower as crude oil prices rally and the outlook for U.S. interest rates stays dovish. Friday's surge in oil prices, which saw the international benchmark Brent rise 6.4% to nearly its highest point this year, is positive for risk appetite and for Malaysia's oil-related revenues. The possibility of oil producers agreeing to reduce output at a meeting this weekend has spurred bullish oil bets. Meanwhile, the dovish stance of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  looks unlikely to change anytime soon, keeping the dollar broadly depressed. In addition to these macroeconomic factors, the rising inflow of foreign investments into Malaysia, mainly from China, is also positive for the ringgit. The Chinese have made massive purchases of Malaysian companies, and have also ramped up buying of its government bonds this year. USD/MYR is now 3.8940 from its Friday close of 3.9000. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0202 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  report says sustained economic growth in Asia will require continued structural reforms as the region faces a raft of headwinds. "The region has benefited from careful macroeconomic policies, including efforts to boost revenue in commodity-exporting countries," said Victoria Kwakwa, incoming World Bank East Asia and Pacific regional vice president. Yet now the region faces sluggish growth in high-income countries as well as emerging markets, weak trade, a slump in commodity prices, and increasingly volatile financial markets. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  forecasts East Asia will grow by 6.3% in 2016 and 6.2% in 2017-18, down from 6.5% in 2015, dragged down mainly by China's slowdown. Outside of China, growth in large Southeast Asian economies such as the Philippines, Vietnam and Indonesia, should pull up regional growth to 4.8% in 2016 and 4.9% in 2017-18, up from 4.7% in 2015. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0201 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rakuten Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief strategist Masayuki Kubota says investors should stay on guard against more declines in Tokyo shares. "Given that G-20 nations are scheduled to meet in Washington this Thursday, it is likely to be difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market ahead of the meeting," he says. "If the yen's rises accelerate, the Nikkei is likely to be sold off further." The Nikkei Stock Average is currently down 1.7% at 15549.24. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kubota\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the index to be in a tug of war between selling by foreign investors and dip-buying by domestic players. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0201 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects economies in East Asia and Pacific, excluding China, to grow 4.8% rate this year and 4.9% in the following two years, as the bigger nations in Southeast Asia gain traction. The region grew 4.7% in 2015. The Philippines and Vietnam have the strongest growth prospects among Southeast Asian economies, with both expected to grow by more than 6% this year, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Indonesia may grow 5.1% in 2016 and 5.3% in 2017, if the recent reform measures are successful and an ambitious public investment program is implemented. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  advises Asian nations to push policies that reduce their exposure to global and regional risks and improve their credibility and market confidence, especially in countries where growth has been sustained by commodity exports or borrowing. Overall, growth in the region will ease modestly to 6.3% in 2016 and 6.2% in 2017 after growing 6.5% in 2015. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China should strengthen market discipline in the financial sector by allowing credit allocation to be more market-driven, gradually expose those parts of the economy dominated by state-owned enterprises to greater competition and continue to reform the household-registration system, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a semiannual economic report on Asia and the Pacific. It also urged a shift in public spending from infrastructure to public services, such as education, health, and social assistance, and also towards environmental protection. (mark.magnier@wsj.com; @MarkMagnier) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0200 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's economy is forecast to grow by 6.7% in 2016 and 6.5% in 2017 assuming it moves ahead with structural and financial reforms aimed at reducing vulnerabilities, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a semiannual economic report on Asia and the Pacific. Asian economies closely linked to China could be vulnerable, it added. "Developing East Asia and Pacific faces elevated risks, including a weaker-than-expected recovery in high-income economies and a faster-than-expected slowdown in China," said World Bank economist Sudhir Shetty. "At the same time, policy makers have less room to maneuver in setting macroeconomic policy." (mark.magnier@wsj.com; Twitter: @MarkMagnier) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 10, 2016 22:14 ET (02:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160411ec4b0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
