\par\par \b \uc2 USD/KRW Opens Down but Firms up With Eye on USD/JPY -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 02:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/KRW opened slightly lower Monday, but is recovering fast, as the yen flirts with an 18-month high versus the dollar, rousing fresh speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  disrupts the market to weaken the yen, the South Korea won is the most vulnerable among regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to collateral damage. The won is closely linked to the yen, due to \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 's vigilant watch over the yen-won exchange rate. The BOK aims to keep the won relatively undervalued versus the yen for export competitiveness, and doesn't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the balance is tipped excessively against the won. USD/KRW is now 1,154.0 from its Friday close of 1,153.8, hovering near the 1,149 entrance of the Bollinger uptrend channel that provides additional support for the pair. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 10, 2016 20:39 ET (00:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160411ec4b0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
