\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Suga: G20 pact does not rule out  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2016\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (This is a repeat of an item issued on Sunday)\par \par \uc2 * Yen rally vs dollar has raised concerns in Japan govt\par \par \uc2 * Strong yen hurts corp earnings, causes deflation pressure\par \par \uc2 * Suga: PM Abe's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments misunderstood\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  mulling econ policy coordination for \uc2 G7\uc2  summit\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White and Takashi Umekawa\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 10 (Reuters) - Japan's top government spokesman said the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2 's agreement to avoid competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation does not mean \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in response to one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the government are tensely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and the government is prepared to take appropriate steps as needed, Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshihide Suga told Reuters, but declined to comment on what steps the government was considering.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's comment to the Wall Street Journal last week that countries should avoid "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," was misunderstood and does not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Suga said.\par \par \uc2 "What the G20 is talking about is arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is different from responding to a one-sided move," Suga told Reuters in an interview on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "The prime minister's comments were based on the G20 understanding that long-term manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undesirable."\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a fresh 17-month low versus the yen last week on expectations that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates very slowly.\par \par \uc2 The yen has gained more than 10 percent against dollar this year, as investors seek the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 A rising yen tends to worry government officials because it decreases exporters' earnings and makes it more difficult to shake off deflation by pushing down import prices.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011. At the time, Tokyo got \uc2 G7\uc2  consent to stem a yen spike driven by speculation that a devastating earthquake and nuclear disaster in March would force Japanese insurers to repatriate funds to pay claims.\par \par \uc2 Some traders have said Japan cannot sell its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, because the G20 warned countries in February to refrain from competitive devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Suga, who coordinates other ministers in Abe's cabinet, rejected this idea outright and said Abe's remarks about arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a Wall Street Journal interview last week were misunderstood.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ's decision in January to adopt negative interest rates has drawn criticism from some countries that say it is a direct attempt to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ says it is using negative rates to meet its price target, a move Suga supports. However, some people are worried the policy will erode their savings, so the BOJ needs to carefully explain the impact of its policies, he said.\par \par \uc2 Suga also rejected the argument that the adoption of negative rates was a sign the BOJ's attempts to meet its 2-percent price target had reached a limit.\par \par \uc2 Abe is meeting foreign economists to prepare to host a summit of \uc2 G7\uc2  finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in May, where he will urge other countries to coordinate policies to accelerate global growth.\par \par \uc2 "The prime minister strongly believes \uc2 G7\uc2  should lead the global economy with sustainable growth," Suga said.\par \par \uc2 "The economy will be the most important item on the agenda, so we need to consider what policies are necessary."\par \par \uc2 Some economists say the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit could pave the way for more fiscal spending by Japan, but Abe is still deciding the best approach for the summit, Suga said.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy could contract for a second consecutive quarter in the January to March period, which would meet the technical definition of a recession and fuel calls for more stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Suga acknowledged that consumer spending was weak, but said an improving labour market and a tourism boom meant the economy remained on track to grow.\par \par \uc2 Suga reiterated the government's view that it will raise the nationwide sales tax to 10 percent from 8 percent next year as scheduled, but some advisers close to Abe are calling for the plan to be shelved. (Editing by Clarence Fernandez)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-13T21:00:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160410ec4a00u65\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
