\par\par \b \uc2 Only  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in Way of Rising Yen -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2016\par 08:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries about a U.S. backlash if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened by its direct action \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Gregor Stuart Hunter in Hong Kong and Hiroyuki Kachi and Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo \f28 \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise to its strongest level in 17 months this past week has left traders around the world pondering one big question: Just how far can the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  go?\par \par \uc2 The answer now appears to have as much to do with politics as economics.\par \par \uc2 After inching up 0.1% Friday to Yen108.06 against the U.S. dollar in late New York trading -- the highest since late October 2014 -- the yen is up 11% versus the greenback this year, making it Asia's best-performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The surge has come despite the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive easing of monetary policy since 2013, which culminated this year in its setting negative interest rates in January for the first time ever.\par \par \uc2 The yen's resilience is largely thanks to powerful external factors that have outweighed the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's intentions. Earlier in the year, amid market turmoil stemming from sharp drops in China's stock markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fears about the health of Europe's banking sector, investors flocked to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a traditional haven at times of turbulence. Recent dollar weakness, because of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's more dovish stance on interest rates, has helped the yen keep on rising.\par \par \uc2 Strategists predicting the yen's path are ripping up their forecasts for 2016 now. With investors seemingly ignoring the BOJ's interest-rate cuts, many now say the only impediment to the yen's continued strengthening is direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities -- a move that in turn is subject to hard-to-gauge issues such as how prepared Tokyo is to annoy other countries.\par \par \uc2 "Political judgment is the most important factor" in making a decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Junya Tanase, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , who now expects the yen to reach Yen103 to the dollar by the end of 2016, compared with his forecast at the start of the year that it would reach Yen110.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been shy about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the past. It did so as recently as 2011, when it sold yen to weaken a surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was threatening to derail its recovery from the Fukushima disaster.\par \par \uc2 Japan's chief cabinet secretary, Yoshihide Suga, on Thursday fueled speculation that policy makers soon could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, by selling off the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen holdings, when he described the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent moves as "one-sided." Policy makers used such language to describe trading in the yen ahead of the BOJ's last two big market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2010 and 2011.\par \par \uc2 But this time around, Japanese officials are worried about the potential U.S. backlash that could ensue if they weaken the yen by direct action. Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  told The Wall Street Journal that countries should refrain from "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and "competitive devaluation." His remarks, which surprised traders who had been expecting Tokyo officials to take a harder line, likely reflect his desire to avoid friction with the U.S., a person familiar with the matter said Friday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. presidential candidates Donald Trump and Hillary Clinton have both effectively labeled Japan a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on the campaign trail, unnerving Tokyo policy makers, the person said. Any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would create negative publicity, especially when the BOJ is generally suspected of having undertaken aggressive monetary easing to undercut the yen, the person added.\par \par \uc2 Japan also feels bound by an accord accompanying a trans-Pacific trade deal that requires members not to cheapen \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a trade advantage, the person said.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities also may be unwilling to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hosts a meeting of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  leading nations in late May, analysts say. Just last month, finance officials from the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  industrial and developing nations agreed not to engage in competitive devaluations as global growth slows.\par \par \uc2 Some think the yen would have to rise a lot further before the BOJ acts. Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stays above Yen100 against the dollar, but would be easier to understand if it slips to Yen80 or Yen90, said Daisaku Ueno, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The yen is giving a lot of people some headaches," said Roy Teo, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2 , who says the yen could strengthen to Yen106 before the BOJ steps in. "The perception is that after the G-20 meeting, the hurdles for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are higher."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high current-account surplus could put further upward pressure on the yen. It jumped 64% to Yen2.43 trillion ($22.46 billion) in February alone, according to data released Friday.\par \par \uc2 The yen could strengthen to Yen105 against the dollar by the end of the year and to around Yen100 by early 2017, thanks to the strong trade balance, \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "We think there could be a limit to how much the negative-interest-rate policy and the dovish comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials help to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Irene Cheung, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 --Ewen Chew in Singapore contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com, Hiroyuki Kachi at hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com and Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 09, 2016 02:01 ET (06:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160409ec4900023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
