\par\par \b \uc2 TREASURIES OUTLOOK-U.S. yields rise on oil gains, 30-year yield falls for 4th straight week\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 22:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * U.S. bond yields up on oil gains, rate hike outlook\par \par \uc2 * Yellen's comments on economy boost demand for risky assets\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk cuts volatility, drives markets (Refiles to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 By Dion Rabouin\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  debt yields rose from six-week lows on Friday, backed by a surge in oil prices and Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's comments on interest-rate hikes, but fell for the second consecutive week with 30-year bond yields falling for the fourth week in a row.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures surged about 6 percent as encouraging economic indicators in the United States and Europe boosted hopes of rising demand.\par \par \uc2 "We seem to be being pulled in a lot of different directions for a variety of reasons," said \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  head of U.S. rates strategy Subadra Rajappa. "But I would say that the correlation with oil is number one on the list."\par \par \uc2 After Thursday's market close, Yellen said in a rare appearance with former Fed chairmen \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 , Alan Greenspan and Paul Volcker that the U.S. economy was close to full strength. She said inflation would not be held down much longer by the strong dollar and low oil prices, putting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on track for further rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Yellen had warned last week that tepid global growth could threaten the U.S. economy, saying the Fed should look to "cautiously" raise rates. The warnings echoed sentiment expressed after the March 16 Federal Open Market Committee meeting and have pushed investors' focus to happenings outside the U.S., analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "Before it was all about China and an intense focus on various data points. More recently it's been Japan," said \uc2 Raman Srivastava\uc2 , deputy chief investment officer at Standish, a BNY Mellon investment company.\par \par \uc2 "Given the volatility that we've seen in the Japanese government bond market as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think some of that volatility is finding its way into other markets in fixed income."\par \par \uc2 Early Friday, Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso warned that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were "undesirable." He said the yen's recent rally that led to a 17-month high against the dollar on Thursday was "one-sided" and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take steps as needed to address it.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would strengthen the dollar against the yen, reduce volatility in markets and drive \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields and riskier assets higher, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year Treasuries fell 8/32 in price as their yields rose nearly 3 basis points to 1.716 percent. Those yields had fallen to their lowest since Feb. 24 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Prices on 30-year Treasuries fell by 23/32 with yields rising 3 basis points to 2.549 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Dion Rabouin; Editing by Bernadette Baum and Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-11T20:15:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160408ec4801ly6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
