\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks, USD/JPY Give Up Bounce as Week Wraps -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 21:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:19 ET - Some markets are moving to the flat line as the week comes to a close. Stocks have shed what this morning was near-1% gains and the dollar has given up nearly all of its rebound versus the yen, trading up a fraction at Y108.27 after earlier rising as much as 0.8%. The yen has been on a tear this year, threatening Japan's efforts to stoke growth through a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cheaper exports. \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  says the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise if the dollar falls to Y100--a situation the analysts see as increasingly likely. The dollar hasn't traded at that level since 2013. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:10 ET - Equity allocations in the 100 largest corporate pension plans dropped to 37% last year, according to Milliman, the lowest level in the 16-year history of the firm's annual pension study. Companies are moving funds out of stocks and into fixed income amid efforts to reduce volatility, but that comes at a price: Lower plan returns. Meanwhile, the plans contributed $31B of cash to the funds, versus $40B in 2014; Milliman says that's likely the result of the pension-funding relief provision in the 2015 federal budget. (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 13:11 ET - Beyond DBRS cooling its outlook on Spain amid the inability to form a coalition government is the country's growing budget deficit. Spain reported a 5% budget deficit in 2015 despite economic growth of 3.2%. "Spain has been viewed as the poster child for reform," notes DBRS. "Now, it once again has experienced significant fiscal slippage. The question is, in the absence of a government, will the country resume its responsible fiscal path and continue with structural reforms?" (jeannette.neumann@wsj.com; @JENeumann)\par \par \uc2 12:33 ET - Fitch says sovereign downgrades doubled upgrades in 2015 after modestly trailing upgrades in 2014. The count includes 11 emerging-market sovereigns getting cut, versus 8 in 2014, as upgrades fell to 5 from 9. Meanwhile, developed-market downgrades and upgrades "were closely aligned with 2014." Fitch recorded one sovereign default in 2015--Ukraine. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1628 GMT - DBRS drops to stable from positive its ratings trend on Spain, reflecting the inability of the main political parties to form a coalition government following general elections on Dec. 20 as well as the risk that a new government, when established, will roll back labor-market reforms and fiscal-consolidation measures. Given the inconclusive electoral outcome, there is "greater uncertainty" about the future direction of economic policies, it adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 15:49 GMT - Mexico is likely to meet most of South Africa's additional demand for white maize, says GrainSA economist Wandile Sihlobo. If South Africa meets its corn production target of 7.1 million metric tons, he estimates it will be able to meet its needs and those of most of its neighbors. The exception is Zimbabwe, which typically imports white maize--the kind of corn used for food across the region--from Zambia. Zambia this week suspended exports, however. The extra demand from Zimbabwe will keep white maizes high in South Africa, even as the harvest progresses, incentivizing Mexican exporters to ship the grain, he says. (ed.ballard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1533 GMT While the euro corporate bond market's spotlight is on European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  QE-eligible issuers, Citi has identified opportunities in non-European names' euro bonds. It favors companies whose euro-denominated bonds look cheap compared with their dollar-denominated couterparts, including \uc2 Pemex\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , Brazil's BRF and \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2 . These companies' euro yield spreads are wide compared to the equivalent dollar rates. However, Citi warns against comparing just the spreads of similar euro and dollar bonds from the same issuer: The average gap stands at 56 bps but shrinks to 5 bps after applying basis swap costs and different swap conventions. (tasos.vossos@dowjones.com, @tasosvos) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1505 GMT Recent EUR/DKK falls raise the risk the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem krona gains. The DNB pegs its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the euro and aims to keep EUR/DKK within a very tight range. Rabobank notes Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lars Rohde\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday the bank was ready to print money to save the EUR/DKK peg. However, if the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has to ease monetary policy this may stoke medium-term upside inflation risks. "There is a risk that speculators may attempt to force the DNB to choose between the two," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. This risk will rise if Danish inflation rises faster than in core eurozone. EUR/DKK last trades at 7.4410.(jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1456 GMT U.S-based Digital Realty tapped the euro corporate bond market with a benchmark-size issue Friday. Bankers involved say the company will use the EUR600 million it raised to refinance a revolving credit facility. Orders were said to have exceeded EUR1 billion earlier today. The 8-year bond is priced at 235 bps above mid-swaps. Meanwhile, French carmaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peugeot\f28 \uc2 \f2  got away a benchmark bond of its own, adding some depth to what has been a subdued primary high yield market this year, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (tasos.vossos@dowjones.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:48 ET - Economists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Nova Scotia\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Canada's March jobs report needs to treated with the proverbial grain of salt. The report indicated a 40.6K gain in the month, with bulk full-time positions and concentrated in private sector. Scotiabank says numbers have "discounted in quality terms," because there's little explanation regarding wild swings in month-over-month numbers on health care, education and tourism over past few months. Employment in those three sectors fell 48K in February, yet rose 50K in March. Manufacturing also posted a surprise 31.8K decline in jobs in March, which firm said was another "dent" in overall strong report. "We'll take the solid headline, but not without strong caveats," Scotiabank says. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1439 GMT Data showing a bigger-than-expected U.K. global trade in goods deficit of GBP12 billion means the 1Q current account deficit could exceed 4Q's record 7% of GDP, Berenberg analysts say. This increases the potential for a "sterling crisis" if the U.K. votes to exit the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a June 23 referendum. Berenberg notes the U.K.'s current account deficit is the biggest in the G7, and has grown from being a "modest" problem to a "serious" one. A "Brexit" could mean investors suddenly fear the U.K. "can no longer meet its obligations later on without a massive devaluation. They would therefore quickly attempt to exit sterling positions. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1411 GMT Spaniards are likely to have to vote again on June 26 after the umpteenth round of negotiations between three major parties fell through Friday, Teneo Intelligence political risk analyst Antonio Barroso says. Talks between the center-left Socialists, centrist Ciudadanos and far-left Podemos collapsed on Friday. Mr. Barroso says there's an 80% probability of new elections. "The chances of a last minute agreement are dim," Mr. Barroso says. New elections have to be called on May 2 if no deal has been reached. (jeannette.neumann@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 08, 2016 15:19 ET (19:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160408ec480031i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
