\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Stronger yen heightens pressure on \uc2 Bank of Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 06:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Quentin Webb\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, April 8 (Reuters Breakingviews) - A stronger yen heightens the pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 . Sharp moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  set back Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's plan to revitalise \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - selling yen and buying dollars - would be unpopular abroad. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , already deep in experimental territory, may need to do more.\par \par \uc2 As of early afternoon in Tokyo on April 8, one dollar bought about 108.60 yen, versus more than 120 at the start of the year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resurgence owes something to the weaker dollar, but also reflects Japan's unlikely but enduring status as a "safe haven": a place to park money amid rising concerns about U.S. presidential elections and Britain's potential exit from the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , among other things.\par \par \uc2 A key plank of "Abenomics" has been huge amounts of BOJ bond-buying, which helped to take the yen from 85 to about 120 against the dollar in the two years after Abe took office, and handed record profits to Japan's export-heavy corporate sector. So the partial reversal is a headache: the threat to earnings has knocked the stock market, in turn denting investor sentiment and confidence in Japan's reforms. A stronger yen also means less imported inflation and bodes ill for government coffers because it translates into lower taxes on wages and profits.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials are trying to talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down. But if the jawboning proves ineffective, it's not obvious what else they can do. Simply selling yen would undercut Abe's recent statement, echoing February's \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  agreement, that countries should avoid "competitive devaluation" and "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". Such a move would be unpopular in Washington and elsewhere, and especially awkward as Japan prepares to host a summit of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  developed countries in May.\par \par \uc2 That puts the spotlight on the BOJ, whose next policy meeting is later this month. It could conceivably buy even more bonds, focus more on riskier assets, or push deeper into negative interest rates. But the BOJ already presides over an enormous balance sheet, with the bond-buying programme totalling a huge 80 trillion yen a year. Meanwhile, January's surprise introduction of negative rates has not only proved deeply unpopular but is still bedding down. The BOJ cannot take the risk of letting markets think it has run out of ammunition, so it may want to hold back. The newly robust yen could be here to stay.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/qtwebb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/qtwebb}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso on April 8 ratcheted up the warning against a rapid rise in the yen, saying the government would take steps as needed to counter "one-sided" moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 - A day earlier, the dollar fell below 108 yen for the first time in 17 months with investors betting that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - Aso called the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's recent moves "one-sided", and said rapid movements were undesirable, reiterating verbal warnings made by Japanese officials on April 7.\par \par \uc2 - "A rapid move toward either yen rise or yen fall is not desirable. It is desirable that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are stable at levels that match the economy's fundamentals," Aso told reporters after a cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: Japan faces trouble controlling damaging yen rises\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: Japan officials step up warning against yen rise\par \par \uc2 RELATED COLUMNS\par \par \uc2 E for arrow\par \par \uc2 Payday moans\par \par \uc2 Code red\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-11T04:37:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160408ec480089b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
