\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET SNAPSHOT: U.S. Stocks Close Marginally Higher But Post Worst Weekly Loss Since February\b0\par\par\uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 23:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Sue Chang and Ellie Ismailidou, \uc2 MarketWatch\par \par \uc2 Gap, \uc2 L Brands\uc2 , \uc2 Under Armour\uc2  sell off; energy companies surge\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks closed moderately higher Friday but still booked their worst week since early February as the market failed to lock in bigger early gains sparked by a crude-oil rally.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 climbed 5.69 points, or 0.3%, to close at 2,047.60, led by a 2% jump in the energy sector that was underpinned by a 6.6% surge in crude prices . The large-cap index fell 1.2% for the week, the worst it's fared since the week ending Feb. 5.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gave up the bulk of a 150-point advance to end the day with a gain of 35 points, or 0.2%, closing at 17,575.96. Its weekly drop of 1.2% was its biggest one-week decline since the week that ended Feb. 12.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite wove in and out of negative territory for most the session and closed higher by 2.32 points at 4,850.69. The tech-heavy index fell 1.3% on the week, the most ground it has lost since it fell 5.4% during the week ended Feb. 5.\par \par \uc2 "Worries about slower growth have increased, and rebounding oil prices may reduce the extra money in consumers' pockets, removing one more support for consumer spending," said Kate Warne, investment strategist at Edward Jones.\par \par \uc2 Then consumer-discretionary sector and health care were the two S&P 500 sectors to finish in negative territory, as retail and apparel companies highlighted by \uc2 Gap Inc\uc2 . tumbled.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about high valuations amid a profit-margin squeeze were the main culprit behind Gap's slide, after the retailer late Thursday said same-store sales fell in March ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/gap-reports-drop-in-march-sales-high-inventory-2016-04-07"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/gap-reports-drop-in-march-sales-high-inventory-2016-04-07}}}\uc2 ). Victoria's Secret owner \uc2 L Brands\uc2  (LB) and \uc2 Coach Inc\uc2 . (COH) were also among the biggest decliners.\par \par \uc2 Christian Magoon, CEO of Amplify Investments, said consumer-discretionary stocks are under pressure as uncertainty rises ahead of quarterly results, and "the most expensive stocks are naturally the first to get hit because they have room to fall."\par \par \uc2 Wholesale inventory data ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/wholesale-inventories-fall-for-fifth-straight-month-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/wholesale-inventories-fall-for-fifth-straight-month-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ) released Friday also highlighted economic uncertainty. U.S. inventories declined 0.5% in February, the fifth drop in five months, as companies have cut back on production, the Commerce Department said Friday.\par \par \uc2 A softening Japanese yen helped to calm some jitters after Thursday's selloff. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped to its highest level against the dollar in almost 18-months ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-slides-to-fresh-18-month-low-vs-yen-as-market-bets-on-no-boj-intervention-2016-04-07"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-slides-to-fresh-18-month-low-vs-yen-as-market-bets-on-no-boj-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 -2016-04-07}}}\uc2 ) on Thursday. The yen, typically viewed as a safe-harbor asset, gained as fears that global central banks won't be able to fix the sluggish global economy sapped investor sentiment and spurred a flight to perceived safety. But the yen eased back on Friday ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-recoups-some-losses-against-yen-after-jawboning-by-japan-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/dollar-recoups-some-losses-against-yen-after-jawboning-by-japan-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ) after Japan's finance minister, Taro Aso, said he may act against what he termed "one-sided" yen rises.\par \par \uc2 "Traders still remain on high alert amid fears that even though Japanese government officials appear to be ruling out immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt yen aggressive appreciation, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take action through monetary policy at its meeting on April 28," said Nawaz Ali, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Western Union\uc2  in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Next week the markets will be buffeted by the upcoming [\uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2 ] meeting on April 17 and earnings," said Kent Engelke, chief economic strategist at \uc2 Capitol Securities Management Inc\uc2 . Whether volatility remains at elevated levels depends on how investors interpret the outcome of the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting and corporate results, he added.\par \par \uc2 Alcoa Inc\uc2 . (AA) is slated to announce its first-quarter financial performance on Monday, marking the unofficial start to the earnings season.\par \par \uc2 More Fed talk: Federal Reserve Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said the U.S. economy was "on a solid course [and was] not a bubble economy" at a gathering with former Fed leaders after U.S. markets had closed on Thursday ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/yellen-backs-gradual-rate-hikes-at-panel-of-fed-chiefs-2016-04-07"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/yellen-backs-gradual-rate-hikes-at-panel-of-fed-chiefs-2016-04-07}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 And New York Fed President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must maintain a cautious and gradual approach to raising interest rates ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-04-08/dudley-calls-for-cautious-and-gradual-fed-tightening-approach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-04-08/dudley-calls-for-cautious-and-gradual-fed-tightening-approach}}}\uc2 ), echoing views about the need for a go-slow approach to rate increases that Yellen expressed last week.\par \par \uc2 Movers and shakers:Oil's Friday rebound ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/oil-rebounds-but-doha-meeting-remains-a-cloud-on-the-horizon-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/oil-rebounds-but-doha-meeting-remains-a-cloud-on-the-horizon-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ) boosted the stocks of energy companies like \uc2 Chevron Corp\uc2 .(CVX) , which was up 1.6%; \uc2 Baker Hughes Inc.(BHI) \uc2 , up 2.8%; and \uc2 Marathon Oil Corp\uc2 .(MRO) , 5% higher.\par \par \uc2 Under Armour Inc\uc2 .(\uc2 UA\uc2 ) underwent an effective 2-for-1 split ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/under-armour-stock-price-splits-after-class-c-stock-issued-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/under-armour-stock-price-splits-after-class-c-stock-issued-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ) Friday, with the athletic-apparel company issuing new Class C shares to existing shareholders.\par \par \uc2 DepoMed Inc\uc2 .(DEPO) surged 13% after \uc2 Starboard Value\uc2  disclosed that the company and its affiliates own 9.8% of \uc2 Depomed\uc2 's shares.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Ruby Tuesday Inc\uc2 .(RT) sank 12% after the restaurant operator said its losses deepened in the latest quarter ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/ruby-tuesday-cuts-forecast-as-losses-widen-2016-04-07-174851854"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/ruby-tuesday-cuts-forecast-as-losses-widen-2016-04-07-174851854}}}\uc2 ), leading to a sharp cut in its annual profit forecast.\par \par \uc2 Other markets: European stock markets rose almost across the board ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/european-stocks-rise-on-rebounding-oil-upbeat-german-trade-data-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/european-stocks-rise-on-rebounding-oil-upbeat-german-trade-data-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ), getting a boost from the rally in oil prices and stronger-than-expected German trade data.\par \par \uc2 Stocks in Asia ended mixed ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/asian-shares-drop-as-investors-pare-risk-in-wake-of-yens-run-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/asian-shares-drop-as-investors-pare-risk-in-wake-of-yens-run-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ), as investors digested the latest move in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Sara Sjolin contributed to this report.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2016 17:12 ET (21:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160408ec48003m6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
