\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Snaps 5-day Slide Vs. Yen, Logs Biggest Weekly Loss Since February\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Joseph Adinolfi, \uc2 MarketWatch\uc2  , Hiroyuki Kachi\par \par \uc2 Japan's Aso says the trade has become 'one-sided'\par \par \uc2 After falling to a nearly 18-month low against the yen earlier in the week, the dollar's Friday gain snapped a five-day losing streak.\par \par \uc2 The buck still managed to log its largest weekly drop against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the week ended Feb. 12, falling 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advanced to as high as Yen108.99 to the dollar early in morning trade before falling back to Yen108.27 late Friday in New York, compared with Yen108.24 late Thursday in New York.\par \par \uc2 That was a recovery from a fresh low set overnight of Yen107.67.\par \par \uc2 Comments from Japanese officials suggesting that the government could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling yen on the open market were widely credited for the greenback's strength.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso became the latest Japanese official to say the yen's moves had become "one sided," implying that the government would take action against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Aso's comments are significant because \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is responsible for determining policy related to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Similar remarks were made by Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshihide Suga, who had expressed concerns about the yen's resilience earlier in the week as well. A too-strong yen can hurt Japan's export market.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on the comments, ignoring a stronger-than-expected reading on Japan's current account balance.\par \par \uc2 Many strategists said they still believe a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely. They said officials were just trying to talk the yen lower.\par \par \uc2 "It's the first time in a while they've come up with this insinuation that they might do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so it's having a decent effect," said Steve Englander, global head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "But the more they do the verbal thing without following up the less effect it's going to have."\par \par \uc2 Read:\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be gentle with yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/japan-cant-be-gentle-with-yen-intervention-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/\b  \highlight22japan\b0 \highlight22 -cant-be-gentle-with-yen-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 -2016-04-08}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The comments were likely meant to stop speculators from piling into long-yen trades by reminding them that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains on the table, Englander said.\par \par \uc2 "Aso's comments were meant to put some fear in the market; I don't think anyone would view them as being committal," Englander said.\par \par \uc2 Read:The 'Incredible Hulk' chart pattern that's smashing the dollar ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/this-incredible-hulk-chart-pattern-thats-smashing-the-dollar-2016-04-07"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/this-incredible-hulk-chart-pattern-thats-smashing-the-dollar-2016-04-07}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The dollar has crumbled 10.2% against the yen since the beginning of the year as fears about global growth drove investors into haven assets like the yen, gold and U.S. sovereign debt. The fact that the yen has remained buoyant even as risk assets like stocks have climbed off their yearly lows suggests investors remain wary of another selloff.\par \par \uc2 In other trading, the euro edged higher to $1.1401 late Friday in New York, finishing the week little-changed from where it started. By comparison, it traded at $1.1376 late Thursday. Meanwhile, the British pound strengthened to $1.4113, compared with $1.4058 Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A surge in oil prices and a spate of strong economic data helped push the Canadian dollar ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/canadian-dollar-retakes-key-level-as-oil-prices-soar-2016-04-08"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/canadian-dollar-retakes-key-level-as-oil-prices-soar-2016-04-08}}}\uc2 ) past a key technical level of C$1.30 to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2016 15:50 ET (19:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160408ec480039c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
