\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen stalls as finance minister warns on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar gets respite vs yen after drop to 17-month low\par \par \uc2 * Japan's finance minister warns against rapid yen rise\par \par \uc2 * Short covering seen supporting dollar/yen for now (Updates prices, adds Aussie and Canadian dollar)\par \par \uc2 By Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 8 (Reuters) - Gains for stock markets and a warning of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister knocked back the yen on Friday after a week of startling gains.\par \par \uc2 The yen surged at one point by as much as 2 percent against the dollar on Thursday, and Minister Taro Aso responded early on Friday by warning rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were "undesirable," that the yen's were "one-sided" and that Japan would take steps as needed.\par \par \uc2 That is language that Tokyo has used in the past to flag \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the yen's run to 17-month highs against the dollar has required investors to believe it would hold fire at least until after next week's G20 meetings in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "Of course there is some fear in the market," said Manuel Oliveri, a strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "But one has to bear in mind that there is the G20 agreement not to, and that to work any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to be large and really that means it would need the support of the Fed and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 By 0738 GMT, the yen had lost 0.5 percent at 108.75 yen per dollar, still up more than 2 percent on the week and around 10 percent on the year so far.\par \par \uc2 The improved tone on European stock markets - still on course for their fourth straight week of losses - helped the Australian and Canadian dollars rise half a percent each.\par \par \uc2 But overall their U.S. counterpart was marginally higher against the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that measures its broad strength, taking some comfort from Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's promise on Thursday that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on course to tighten rates gradually.\par \par \uc2 Yellen's statement last week that the Fed should proceed cautiously in light of looming global risks to the U.S. economy have been at the heart of sharp falls over the past 10 days for the dollar against the euro and yen.\par \par \uc2 Both of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been treated as safe havens by investors sharing growing concerns that much of the developed world is falling into a debilitating cycle of deflation that central banks are powerless to stop.\par \par \uc2 "This is really just a bit of Friday respite," said a dealer with one international bank in London. "Where we go next week seems set to depend on risk appetite again. As long as stocks are falling, the dollar will be a sell on any rallies."\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of strategists released on Thursday showed the broader dollar rally that began in mid-2014 has nearly run its course and will only gain slightly over the coming year, with respondents saying risks to their forecasts are tilted more to the downside.\par \par \uc2 The dollar inched up 0.15 percent to $1.1360 per euro in morning trade in Europe.\par \par \uc2 "We think a combination of falling US real rates and elevated market volatility are weighing on the dollar against the current account surplus-backed euro and yen," analysts from \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "(But) we remain constructive on the dollar against the Australian and Canadian dollars." (Editing by Richard Balmforth) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-8T13:03:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160408ec4800txy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Mostly Lower After Wall Street Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - Asian stocks closed mostly lower on Friday after oil prices fell over 1 percent overnight and shares in leading European and U.S. banks fell sharply on earnings worries. Japanese markets stood out in a subdued trading session as the yen weakened slightly against the dollar in the wake of comments from Japan's Finance Minister and Fed Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso warned against rapid yen rise and vowed to take action if necessary, Yellen touted the strength of the world's largest economy while speaking in New York alongside three of her predecessors - \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 , Paul Volcker, and Alan Greenspan.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices rebounded in Asian deals from the overnight selloff on bets that a meeting between \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and non-OPEC countries in Qatar on April 17 will deliver an agreement to hold output at January highs.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite index slipped 23.46 points or 0.78 percent to 2,984.96 as investors awaited a slew of March economic indicators including exports, new lending and inflation due over the next week. Hong Kong's Hang Seng index rose 104.35 points or 0.51 percent to finish at 20,370.40.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves grew in March for the first time in five months, indicating that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had eased its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei index erased early losses to end 71.68 points or 0.46 percent higher at 15,821.52 after the government said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen falls further. The broader Topix index gained 1.18 percent to finish at 1,287.69 as the yen's recent strength spurred hopes of new stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The yen retreated a little bit but held near an 18-month high versus the dollar after the release of positive economic data. While Japan's current account surplus hit an 11-month high in February, a gauge of consumer confidence strengthened more than expected in March after easing for two consecutive months.\par \par \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  rose over 1 percent on a Bloomberg report it has recalled about 143,000 vehicles in Japan after an airbag failed to deploy in a car collision. Energy explorer \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2  rose 1.4 percent and \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  rallied 2.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Retailer \uc2 Seven & I Holdings\uc2  jumped 3.7 percent after chairman and CEO \uc2 Toshifumi Suzuki\uc2  announced his resignation. Apparel giant \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  plunged 12.7 percent, the most in five years, after lowering its profit outlook, citing significant declines in first-half operating income from the Uniqlo clothing brand.\par \par \uc2 Australian shares fell modestly, dragged down by banks. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 dropped 26.50 points or 0.53 percent to 4,937.60, taking the weekly loss to 1.2 percent. The broader All Ordinaries slipped 0.48 percent to 5,018 and lost 1.1 percent for the week. The big four banks fell between 0.6 percent and 1.5 percent on bad debt worries.\par \par \uc2 Miners turned in a mixed performance, with \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  losing 1.2 percent while \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  advanced 0.8 percent and smaller rival \uc2 Fortescue Metals Group\uc2  climbed 3.9 percent. Gold miner Newcrest rallied 2.6 percent and rival \uc2 Evolution Mining\uc2  soared over 6 percent after gold prices rose almost two percent overnight.\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks closed narrowly mixed as oil prices bounced back from overnight losses in Asian deals. Building materials group \uc2 CSR\uc2  shed 0.9 percent after its CFO \uc2 Greg Barnes\uc2  quit the company to take on a similar role at another Australian listed company.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi average slipped 1.84 points or 0.09 percent to 1,972.05 amid institutional selling. Foreign investors turned net buyers in late trade, helping limit the downside. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  fell 1.8 percent despite forecasting a better than expected profit for its first quarter. Rival \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  advanced 3.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's NZX-50 index dropped 24.95 points or 0.37 percent to 6,730.29 as some investors sought to lock in profits from recent gains that lifted the benchmark index to a fresh record high the previous day. While \uc2 Sky Network Television\uc2  and SkyCity Entertainment fell 2-3 percent, Kathmandu Holdings and Orion Health Group rose about 3 percent each.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, benchmark indexes in India, Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore were down between 0.2 percent and 0.6 percent while the Taiwan Weighted gained 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the major averages fell between 1 percent and 1.5 percent overnight, led by losses in banks, technology and commodity-related companies as oil prices inched lower after a 5 percent jump the previous day and global growth worries resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160408ec48002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
