\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-DoubleLine's Gundlach says negative interest rates backfiring\b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2016\par 21:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds further comment from Gundlach, details on DoubleLine, paragraphs 2-7)\par \par \uc2 By Jennifer Ablan\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 7 (Reuters) - \uc2 Jeffrey Gundlach\uc2 , the widely followed investor who runs \uc2 DoubleLine Capital\uc2 , said on Thursday that negative interest rates implemented by some major central banks, notably in Japan, are backfiring.\par \par \uc2 "The negative interest rate experiment seems to be backfiring," said Gundlach, who helps oversee $95 billion for Los Angeles-based DoubleLine. "The best evidence of negative interest rates backfiring is the yen versus the dollar and the Nikkei."\par \par \uc2 The dollar slumped against the yen again on Thursday in the wake of minutes from the last \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting and expectations the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while global growth concerns weighed on equities.\par \par \uc2 In January, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the central banks of Sweden, Denmark and Switzerland in negative territory, in effort to boost their economies partly by way of weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last down 1.4 percent at 108.01, its biggest daily percentage drop in two months. The decline put the greenback's losses at about 10 percent for the year.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach said: "Negative interest rates are not just deflationary, they are deflation. You lose money."\par \par \uc2 In both 2015 and 2014 Gundlach made correct predictions on market events. In 2015, he forecast that oil prices would plunge, junk bonds would live up to their name and China's slowing economy would pressure emerging markets. In 2014, he forecast that \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields would fall, rather than rise as many others had expected. (Reporting By Jennifer Ablan; Editing by David Gregorio) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-7T20:53:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160420ec470148u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
