\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Strength Reflects Loss of Faith in BoJ -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2016\par 19:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:59 ET - The yen's recent strength has been driven by a growing view the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  won't be able to accomplish its goals, says Nathan Thooft at John Hancock Asset Management. The dollar has retreated more than 10% against the yen so far this year and he says the move might not be done. Thooft has trimmed his exposure to Japanese stocks, on a hedged basis, to neutral. "The whole thesis on Japanese equities was that if they could win the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war and get their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak enough, it would support exports--which would therefore support earnings growth." (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 13:58 ET - Front-month Brent crude futures for June delivery briefly traded above July futures, a market structure called "backwardation" that is seen by some as a sign of tightness in the market. If supplies are limited, traders will pay a premium to buy crude today, whereas if the market is oversupplied, later-dated contracts tend to be more valuable, a structure called "contango." When Brent switched from backwardation to contango in mid-2014, it was seen by some traders as a key sign that prices would fall further. Supply outages in Nigeria and Iraq have limited global supplies in recent months, but analysts say the market is still oversupplied. The US crude benchmark remains in contango, with May futures $1.20/barrel cheaper than June. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 13:41 ET - Claire's, the Apollo (APO)-backed tween retailer, faces "an acute likelihood of a debt restructuring ahead of a June 2017 maturity" of about $260M in subordinated notes, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. Claire's has $2.37B in debt, the legacy of APO's $3.1B LBO in 2007. The retailer hasn't had a profitable year since 2013 and had some $29M in cash as of Jan. 31, according to filings. The past year's bankruptcies of mall retailers Quicksilver and \uc2 Pacific Sunwear\uc2  (PSUN) highlight the challenging environment for the sector. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  today pushed Claire's deeper into highly speculative territory. (matthew.jarzemsky@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:36 ET - Williams (WMB) CEO Alan Armstrong shied away from answering questions about lawsuits his company filed against Dallas-based \uc2 Energy Transfer Equity\uc2  (ETE) while speaking at WSJ's ECO:nomics conference. "I would just say that it's been a long, perilous journey," he says of the troubled proposed merger of big pipeline companies. "We're certainly out to defend our shareholder rights." (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\par \par \uc2 13:35 ET - Online financial adviser SigFig Wealth Management says it has partnered with \uc2 Cambridge Savings Bank\uc2  in Massachusetts to integrate the start-up's "robo-advisory" services directly into the bank's website. While several community banks have entered into partnerships with online consumer and small-business lenders to give their customers access to more kinds of credit, robo-advisory partnerships have been less common. SigFig's larger rivals Betterment and Wealthfront have grown in prominence and in assets under management largely by marketing directly to consumers. But as large asset managers such as \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2  and \uc2 Vanguard Group\uc2  expand their digital wealth products to existing customers, questions have emerged around whether robo-advisory services can remain a stand-alone product. (peter.rudegeair@wsj.com; @rudegeair)\par \par \uc2 13:30 ET - In a further sign that New York-based hedge fund \uc2 GoldenTree Asset Management\uc2  wants to sell its big stake in Postmedia (PNC.B.T), the Canadian newspaper publisher announces GoldenTree partner Ted Lodge has resigned from the company's board to "focus on GoldenTree's investment" in PNC.B.T. WSJ first reported last month on GoldenTree's efforts to try to sell its stake. But PNC.B.T's widening losses underscore the challenges GoldenTree faces in trying to find a buyer for its stake in the debt-laden and money-losing company. (ben.dummett@wsj.com; @bendummett)\par \par \uc2 13:26 ET - "It's hard for me to argue against global warming," said \uc2 Scott Sheffield\uc2 , CEO of Pioneer Natural Resources. "I don't think we'll stop the climate change movement." Sheffield told an audience at The Wall Street Journal's ECOnomics conference that the political winds have changed, with countries around the world coming out against fossil fuels, closing down coal fired power plants. Even Saudi Arabia recognizes the traction the environmental movement has gained, he said, pointing to The Kingdom's recent moves to invest in renewables and desire to transition its economy from being so dependent on oil. (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\par \par \uc2 13:24 ET - Hope is running out for clean-technology startups which are still around, according to a longtime investor in the sector. Rob Day, partner at Black Coral Capital, writes in \uc2 Greentech Media\uc2  that his pessimism stems from the type of companies which could acquire startups in the energy- and water-technology space aren't tech giants or big banks but instead the likes of "chemical producers, oil companies, industrial-equipment OEMs, utilities." And they tend to move slowly, hunt for bargains and consider only profitable targets. That means most startups will simply run out of venture capital before a deal. Day was a director at \uc2 Next Step Living\uc2 , a fairly large energy-efficiency company that shut down recently. (yuliya.chernova@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:58 ET - Telecom analysts react generally favorably to \uc2 Sprint\uc2 's (S) plan to raise $2.2B cash and use some network equipment as collateral. The company has been open about working on the deal for some time, so it wasn't a surprise. Still, Raymond James notes, "This transaction improves \uc2 Sprint\uc2 's liquidity as it continues to find ways to finance its network densification plan and meet upcoming debt maturities." Nomura calls the transaction was "a good start" for S' funding efforts. The carrier has $2.3B of debt coming due in December and hasn't had an annual profit since 2006. Amid today's market pullback, S is down 3.5%. (ryan.knutson@wsj.com; @ryan_knutson)\par \par \uc2 12:55 ET - The bar is high for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 --especially unilaterally--in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so analysts are starting to wonder whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may announce fresh stimulus later this month in an effort to keep a lid on the yen. "The BoJ may well find itself between a rock and a hard place should JPY strength persist," says Viraj Patel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING. "With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely this side of Y105, the BoJ's biggest challenge will be to deliver a stimulus package that does more good than harm." He adds: "Getting it wrong could prove costly in terms of fuelling JPY upside." The dollar was recently down 1.5% at Y108.13. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:52 ET - Consensus 2016 EPS estimates for Meritage (MTH) fell some 15% in 2H15 amid concerns about the homebuilder's exposure to the Houston area amid oil's price slump. But \uc2 Barclays\uc2  thinks Street views "are more than sufficiently conservative to allow the stock to outperform over the next year despite a modest headwind from Houston." That metro area has made up some 12% of MTH's closings. "Our recent visit to Houston suggests that the market overall is not declining as rapidly as feared and that MTH's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  there are outperforming the competition." So \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ticks up EPS views through 2017 and boosts multiple targets, pushing the bank's stock target up $6 to $42 and prompting an upgrade to overweight. MTH rises 3% to $37.98, putting the year's gain at 12%. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:48 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of many emerging countries tumble on a classic risk-off day, with gold and the Japanese yen rallying strongly as investors pile into safe-haven assets. The Brazilian real weakens 1.7% against the dollar to 3.7031, while the Mexican peso and the Malaysian ringgit weakened 1.6% and 1.4%, respectively. "In recent days, what's been quite puzzling is that (while) the dollar-yen has been moving lower, the risk appetite more broadly has held up well," \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  says. "Today, it seems other assets are following dollar-yen lower." The Japanese yen recently strengthened 1.6% against the dollar at 108.073. Thursday's big losers in emerging markets are those "high-yielding" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which are more sensitive to macro moves. But \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  says it's "too early to call it a start of a period of renewed risk-aversion," as many supportive factors remain intact, such as a dovish Fed, a relatively calming Chinese economy and stronger commodity prices. (carolyn.cui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 07, 2016 13:59 ET (17:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160407ec47002w6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
