\par\par \b \uc2 Could Soaring Yen Push BoJ to Add Stimulus? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2016\par 18:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:55 ET - The bar is high for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 --especially unilaterally--in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, so analysts are starting to wonder whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may announce fresh stimulus later this month in an effort to keep a lid on the yen. "The BoJ may well find itself between a rock and a hard place should JPY strength persist," says Viraj Patel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING. "With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely this side of Y105, the BoJ's biggest challenge will be to deliver a stimulus package that does more good than harm." He adds: "Getting it wrong could prove costly in terms of fuelling JPY upside." The dollar was recently down 1.5% at Y108.13. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:52 ET - Consensus 2016 EPS estimates for Meritage (MTH) fell some 15% in 2H15 amid concerns about the homebuilder's exposure to the Houston area amid oil's price slump. But \uc2 Barclays\uc2  thinks Street views "are more than sufficiently conservative to allow the stock to outperform over the next year despite a modest headwind from Houston." That metro area has made up some 12% of MTH's closings. "Our recent visit to Houston suggests that the market overall is not declining as rapidly as feared and that MTH's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  there are outperforming the competition." So \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ticks up EPS views through 2017 and boosts multiple targets, pushing the bank's stock target up $6 to $42 and prompting an upgrade to overweight. MTH rises 3% to $37.98, putting the year's gain at 12%. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:48 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of many emerging countries tumble on a classic risk-off day, with gold and the Japanese yen rallying strongly as investors pile into safe-haven assets. The Brazilian real weakens 1.7% against the dollar to 3.7031, while the Mexican peso and the Malaysian ringgit weakened 1.6% and 1.4%, respectively. "In recent days, what's been quite puzzling is that (while) the dollar-yen has been moving lower, the risk appetite more broadly has held up well," \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  says. "Today, it seems other assets are following dollar-yen lower." The Japanese yen recently strengthened 1.6% against the dollar at 108.073. Thursday's big losers in emerging markets are those "high-yielding" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which are more sensitive to macro moves. But \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  says it's "too early to call it a start of a period of renewed risk-aversion," as many supportive factors remain intact, such as a dovish Fed, a relatively calming Chinese economy and stronger commodity prices. (carolyn.cui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:43 ET - The best energy investment that Douglas Kimmelman, Senior Partner at \uc2 Energy Capital Partners\uc2  ever made was a coal-fired power plant in the US northeast, and fossil fuels aren't going away any time soon he told an audience at The Wall Street Journal's ECOnomics conference in Santa Barbara. "Global demand for coal is on the rise, not decline," he said. Despite bankruptcies in the sector, including \uc2 Peabody Energy\uc2 , coal provides stable power generation to the world at low costs, which means it can't be totally phased out entirely and there's money to be made in cleaning it up, Mr. Kimmelman said. And as the Obama Administration's new regulations shutter many coal plants, that makes the power generating units left running even more valuable, whether they burn coal or natural gas, he added. (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\par \par \uc2 12:30 ET - Car-loan delinquencies continued to increase in contrast to most other consumer loan categories in 4Q15, according to data from the \uc2 American Bankers Association\uc2 . There was a slight year-over-year uptick in the share of car loans that borrowers were 30 days or more late with payments on, to 1.54% for car loans received at dealerships and 0.75% for those they received directly from lenders. Delinquencies for both types of car loans remain significantly below their peaks of around 3% to 3.5% in 2008 and 2009. Missed payments on loans borrowers received at dealerships, which account for the bulk of auto lending, exceed the national average delinquency rate for all consumer loans, which stood at 1.41% in 4Q15. (annamaria.andriotis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:25 ET - Here's a catch from the soaring yen--it could dim Japanese investors' appetite to load up on Treasurys. A stronger dollar--or weaker yen--the past year and a half has been part of the reasons stoking demand for US debt because income from the US was inflated when converting back to yen. But this year the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been soaring and recent data showed investors there sold $14.2B in overseas notes and bonds the week ended April 1 after buying a net $10.4B the week before, says CRT Capital. Still, Japanese investors boosted demand for foreign bonds in February and March as a whole, so it may be premature to jump to conclusions. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:56 ET - No tools exist to accurately measure overall foreign investment in Canadian real estate, the country's federal housing agency says. \uc2 Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation\uc2  made the comment in a report on condominium ownership that found foreign buyers in Toronto and Vancouver were more likely to own condos built after 2010 than condos built earlier. The study comes several weeks after Ottawa earmarked C$500,000 for Canada's statistics agency to study the issue more closely and confirms what many observers already knew: "At this time, no existing tool can provide a definitive measure of the level of foreign investment in Canada's housing markets." (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 11:54 ET - The risk-off trade that's sending investors into the yen is also pushing them out of the Australian dollar, which is down 1.4% against the greenback today. Part of that selloff can be chalked up to \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which said Tuesday that a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be an impediment to the economy. "We heard more of a dovish tone from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 ," says David Song, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at DailyFX. But the outsize moves today are picking up as investors sell risk assets and buy safe ones. That's pushed the yen up 1.6% against the US dollar as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise builds momentum. "Some of these moves may continue over the near-term," says Song. Elsewhere, fellow safe haven gold is up 1.5% while the S&P 500 drops 0.9% and high-grade copper futures are down 2.9%. (ben.eisen@wsj.com; @beneisen)\par \par \uc2 11:50 ET - Tesla (TSLA) says its reservations for the Model 3, due out late next year, have totaled 325K for the 1st week. The refundable $1K reservation represents about $14B in potential revenue and a truly huge boost to the electric-vehicle park. The reservation list grew by 49K since late Saturday, when CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  gave an update. TSLA is now rushing to increase its production plans to meet the demand for the 5-seat electric car. Shares are down 2% today at $260.15 amid the broader market's pullback, trimming TSLA's gain for the week to 9.5%. (michael.ramsey@wsj.com; @mramseywsj)\par \par \uc2 11:46 ET - Department of Justice's lawsuit against activist investor ValueAct this week about disclosures of trading in \uc2 Baker Hughes (BHI) \uc2 and \uc2 Halliburton\uc2  (HAL), could chill talks activists have with other investors. Antitrust rules demand investors who buy more than $76.3M in stock seek approval, unless they are under 10% and for "investment purposes only." The DOJ suit hits ValueAct for seeking discussions with management about compensation and sharing its views on best practices. That, says law firm David Polk, sounds like conversations non-activists often have. But those funds don't normally seeking antitrust clearance. This suit could lead them to realize even talking with activists about such changes could require them to seek approval before buying more stock, in turn leading them to hold off on talking. (david.benoit@wsj.com; @DaveCBenoit)\par \par \uc2 11:37 ET - Some banks' optimistic price targets on the yen turn out to not be bullish enough as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallies to its highest point since 2014 this week. In February, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  estimated the US dollar would fall to Y110 within three months, a forecast that surprised many at the time. The dollar broke through that level Wednesday and is now trading at Y107.86. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  had expected the dollar to fall to Y108 by mid-year. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  strategist Alvise Marino say the dollar has further to fall against the yen as investors seek out safe-haven assets and the Fed proceeds cautiously to raising rates this year. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:36 ET - The Supreme Court blocking Obama's signature climate-change regulation while litigation plays out is sending a chilling message to the international community, UN Secretary General Ban Ki-moon contended late Wednesday at WSJ's ECO:nomics conference. "It is true it has given certain negative" signals to other nations, he said. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  rule cutting power-plant carbon emissions makes up the bulk of Obama's pledge to the \uc2 UN\uc2  climate deal and has enabled the president to convince many other nations, including China, to put on the table similar commitments to cut greenhouse-gas emissions. (amy.harder@wsj.com; @AmyAHarder)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 07, 2016 12:55 ET (16:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160407ec47002r5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
