\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls on bets BoJ will stand pat, dovish Fed stance\b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2016\par 22:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Fed March meeting minutes seen as dovish\par \par \uc2 * Dollar hits 17-month low of 109.36 yen\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  PM says countries should avoid "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 "\par \par \uc2 * Oil inventory data boosts commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Sam Forgione\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 6 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar hit a 17-month trough against the yen on Wednesday and remained broadly weaker on bets that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rally and on \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  minutes that confirmed a dovish tone at the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell below 110 yen for a second straight day and hit 109.36, its lowest since late October 2014. Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  told the Wall Street Journal that countries should avoid seeking to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The remarks fueled expectations that the BoJ likely would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rally. In addition, minutes of the Fed's March policy meeting confirmed expectations for a dovish tone by showing a consensus emerged that risks from a global economic slowdown warranted a cautious approach. Both developments hurt the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar last traded down 0.61 percent against the yen at 109.69 yen. The dollar has fallen more than 8 percent against the yen so far this year, partly on a more dovish Fed.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's strength is on concerns that the BoJ is not going to be doing anything," said Jason Leinwand, managing director at derivatives advisory firm Riverside Risk Advisors in New York. At the same time, he said, "you got a really dovish tone out of the minutes."\par \par \uc2 Analysts also said riskier and commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained against the dollar after data showing U.S. crude oil inventories unexpectedly fell from record highs last week, which led to more than 5 percent gains for U.S. crude oil prices .\par \par \uc2 "That gave risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a bit of a boost, and that is one of the factors weighing on the dollar," said Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac Banking Corporation\uc2  in New York, in reference to the inventories data.\par \par \uc2 The commodity-linked Australian dollar was last up 0.73 percent at $0.76.\par \par \uc2 The euro was last up 0.16 percent at $1.1400 after hitting a session high of $1.1432, near Friday's 5-1/2-month high of $1.1437. The dollar hit a roughly 5-1/2-month low against the Swiss franc of 0.9532 franc.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against a basket of six other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was last down 0.18 percent at 94.457. (Reporting by Sam Forgione; Additional reporting by Patrick Graham in London; Editing by Tom Brown and James Dalgleish) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-6T21:04:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160406ec4601ula\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
