\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar nurses deep losses vs yen after Japan PM's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  comments\b0\par\par\uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2016\par 05:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar/yen on defensive after hitting 17-mth low below 110\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  PM: countries should avoid "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 "-WSJ\par \par \uc2 * Euro steady, oil rebound supports Aussie, loonie (Updates prices, adds details and quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 6 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near a 17-month low against the yen on Wednesday after taking a fresh knock overnight on comments by Japan's prime minister which suggested that authorities were cautious towards arresting the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inched up 0.1 percent to 110.51 yen but remained in striking distance of 109.92, its lowest level since Oct. 31, 2014 hit late on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  told the Wall Street Journal that countries should avoid seeking to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with "arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Amid earlier turmoil in the global markets, the yen has advanced steadily this year due to its safe-haven status and more recently on expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would not hike interest rates as aggressively this year as initially anticipated.\par \par \uc2 But each significant advance had been accompanied by some wariness in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a stronger yen, which is an unwelcome factor for a government trying to shore up a moribund economy.\par \par \uc2 "Japan will host the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit in May. It cannot afford to invite almost guaranteed criticism by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through yen-selling after it adopted negative interest rates," said Junichi Ishikawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  adopted negative interest rates late in January, but the shock move did little to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities also have to keep U.S. political developments in mind, as presidential hopefuls Trump and Clinton have both been critical of Japan's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Ishikawa added.\par \par \uc2 Even without the Japanese prime minister's comments, the yen was on a strong footing thanks to wobbly global stock markets and lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, which lessens support for the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has lost more than one percent against the yen so far this week, and appears on track to post its third weekly loss in four.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite difficult for the Japanese government to resort to measures directly aiming at reversing yen strength, like \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or BOJ's additional easing," wrote Osamu Takashima, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citigroup Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "What the Japanese government can do at present is just to proceed with the organization of the fiscal stimulus package."\par \par \uc2 Japan's parliament last week approved a record state budget for fiscal 2016, paving the way for a debate on additional stimulus spending.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's sharp fall against the yen enabled the euro in turn to stay firm against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite data showing a drop in German factory orders and a subdued start to the euro zone's business activity in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nearly flat at $1.1375 after probing a low of $1.1335 overnight.\par \par \uc2 The euro managed to touch a 5-1/2-month high of $1.1438 last Friday after U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  indicated she was in no hurry to tighten monetary policy last week.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar staged a modest bounce on the back of a rebound in commodity prices, rising 0.3 percent to $0.7565 after losing 0.7 percent overnight. The Aussie was hurt earlier this week by a dent in global risk appetite and slide in commodities.\par \par \uc2 Other commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Canadian dollar also benefited as crude oil rebounded from one-month lows. The loonie was steady at C$1.3129 per dollar after pulling away from an eight-day low of C$1.3219 reached on Tuesday. (Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-6T04:17:38.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160406ec460080y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
