\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Economy in Worst Crisis Since Lehman -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2016\par 01:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 19:31 EDT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 [ Japan's economy is in its worst crisis since the Lehman crisis, says ruling party lawmaker and Abe ally Kozo Yamamoto. "The effect of the sales tax increase was extremely large," he says talking about a 2014 sales tax increase. "Consumption data is such that you could even say we need a tax cut." Mr. Yamamoto speaks at a study session of ruling party politicians that are meeting for the first time this year to discuss the sales tax planned for 2017 and Japan's fiscal situation. Mr. Yamamoto convened the same study session in 2014 that helped solidify party support to postpone a second sales tax increase until 2017. (Eleanor Warnock; @misssaxbys)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  has revised higher its forecasts for the Australian dollar in 2016. The changes largely stem from the bank's decision to remove two Fed hikes from its forecasts. The Australian dollar is now expected to end the year at US$6900, up from US$0.6500, it says. "The key risk to our revised forecasts lie more in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than rates space," it said. "The widening in rate differentials and the preponderance of global central banks to ease even further points to the risk of a further leg higher in Australian dollar near term exacerbated by a reluctant RBA," it says. "Further tightening in financial conditions will eventually transmit through to the real economy and necessitate a response from the RBA. We are, therefore, comfortable with a lower Australian dollar forecast by year end," RBC added. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 23:26 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Exchange operator ASX (ASX.AU) notched up strong future activity in the first three months of 2016 but that was offset by a slow start to the year for listings, \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes. Overall, it was a mixed fiscal third quarter for \uc2 ASX\uc2 , the result of which is negligible changes to group-wide forecasts. The brokerage says futures activity was the highlight for the quarter, with average contracts up 8.5% on-year. Cash market's were also stronger, although largely in lower margin products, while options activity continued to slide and the volume of IPOs and secondary capital raisings was subdued after a record first half of the fiscal year. \uc2 UBS\uc2  remains neutral on the stock, which was last at A$41.40. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--\uc2 Ted Schadler\uc2 , an analyst at Forrester, says there will be "blood in the water" after investors dramatically drove up the number of private technology companies valued at more than $1 billion in recent years. However, he says, a downturn in valuations wouldn't lead to an industry-wide meltdown comparable to the dot-com bust. He says consumer-service companies, such as shopping and car services, will likely be hit the hardest. Early entrants in those categories such as Gilt Groupe and Sidecar have already been acquired. (scott.martin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 23:23 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The ongoing volatility in financial markets is weighing on the investment earnings of Australia's insurers and putting pressure on dividends, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  argues. It notes global bond yields reversed the recovery from the previous quarter, while the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 Accumulation index was down 2.7% for the March quarter and the MSCI World Index down 5.8%. It reckons \uc2 Insurance Australia Group\uc2  (IAG.AU) is the most exposed to equity movements, triggering a 9.7% cut to its fiscal 2016 earnings forecast. Its forecasts for Suncorp (SUN.AU) fall 4% and for \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) by 12%. The expected equities market hit for Challenger (CGF.AU) prompts a 15% cut to its FY16 profit estimate. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 23:19 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY may test its downside again during Asia trade, positioning the pair to move in a 109.80-110.80 range, says Mizuho Securities chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kengo Suzuki. "We saw the pair managed to bounce back, but the pace is slow," suggesting that the pair (now at 110.29) may test 110-mark again later in the session, says Suzuki. Investors need to focus on potential verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, as well as on how riskier assets such as stocks and oil will perform today, says Suzuki. But the pair's sustained fall below 110 may intensify the downside bias again to bring down the pair toward 106 eventually, he says. The USD/JPY hit as low as 109.92, its lowest since October 2014 overnight. Japan Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  tells WSJ that "whatever the circumstances, we must definitely avoid competitive devaluation and I think we should refrain from arbitrary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.1320-1.1420 range and is now at 1.1381. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 19:15 EDT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Ted Schadler\uc2 , an analyst at Forester, said there will be "blood in the water" after investors dramatically drove up the number of private technology companies valued at more than $1 billion in recent years. However he said a downturn in valuations wouldn't lead to an industry-wide meltdown comparable to the dot-com bust. He says consumer service companies, like shopping and car services, will likely be hit the hardest. Early entrants in those spaces, like Gilt Groupe and Sidecar, have already been acquired. (Cat Zakrzewski)\par \par \uc2 23:13 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Australian dollar remains weaker in the aftermath of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting Tuesday which produced warnings about complications for the economy if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains elevated. ANZ said that weakness, which sees the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now trading closer to US$0.7500 than US$0.7600, is also due to news overnight of solid U.S. non-manufacturing data. "It is clear that the U.S. economy is doing enough for normalisation to strengthen the U.S. dollar, but markets are wary of acting on that fact," ANZ said. "Continued risk-off trading would suggest a further fall in the Australian dollar, however expect support around US$0.7500, it adds. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair currently trades at US$0.7545. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 32:02 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] After \uc2 Growthpoint Properties Australia\uc2  (GOZ.AU) bid for \uc2 GPT Metro Office Fund\uc2 , \uc2 UBS\uc2  considers the pros and cons of the proposed transaction. On the positive side, it provides GPT Metro with an exposure to a larger diversified office portfolio, earnings accretion for both parties, reduced fees for GPT Metro, improved liquidity for GPT Metro unitholders as Growthpoint is in the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200, and increased free float for Growthpoint investors with South African majority owner \uc2 Growthpoint Properties Ltd\uc2 .'s (GRT.JO) stake to be diluted. "This is balanced by increased gearing of the combined entity. For GMF the exposure to industrial assets and loss of \uc2 GPT\uc2 's management/platform expertise may be seen as a negative. In addition, GOZ have foreshadowed A$150 million of asset sales which would be 3% dilutive to GOZ earnings." (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Australian shares look set for another weak opening, tracking overnight losses in global markets as investors remain wary amid an uncertain economic outlook. IG expects the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 will open 0.4% lower, after falling 1.4% on Tuesday to a one-month low at 4924.4. "Investors are turning cautious after worse-than-expected economic data in the U.S. and Germany this week, and comments from \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  head \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  highlighting greater risks to the global economy," says Niv Dagan, executive director at Peak Asset Management, adding that marks a reversal from investor optimism last month. Still, energy stocks may see some relief from recent declines after U.S. and global crude markets managed to edge higher Tuesday, rallying in the late minutes of trading. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 18:48 ET [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Sen. Richard Blumenthal (D., Conn.) and a former Federal Trade Commission adviser suggested in a congressional hearing Tuesday that the FTC should reopen its antitrust investigation into Alphabet's \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOGL). The pair cited recent data that suggests GOOGL's local-search results are worse for consumers than competitors'. The former FTC adviser, Tim Wu, said in a study last year partly funded by Yelp (YELP) that research showed users clicked far less on GOOGL information about local business than on links from local-review sites like YELP. Wu said Tuesday that that research represents "stronger evidence of consumer harm now than there was when the FTC did its investigation," which ended in 2012. Sen. Blumenthal agreed with Wu and asked, "Shouldn't there be basis for" an antitrust investigation? (jack.nicas@wsj.com; @jacknicas)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 UBS\uc2  cuts its price target on Nine Entertainment (NEC.AU) to A$1.30 a share from A$1.50 after the free-to-air broadcaster reported an 11% revenue slide in its third quarter. However, it keeps a buy rating on the stock, citing valuation grounds, with the shares trading on a fiscal 2016 price-to-earnings ratio of 10 times. "Potential catalysts are affiliate fee renegotiations, license fee cuts to be potentially considered at the May 3 budget, and potential changes in media ownership laws," the broker says. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 05, 2016 19:31 ET (23:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160405ec45004k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
