\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares: Japan Shares Fall Sharply -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2016\par 15:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks in Japan dropped sharply Tuesday, as the yen surged to its strongest level against the U.S. dollar since October 2014 and bank stocks plunged amid worries about the fallout from negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average closed down 2.4% at 15732.82, marking its lowest finish since Feb. 12.\par \par \uc2 Bank-related stocks were among the worst performers: \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group Inc\uc2 . fell 4.8% to 153.2 yen a share, while \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Trust Holdings Inc\uc2 . lost 4.4% to 305.1 yen a share.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  started imposing a minus 0.1% rate on some deposits held by commercial banks in February, meaning that those banks now have to pay a small fee when they add to their money parked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The financial sector has suffered amid worries that banks can't pass on negative interest rate to their depositors and therefore will take a hit to their profits.\par \par \uc2 "There were a lot of large money managers who were very optimistic toward the BOJ" last year and in the first quarter, said Ilya Feygin, managing director at WallachBeth Capital LLC. But now, "the [Japanese] market is starting to sense that there are limits to both monetary and fiscal policy."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a rush to safe-haven assets pushed Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to as strong as 110.24 yen to one U.S. dollar in the Asia evening, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 That marks its strongest level since Oct. 31, 2014, when Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocked markets by expanding its massive stimulus program. On that day, the yen was as strong as 109.15 yen to one U.S. dollar before weakening, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 A stronger yen is generally seen as hurting Japan's big exporters and its current strength coincides with renewed weakness in the economy.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rise against the U.S. dollar has stirred expectation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. On Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshihide Suga warned against excessive movements in the yen, saying the government would continue monitoring market movements and take appropriate action if necessary.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't all bad news on Tuesday in the Asia region:\par \par \uc2 China has become unlikely bright spot among major global share markets, as investors grow less pessimistic about its economy. Recent data suggest improvement in China's manufacturing activity and property market.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index finished up 1.5% at 3053.07, its highest close since Jan. 8.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark has gained 6.2% in the past four weeks, compared to a 7.5% loss in the Nikkei during the same period.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, trading volumes in China shot up. In Shanghai and Shenzhen, they rose to 712.7 billion yuan, compared with 582.2 billion yuan in the previous session. Local sentiment also got a boost from speculation about a debt-for-equity swap plan, which Chinese media publication Caixin Weekly reported would convert up to one trillion yuan of bad loans into equities.\par \par \uc2 The gains, however, haven't dug the Shanghai Composite out of the hole for the year, leaving it overall down 13.7% as of Tuesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Most other Asian shares were in the red Tuesday, reflecting a cautious view by traders and fund managers. Many say that markets got overheated in March, and are struggling to find catalysts for shares to go higher. In the U.S., shares slipped overnight, amid an uncertain global economic outlook ahead of what could be a bleak corporate-earnings season.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 and Hong Kong's Hang Seng were off 1.4% and 1.6%, respectively, while South Korea's Kospi lost 0.8%. Hong Kong and China were reopening after a national holiday on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of stuff just got ahead of itself and benefited from turn-of-the month inflows that were mechanical and not really based on fundamentals and news," Mr. Feygin of WallachBeth said of the Asia region.\par \par \uc2 A drop in crude oil overnight and during the Asia trading day weighed on the region, with energy stocks in Australia and Hong Kong both down around 3%. Brent oil was last off 0.7% at $37.44 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 In India, the S&P \uc2 BSE\uc2  Sensex was last down 2%, after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  cut its main interest rate by 0.25 percentage points, as predicted by economists. The move signaled India's fight against inflation may be bearing the first substantial fruits.\par \par \uc2 Kosaku Narioka, Hiroyuki Kachi, Yifan Xie and Gabriele Parussini contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 05, 2016 09:09 ET (13:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160405ec45001or\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
