\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sell-off wanes, but on track for weekly losses on Fed outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 13:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Focus on how Japanese authorities respond to firmer yen\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index down for third straight week\par \par \uc2 * Some banks revising euro/dollar forecasts upwards (updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 18 (Reuters) - The dollar index recovered from a five-month low on Friday, but was still on track for a third straight week of losses, as investors cut favourable bets after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  appeared cautious about raising rates at a steady pace this year.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was flat against the yen, having hit a 17-month low on Thursday, with traders worried that a sharp rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would elicit some kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at 111.41 yen, off a low of 110.67 plumbed on Thursday that was its weakest since October, 2014. The euro retreated from a five-week high of $1.1342 to $1.1280, but was still on track to gain 1.2 percent on the week.\par \par \uc2 All this saw the dollar index trade 0.25 percent higher at 95.001, having fallen to a five-month trough of 94.578 in Asian trade. It was set to end the week 1.2 percent lower.\par \par \uc2 "At these levels, the market is nervous about pushing dollar/yen too far lower just in case there is some response from the BOJ," said Yujiro Goto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Nomura.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen sharply since the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  lowered its expectations for interest rate increases this year at the midweek Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) meeting. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields have slid since the policy meeting, fuelling the dollar's fall.\par \par \uc2 "Short-term speculators are leading the action. They appear to be selling the dollar and Japanese stocks in tandem, trying to probe for a bottom," said Kaneo Ogino, director at \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research firm Global-info Co in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "They are testing to see if the trough of the recent 110-115 yen range can hold," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's post-FOMC weakness against the yen presents a headache for Japan. The country would like to see the yen remain relatively weak to support its exporters, but the market thinks Japan has to tread carefully lest it is accused of engineering competitive devaluations.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness poses a challenge to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too. \uc2 The ECB\uc2  eased extensively last week, hoping to weaken the euro somewhat and boost inflation.\par \par \uc2 But some banks are already revising their euro/dollar forecasts higher. Dutch bank ABN-Amro on Friday said it expected the euro to trade around $1.15 in the foreseeable future with a further adjustment in Fed rate hike expectations likely to see the euro touch $1.20.\par \par \uc2 "The longer-term picture has changed for the dollar," said Georgette Boele, analyst at ABN Amro. "We think the multi-year bull trend of the dollar has come to an end. So if the range in euro/dollar is broken, it will likely be on the dollar weakness side, in our view." (additional reporting by Shinichi Saoshiro; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-3-18T13:04:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160318ec3i010yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
