\par\par \b \uc2 Global Pressure Curbs Japan's Options On Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 08:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B5\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 3/5/16) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- What do Donald Trump, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hillary Clinton\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the Dutch finance minister have in common? They all have expressed concern recently about the way Japan is handling the yen, much to the consternation of policy makers in Tokyo struggling to revive the country's economy with ever-looser monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 For the two front-runners in the U.S. presidential race, Japan's moves -- including setting negative interest rates on some bank reserves for the first time in late January -- amount to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. Mrs. Clinton last week referred to Japan as a long-term culprit.\par \par \uc2 European officials, meanwhile, used the recent gathering of \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  economic policy makers in Shanghai to warn that any direct Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets could spark a series of competitive devaluations around the world, according to people familiar with the matter. The officials' comments were made at a separate, undisclosed meeting of top finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials from the eurozone, U.S., China and Japan known as the Group of Four, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Jeroen Dijsselbloem, the Dutch finance minister, said the risk of Japan sparking a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war had been discussed at meetings on the sidelines during the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The international pressure on Japan comes as worries grow that major economic powers could seek to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate growth, roiling global markets. China, too, has been criticized for allegedly letting the yuan weaken this year as its economy stutters.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, sensitivity to accusations of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation has been evident in the confused official response to the Dutch finance minister's comments. Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  told Parliament on Friday that \uc2 European Commission\uc2  officials had informed their Japanese counterparts that Mr. Dijsselbloem had been misquoted in Shanghai. Tokyo also has denied that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a target of concern at the full G-20 meeting.\par \par \uc2 A European Council spokesman told The Wall Street Journal earlier this past week that Mr. Dijsselbloem's comments had been reported accurately. \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment later Friday on the G-4 discussions.\par \par \uc2 Still, the yen's value is proving a knotty problem for Japan. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a key element of Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  's economic program, commonly known as Abenomics, helping drive corporate profits to records in recent years as Japanese exporters benefited.\par \par \uc2 But the yen rose to its highest level against the U.S. dollar in more than a year amid the global market turmoil at the start of this year. At such times, investors often buy the yen, which is seen as a haven asset. A growing perception that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  's loose policies were losing their effectiveness also has contributed to the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 While Abenomics nominally includes structural overhauls and fiscal spending, its primary tool has been monetary policy. International opposition could now make it difficult for the BOJ to pursue policies that further weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even indirectly, including additional cuts to negative interest rates.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  "probably has no choice" but to refrain from directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the U.S. and Europe are opposed to it, and may find it difficult to push rates deeper into negative territory, said Koji Fukaya, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst and chief executive of FPG Securities in Tokyo. "Neither is president yet, but be it Clinton or Trump, [criticism of Japan] could escalate if Tokyo now tries to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sought to play down the international criticism. BOJ Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda a week earlier said Mrs. Clinton's and Mr. Trump's comments wouldn't limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy options.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and Europe generally don't object to indirect weakening of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through monetary policy, such as quantitative easing, which they have undertaken themselves. But they are opposed to direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Washington even frown on so-called oral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- when authorities talk about exchange rates in such a way as to spook markets into selling a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to weaken the yen since the winter of 2011, after multiple \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  then drew harsh criticism from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Still, speaking in Parliament last month, Mr. Abe described "sharp fluctuations" in the yen as undesirable, saying he wanted Mr. Aso to "take action as necessary."\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials also are concerned that unless Tokyo presses ahead with Mr. Abe's promised structural overhauls and looser fiscal policy, authorities there will rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action and cheapening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost growth.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ian Talley contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 05, 2016 02:38 ET (07:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160305ec35000gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
