\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Markets Briefing: U.S. Stocks Recover as Oil Prices Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 2,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2016\par 23:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS AT A GLANCE\par \par \uc2 (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      LAST     CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                 16485    53.21    0.32% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2                 4542.61  39.02    0.87% \par S&P 500              1929.8   8.53     0.44% \par Japan: Nikkei 225    15915.8  -136.26  -0.85% \par Hang Seng            19192.5  -222.33  -1.15% \par Shanghai Composite   2928.9   25.56    0.88% \par S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2   Sensex       23088.9  -321.25  -1.37% \par Australia: S&P/ASX   4875     -104.6   -2.10% \par UK: FTSE 100         5867.18  -95.13   -1.60% \par  \par  \par                     PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year         0/32       0.766 \par U.S. 5 Year          -3/32     1.219 \par U.S. 10 Year         -7/32     1.748 \par Australia 10 Year    -12/32    2.447 \par China 10 Year       -1         2.95 \par India 10 Year        -4/32     7.824 \par Japan 10 Year       17/32      -0.05 \par German 10 Year       7/32      0.163 \par  \par  \par                           LAST(MID)  CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)     0.7197     0.0001 \par Yen (USD/JPY)             112.2      0.03 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)   1232.53    -0.18 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)    6.5316     0.0033 \par Euro (EUR/USD)            1.1016     0.0001 \par WSJ Dollar Index          89.45      0 \par  \par  \par               LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil     32.22   0.35    1.10% \par Brent Crude   35.07   1.1     3.24% \par Gold          1229.4  6.8     0.56% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SNAPSHOT:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose, recovering from earlier losses as oil prices turned higher. Prices of \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt gave back gains as oil and equities rose. The Japanese yen ended the session little changed after rising earlier against the dollar and reaching its strongest level versus the euro in nearly three years. Gold prices rose as sharp drops in oil and equities sent investors into the safe-haven metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPENING CALL:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jawboning in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets is called jawboning because it normally lacks subtlety. Recent likely attempts by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at alerting the markets about its rising concern over the level of the Aussie dollar have had mixed results. Indeed some of it has been missed entirely. RBA Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glenn Stevens\f28 \uc2 \f2  told parliament earlier this month that the debate on interest rates was about remaining steady or cutting. "I think, it is pretty unlikely we are going to be raising rates any time soon. The question will be should we sit or go down some more," Stevens told parliament's economics committee. The blunt reminder about the risk of cuts was mostly ignored. There was also a line from Stevens that: "The Australian dollar is about where it was when we last met with the committee, though it is noteworthy that commodity prices are lower than they were then." These comments, along with more direct remarks Friday by RBA board member John Edwards that a lower \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be good, are likely to be linked. While not yet a first order issue for the RBA, a lower \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be welcomed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose, recovering from earlier losses as oil prices turned higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rebound was broad-based, with nearly every S&P 500 sector eking out a gain. Stocks sensitive to changes in commodity prices, including shares of materials and energy companies, rose the most.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, traders noted that trading volumes have slumped in recent sessions, which could exaggerate some of the market moves. The last three trading sessions ranked among the lowest volume days of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For most of the session, financial stocks weighed on the broader indexes. Financial stocks in the S&P 500 have slumped 13% so far in 2016, notching the biggest sector decline. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares have dropped 28% so far this year, and shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  have declined 26%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selloff in bank stocks underscores the lingering worries about the health of the U.S. economy, the prospect of interest rates staying lower for longer and the fallout from months of declining oil prices. Highlighting the oil risk, \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  said this week it expects to further build reserves related to potential energy losses in the first quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks in Shanghai closed slightly higher Wednesday, as expectations built that Chinese authorities will soon announce a trial program aimed at improving state-owned enterprises. Most other Asian markets, however, fell under the pressure of lower oil prices.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese yen ended the session little changed after rising earlier against the dollar and reaching its strongest level versus the euro in nearly three years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The buying in the yen was the strongest during the morning session, when a decline in the prices of crude oil and U.S. stocks stoked demand for haven assets. The yen gradually gave up its strength in the afternoon as equity and oil prices recovered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen has climbed more than 6% against the dollar since the start of the year as worries over the global economic outlook has driven many investors to cut holdings of riskier assets and preserve capital. The dollar fell to Yen110.985 on Feb. 11, its lowest intraday level since October 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger yen hurts the competitiveness of Japanese exports and raises the risk that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to curb the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The British pound fell below $1.40 for the first time since 2009 as investors continued to fret over the uncertainty surrounding the U.K.'s future in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound also fell to the lowest level since December 2014 against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound has been down since the start of the week as investors are concerned about the potential economic fallout if the U.K. exits the union. The U.K. referendum is set in June, and analysts say the uncertainty would continue to weigh on the British \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are using the pound as a vehicle to hedge their investments in the U.K.--from stocks, bonds to real estate in case the U.K. decides to bolt from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  , known as Brexit, traders said. Foreign investment provides an important source of funding for the U.K.'s trade shortfall, so any large cutback would hurt the U.K.'s growth outlook and make the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less appealing to buy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The uncertainty may push the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  to be more cautious in raising interest rates, another factor that would make the pound less attractive, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also shed exposure to the euro on concerns about potential ripples from the U.K.'s possible exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  , given the close economic and trade links between the eurozone and the U.K.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt waxed and waned with the fortunes of the oil market, once again illustrating crude oil's power to drive prices in other markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note ended unchanged from Tuesday's close. But over the course of the trading session, Treasurys rose and fell opposite oil and stocks, which have also been affected by the deep selloff in crude prices. Yields fall as bond prices rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. bonds and other so-called safe-haven assets have been trading in an inverse relationship to stocks and oil, as the financial markets turmoil since the start of the year prompted investors to toggle between risk-on and risk-off positioning. Yields on Treasurys have fallen sharply this year as investors rushed into the market as a haven from the selloffs in global stocks, oil and other risk assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For most of this year, and the past couple days once again, Treasurys are just mirroring risk assets overall, and equities and oil prices in particular," said John Canavan, market analyst at Stone and McCarthy Research Associates in Princeton, N.J. "That seems to be what we see today."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bearish U.S. housing data also lent an early tilt to Treasurys, as the Commerce Department said new home sales fell 9.2% in January, well below expectations, to its slowest pace since October. And an auction of 5-year Treasury notes in midafternoon demonstrated strong demand for safe-haven government debt, with the sale oversubscribed by more than 2-to-1 and so-called indirect buyers, a measure of investment interest from foreign buyers, taking more than two-thirds of the issue, compared with an average of 56%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMODITIES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices erased losses to settle higher Wednesday after U.S. inventory data showed that crude-oil stockpiles rose to another record high last week but supplies of refined products such as gasoline fell.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record-high inventories of crude oil and refined products around the world have weighed on the oil market in recent months and helped send prices to 13-year lows earlier this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude inventories rose by 3.5 million barrels in the week ended Feb. 19 to 507.6 million barrels, a weekly record high, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Energy Information Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Wednesday. In monthly data, which don't line up exactly with weekly data, inventories last exceeded 500 million barrels in 1930.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices might have risen after the report came out because a stockpile increase was expected, analysts said. Crude stockpiles typically rise in late February and early March as refiners process less crude into gasoline and other fuels while performing seasonal maintenance. Refineries ran at 87.3% of capacity last week, down from 88.3% the prior week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gasoline futures rose as the EIA data showed an unexpectedly large stockpile decline of 2.2 million barrels last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for gasoline and other refined products rose in the week, the EIA said. Dow Chemical CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Andrew Liveris\f28 \uc2 \f2  said at an energy conference in Houston that low oil prices are fueling more demand, which will ultimately be good for the global energy sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the precious metals market, gold prices rose, as volatile stock markets and fears that Britain will leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  sent investors into the safe haven metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S HEADLINES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HP Earnings Fall Amid Weakness in Printers, PCs\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HP\f28 \uc2 \f2  met Wall Street's modest expectations in its first quarter as an independent company, despite signs that weak conditions in printing and personal computers aren't improving quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Widens Foreign Access to Bond Purchases\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Feb 2016 17:33 ET \par Asian Markets Briefing: U.S. Stocks Recover as -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would make it easier for foreign institutional investors to buy bonds issued in the country, a fresh step intended to drive demand for the yuan among global insurers, endowments and pension funds as its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy faces increased global scrutiny.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  Says G-20 Countries Need to Ramp Up Government Spending\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The world's largest economies should agree to a coordinated increase in government spending to counter the growing risk of a deeper global economic slowdown, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Target Swings to a Profit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Target swung to a profit in the fourth quarter, helped by a gain on the sale of its pharmacy and clinic businesses and a jump in digital sales as the retailer continues to revamp its merchandise and attract more customers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. New-Home Sales Fell Sharply in January\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sales of newly built U.S. homes declined last month, a sign the housing market's recovery remains uneven.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed's Lacker Says Prospects for Rate Increases This Year 'the Logical One'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Continued economic growth makes it appropriate for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to continue raising interest rates this year, Richmond Fed President Jeffrey Lacker said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weidmann Warns \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  Against Further Easing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council member \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jens Weidmann\f28 \uc2 \f2  expressed reservations about further expansionary monetary policy to combat very low rates of inflation in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina, Hedge Funds Move Closer to Reaching Deal\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina and hedge funds that are owed $1.8 billion over the country's defaulted debt moved closer to reaching a deal that would pay those hedge funds $5 billion, according to a person close to the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Salesforce.com\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Revenue, Quarterly Outlook Top Expectations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Salesforce.com Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  . said its revenue rose 25% in the latest quarter, and it boosted its revenue view for the current fiscal year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chesapeake Energy Posts Deep Loss on Big Write-Down\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chesapeake Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  swung to a heavy loss in its fourth quarter amid tumbling energy prices as the U.S. shale driller took a write-down on some of its energy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Uber Competitor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Didi Kuaidi\f28 \uc2 \f2  Planning to Raise Around $1 Billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wanda Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  Seeks $1.5 Billion for Piece of Movie Business\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Honeywell\f28 \uc2 \f2  Seeking Buyer for Building-Solutions Business\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Why a Global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Accord Won't Happen\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Six of 13 IRS-Approved Online Tax-Prep Services Fail Cybersecurity Test\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S CALENDAR:\f28 \par \p