\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,197 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2016\par 02:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Australia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Watchful Of Global Mkt Turbulence \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains watchful of turbulence in global markets, saying Tuesday it has flexibility to lower interest rates further if the economy requires it. (published 0032 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Japan Finance Minister: Additional Fiscal Stimulus Not on Table \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said Tuesday that he isn't considering any additional fiscal stimulus measures, playing down economic weakness over the winter months as a phenomenon caused by unusually warm weather. (published 0007 GMT) \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Prime Minister Abe Adviser Hamada Says Finance Ministry Should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An adviser to Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  should \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to curb volatility in the yen, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rebound since last week is unusually sharp. (published 2348 GMT Monday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par China Huarong Plans to Raise up to CNY10 Billion Via Bond Issuance in China \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Huarong Asset Management Co. is seeking to raise up to 10 billion yuan via a bond issuance in China for working capital and business development. (published 0001 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Canada's Banking Regulator Takes Control of Domestic Maple Bank Branch \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's top banking regulator announced Monday it has taken "permanent control" of the assets of Frankfurt-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Maple Bank GmbH\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Canadian branch. (published 2237 GMT Monday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par New Zealand Retail Sales Growth Slows in 4Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in retail spending slowed in New Zealand in the fourth quarter, as a fall in motor vehicle sales offset gains in hardware supplies. (published 2206 GMT Monday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Central European Leaders Call for Solution on Migration -- Update \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central European leaders said Monday they were ready to support Macedonia and Bulgaria in guarding their borders with Greece to stem the flow of migrants making their way across Europe. (published 2010 GMT Monday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  \par China's Reserves Plunge While Stresses Mount -- Heard on the Street \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's supposedly unassailable war chest of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves is looking less and less unassailable. (published 2322 GMT Monday) \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 Feb 2016 22:00 ET \par News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Malaysia 4th Quarter GDP Likely Grew 4.3%, WSJ Poll Shows \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's economic growth likely slowed in the fourth quarter as declining demand from its largest trading partner China, weaker energy prices and softer domestic demand took a toll on the Southeast Asian nation's economy. (published 0239 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par China Premier Vows to Guard Against Financial Risks \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Premier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Keqiang\f28 \uc2 \f2  pledged to step up efforts to stay vigilant against risks in the financial market, while reiterating that the country's economic fundamentals remain solid due to steady employment. (published 0206 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Australia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Watchful Of Global Mkt Turbulence \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains watchful of turbulence in global markets, saying Tuesday it has flexibility to lower interest rates further if the economy requires it. (published 0032 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Japan Finance Minister: Additional Fiscal Stimulus Not on Table \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said Tuesday that he isn't considering any additional fiscal stimulus measures, playing down economic weakness over the winter months as a phenomenon caused by unusually warm weather. (published 0007 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBNZ 1Q Survey: 12-Month Inflation Expectations 1.09% Vs. 1.51% in Prior Survey \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 First quarter inflation expectations have fallen, according to a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  survey released Tuesday. (published 0206 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par European Countries Weigh New Barriers Along Migrant Path \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several small countries in Europe are banding together in their most concerted effort yet to set new roadblocks along the path that has funneled a million refugees and migrants across the Continent. (published 0113 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par China Huarong Plans to Raise up to CNY10 Billion Via Bond Issuance in China \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Huarong Asset Management Co. is seeking to raise up to 10 billion yuan via a bond issuance in China for working capital and business development. (published 0001 GMT) \f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Feb 2016 01:00 ET \par News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Bank of Korea Keeps Base Rate Unchanged at 1.50% -- Update \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left its policy rate unchanged Tuesday, as expected, despite growing pressure to ease policy and help the economy. (published 0544 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par New Credit Extended by Chinese Banks Hit New Monthly High \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New credit extended by Chinese banks hit a new monthly high in January, as lenders front-loaded lending at the beginning of the year while Chinese companies moved to cut foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt and increase local borrowing amid the weakening yuan. (published 0527 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Brussels Already Is Changing Europe's Tax Laws \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Large corporations have been keenly aware in recent years of mounting public pressure for changes to the international tax system. (published 0530 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Malaysia 4th Quarter GDP Likely Grew 4.3%, WSJ Poll Shows \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's economic growth likely slowed in the fourth quarter as declining demand from its largest trading partner China, weaker energy prices and softer domestic demand took a toll on the Southeast Asian nation's economy. (published 0239 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par China Premier Vows to Guard Against Financial Risks \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Premier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Keqiang\f28 \uc2 \f2  pledged to step up efforts to stay vigilant against risks in the financial market, while reiterating that the country's economic fundamentals remain solid due to steady employment. (published 0206 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Australia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Watchful Of Global Mkt Turbulence \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains watchful of turbulence in global markets, saying Tuesday it has flexibility to lower interest rates further if the economy requires it. (published 0032 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Japan Finance Minister: Additional Fiscal Stimulus Not on Table \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said Tuesday that he isn't considering any additional fiscal stimulus measures, playing down economic weakness over the winter months as a phenomenon caused by unusually warm weather. (published 0007 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par China Jan New Yuan Loans Hit Monthly Record High \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese financial institutions issued 2.51 trillion yuan worth of new yuan loans in January, the highest monthly level on record, data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  showed Tuesday. (published 0216 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBNZ 1Q Survey: 12-Month Inflation Expectations 1.09% Vs. 1.51% in Prior Survey \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 First quarter inflation expectations have fallen, according to a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  survey released Tuesday. (published 0206 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par European Countries Weigh New Barriers Along Migrant Path \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several small countries in Europe are banding together in their most concerted effort yet to set new roadblocks along the path that has funneled a million refugees and migrants across the Continent. (published 0113 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Remittances to Latin America, Caribbean Hit $68.3 billion in 2015 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The amount 