\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Prime Minister Abe Adviser Hamada Says Finance Ministry Should  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in Curre\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 258 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2016\par 01:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO--An adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb volatility in the yen, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rebound since last week is unusually sharp.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's recent appreciation is abnormal from statistical perspectives" and appears driven by hedge funds, said Koichi Hamada, one of Mr. Abe's close economic advisers and a Yale University professor emeritus, in a recent interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "Accordingly, I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets, not to guide the yen in any particular direction but to prevent high volatility by sending a warning to speculators."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hamada emphasized that the idea was his "personal opinion" and that it is up to the finance ministry to decide whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The ministry sets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would supplement aggressive monetary easing being implemented by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , Mr. Hamada said.\par \par \uc2 The latest remarks by Mr. Hamada signaled his change of heart since late last week, when he told The Wall Street Journal that he wasn't so worried about the yen. But Mr. Hamada maintained that Japan's economic fundamentals are solid and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easing measures are exerting their intended effects on domestic financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-02-16 0046GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160216ec2g00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
