\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Stocks Rise, Tracking Nikkei Higher -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2016\par 05:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong stocks jumped Monday morning, tracking steady gains in Japanese shares instead of the sell-off in Chinese equities. The Hang Seng Index shot up 2.7% to 18,820 while the Nikkei rose 6.2%. Chinese markets remained relatively calm despite weaker-than-expected trade data released in the morning, with the Shanghai Composite Index paring losses to close at the midday break down 1.6%. Rebounds in gaming and oil stocks helped push the Hong Kong benchmark higher, as investors digested China's weak trade figures, as they tend to be distorted by production slowing down before the public holiday. "Today's trade figures make for gloomy reading and point to a shaky start to the year," says research firm \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . "That said, apparent distortions due to Lunar New Year and capital flows leave us with some hope that things may not be quite as bad as they look." (dominique.fong@wsj.com; Twitter: @dominiquefong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A recovery in Thai growth in 2015 still leaves economists divided over whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates in 2016. \uc2 The Bank of Thailand\uc2  has kept the benchmark interest rate at 1.5% since April while standing ready to utilize all available tools to support the economy, which grew 2.8% in 2015 and is expected to grow 2.8%-3.8% in 2016. "We think the risk of a [rate] cut would rise if domestic growth, especially public spending, shows signs of moderating in 1Q," says Rahul Bajoria, an economist at Barclays Bank. "If the baht appreciates materially, we would not rule out the possibility of rate cuts aimed at weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." But, ANZ Research is confident that Thailand's policy rate is now at its terminal low, saying "the recent firm recovery in Thailand's domestic activity in particular--if sustained--will allow \uc2 the Bank of Thailand\uc2  to keep its gunpowder dry." (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0446 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Inter-Korean tensions in the aftermath of North Korea's recent rocket launch and nuclear test raise the chances for miscalculation as the two sides "are heading for a new crisis," says Yoel Sano, London-based head of political risk at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BMI Research\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in a note to clients on Friday. Sano argues that "the scene is now set for another spring crisis this year," thanks to the largest-ever annual joint military exercises between the U.S. and South Korea in the spring, as well as a parliamentary election in South Korea in April and a once-in-a-generation party congress in North Korea in May. "The two Koreas could end up fighting a major battle at sea or on land near their border, leading to hundreds of deaths. At that stage, they would be perilously close to all-out war," Sano writes. (jonathan.cheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @jchengwsj) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0439 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Copper prices rose sharply following the release of China's strong import data for the industrial metal for January. China's copper and copper product imports in January rose 7.3% on year to 440,000 metric tons, while iron-ore imports also rose 4.6% during the same comparative period. China is the world's largest consumer for copper as well as steel-making raw material iron-ore. Three-month copper futures price on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.8% to $4,582/ton, zinc futures prices rose by 0.10% to $1,707.50/ton and aluminum futures price rose 0.7% to $1,511/ton. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0438 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] New Zealand's retail sales likely rose again in the fourth quarter, according to a Wall Street Journal survey of economists. The volume of retail sales likely rose 1.4% on-quarter in the three months to the end of December, a median forecast of 10 economists surveyed shows. The value of retail sales likely gained 1.3% for the quarter, the poll indicates. Growing retail sales suggests some buoyancy in domestic demand despite recent weak inflation data. (kate.geenty@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0437 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is now losing the most important force behind its uptrend to date: strength in the U.S. economy. So says Deutsche chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka in a note. "We've decided to abandon the USD/JPY bullish outlook we've been keeping since 2012, and instead will watch the possibly (of the pair) to move in a 105-110 range over the next several months," says Tanaka. The U.S. economy is showing signs of a slowdown, which has raised speculation about a possible recession, says Tanaka. "The USD/JPY may show a technical rebound from excessive response last week, but selling on recovery will likely predominate," says Tanaka. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0433 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China was the leader for Asian financial markets earlier this year, but that distinction now belongs to Japan, it seems. After a week-long break for the Lunar New Year holiday, during which Japan's stock market and the yen made huge moves that reverberated across the region, gyrations in Chinese markets seem less relevant. Today's stellar 6.1% rally of Japan's Nikkei, which has reduced by more than half last week's 11.1% collapse, is egging on other stock markets in Asia. Singapore's Straits Times Index is up 2.5%, while South Korea's Kospi is up 1.4%. The driver for the Nikkei may be optimism for additional monetary policy stimulus by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , following a worse-than-expected fourth quarter gross domestic product data released earlier. For the same reason, USD/JPY has rallied. Bets on BOJ action to weaken the yen are back in vogue, pushing USD/JPY well away from its 15-month low notched Thursday. The rise of USD/JPY is lifting most other USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs--which are ignoring the downturn in USD/CNY that has been labelled as a catch-up move to last week's dollar selloff. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0433 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's economic recovery in 2016 may be setting off on a firm footing but the process may be modest under the pressure of both external and domestic risks. The country's gross domestic product grew 2.8% on year in 2015, compared with revised growth of 0.8% in 2014. Rahul Bajoria, an economist at Barclays Bank, believes Thailand's growth this year will continue seeing downside risks from poor trade and drought conditions. Some economists also point to domestic politics and debt levels as other negative factors. "We doubt growth will pick up strongly this year. Thailand's unstable political situation will continue to hang over the outlook for private investment, while high levels of household debt will keep private consumption growth subdued," says Krystal Tan, Asia economist at Capital Economics, in a note. "Meanwhile, lackluster external demand and declining competitiveness will weigh on goods exports."(nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0433 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CNY has extended even lower, breaking the 6.5000 round-figure trading barrier, in a continuation of the catch-up move after a week-long holiday. The yuan is now 1.2% stronger versus the U.S. dollar compared to its last close, after clocking a 0.9% gain earlier. The fall of USD/CNY is creating a drag on USD/Asia pairs--which were advancing on the back of USD/JPY rallying due to bets of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or policy action by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Traders are wagering that the BOJ may act again soon, after a worse-than-expected fourth quarter gross domestic product data released earlier. For the same reason, the benchmark Nikkei stock index has soared 6.5%. Although the downturn in USD/CNY has been labelled as a catch-up move to last week's dollar selloff, if the move accelerates, it could reduce Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' reliance on the yen as a lead indicator--a correlation that was strengthened while China was closed last week. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0431 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Yen appreciation at the rate of one yen per U.S. dollar or euro will push down operating profits of Japanese oil and coal companies, marine transporters, carmakers, and precision equipment and machine makers by more than 1%, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley chief strategist Chisato Haganuma. On the contrary, a stronger yen will likely buoy profits of pulp and paper companies and air carriers, he adds. "If the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trades around 110 yen against the dollar, concerns will likely spread that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations will reduce corporate profits for fiscal 2016," he says. The dollar is currently around 113.90 yen. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0428 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  reaffirms its projection that Japan's economy will shrink for a second straight quarter in the January-March period, albeit at a modest annualized rate of 0.1%. Leap-year effects may work in favor of growth in the first quarter, but because of "negative base effects in private consumption ... we look for a slight contraction," writes the company's chief Japan economist Kyohei Morita. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's projections of 1.1% growth for the year ending March and a 1.5% rise for the following year are already faced with a "downside risk," he says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  tips the BOJ to ease further at a planned March 14-15 policy meeting, provided that USD/JPY will stay below Y115. Pair last trades at 113.85.(takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0412 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY extends earlier gains at 113.89 from 113.29 late Friday in New York on upbeat U.S. retail sales, helping push the Nikkei up 5.1% midday. "Oil prices recovered slightly and shares of banking sector in the U.S. and Europe stopped falling," says Koji Fukaya, chief executive of FPG Securities. "If the trend continues in this direction, we will be able to avoid another selloff," he adds. But Mr. Fukaya says it may take much longer or at least 3 months to see a full-sale rebound in risk sentiment. "For now, we would like to watch closely policy-makers move and economic indicators" until early next month including G-20 meeting and U.S. jobs data. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 14, 2016 23:57 ET (04:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160215ec2f00098\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
