\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Ends Week Down 11%, Yen Rallies -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Guy   \par \uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2016\par 09:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 All eyes in Asian trading were firmly fixed on the bloodbath that was Japanese stocks.\par \par \uc2 It was a brutal day for traders in Tokyo as the Nikkei 225 Index tumbled 4.8% as investors desperate for safe havens jettisoned stocks and bought the yen. Japan's benchmark index finished down 11% for the week - its biggest weekly decline since October 2008 - and is now down 21% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan governor Haruhiko Kuroda's embrace of negative interest rates has turned into a monetary policy faceplant as the yen hovers around 112 against the U.S. dollar, its strongest level since October 2014. So much for negative rates taking the steam out of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has rallied from 120 against the greenback at the start of the month.\par \par \uc2 The stronger yen poses a threat to the efforts by Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  to revive Japan's lackluster economy and beat back deflation. It's these concerns that has \uc2 ABN Amro\uc2  thinking \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and give speculators a whack across the knuckles:\par \par \uc2 ...the pressure is now on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to warn speculators that the direction in the yen is not a one-way bet. Indeed, data from CFTC show that speculators' net long futures position in the yen have surged in the past few months to the highest levels since the MoF last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October 2011.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the yen's volatility has more than doubled since December last year. Indeed, Finance Minister Aso and his deputy have commented that recent moves in the yen have been 'rough' and are watching for speculative moves. However, financial markets have continued to push USD/JPY lower as they test when \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials would pull the trigger to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. If there is no \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and/or improvement in investor sentiment, further downside in USD/JPY towards 110 in the coming weeks cannot be ruled out. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 12, 2016 03:21 ET (08:21 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160212ec2c00130\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
