\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Lost 11% This Week, Worst Since October 2008 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2016\par 07:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan's stock benchmark Nikkei lost 11% this week, the biggest weekly percentage loss since October 2008, hit by concerns about health of the U.S. economy and banks globally. The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's cautious stance on further rate increases raised doubts about the economy and led to the yen's sharp rise against the dollar. The dollar fell sharply to 110.99 on Thursday, the lowest level since Oct. 31 2014, the day when the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  expanded its easing program. The USD/JPY is at 112.11. Because of slower global growth, ultra-low interest rate environment, bad loans from commodity-related lending, there are some, not dominant, concerns that there may be a banking crisis. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 760.78 points, or 4.8%, to 14952.61 on Friday. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan has made some, but not enough, structural reforms while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  was stimulating the economy through monetary easing, says Fumio Nakakubo, chief investment officer for Japan at UBS's wealth management division. Relaxation of visa rules boosted the number of foreign, particularly Chinese, visitors to Japan. But their spending is small compared to Japan's total economy and could slow going forward because of China's economic troubles, he says. Some people may be making a little more money, but that isn't likely to offset negative effects from declining population to the entire economy. Meanwhile, the government so far failed to implement serious cuts into medical and social welfare spending that are ballooning. "The reforms in social welfare spending was the single most important thing they should have done when the economy was good," he says. "If they'd have to start that now, when financial markets are as volatile as today, the consequence would be terrible." (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0611 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Defense industry stocks in Seoul are bucking the trend, rising sharply, in a rally triggered by escalating geopolitical tensions between the two Koreas. "The rise in military tension in East Asia is will likely lead to increased national spending by South Korea, China, Japan and neighboring countries on defense," T.H. Kang, an analyst with KTB Investment & Securities in Seoul wrote in a research note. Seoul and Washington have initiated talks over the deployment of an advanced missile defense shield in South Korea in response to a long-range rocket launch by North Korea. Among Kosdaq-listed companies, maker of attack detection systems Victek (065450.KQ) is up 7.2% at 3,885 won following a 30.0% surge Thursday; manufacturer of tanks and armored vehicles Formetal (119500.KQ) is up 29.9% at 4,370 won. The Kosdaq is off 6.2%. Kang notes global defense heavyweights like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lockheed Martin\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LMT), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Northrop Grumman (NOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thales S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (HO.FR) are also likely to benefit from the latest string of provocative moves by North Korea.(min-jeong.lee@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0555 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A bipartisan bill by U.S. Senate Commerce Committee leaders would help encourage the making of more spectrum--including government-controlled spectrum--available for next-generation 5G wireless broadband. That's a potentially important development for telecom companies. But the bill unveiled Thursday night by Commerce Committee chairman John Thune (R., S.D.) and ranking member Bill Nelson (D., Fla.) also requires a lot of future studies to reach its goal. The Mobile Now Act would direct federal agencies to evaluate a wide range of spectrum bands for possible commercial use, committee officials said. The outlook for the legislation in the House is uncertain. (john.mckinnon@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0551 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  will increase lending to Indonesia, in another sign that establishment of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in which Indonesia is a founding member, has sparked something of a turf war by the two main multilateral lenders of the region (the ADB and \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). The ADB said it will increase annual funding assistance for Indonesia to $2 billion over the next five years, up from $740 million annually between 2010 and 2014. It comes after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  last year offered as much as $11 billion to Jakarta over the next three to four years. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0533 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The sharp selloff in Japanese stocks stems from a combination of factors, including China's economic slowdown, falling crude-oil prices and concerns about the health of European banks, says Fumio Nakakubo, chief investment officer for Japan at UBS's wealth management division. He says these factors are causing global market weakness, leading to an appreciation of the yen, a haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "In Japan's case, the impact on earnings from the yen strength is very big," he says. However, despite market worries about the global banking sector, systemic risk is minimized because lenders today are well capitalized, he says. The Nikkei 225 index is down 3.0% at 15,248.79, the lowest level since October 2014, as the yen surged to the highest level in more than a year against the U.S. dollar.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0528 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee has depreciated again Friday, surpassing yesterday's more-than-two-year low of 68.35 to reach a new trough of 68.44 to the U.S. dollar, as the benchmark domestic stock index slips 0.6% after Thursday's 3.4% decline. The rupee has crumbled to its weakest level since September 2013, as risk aversion hit Indian banking stocks, due to concerns revolving around the magnitude of the industry's non-performing loans. With India stocks having lost 12.7% after only six weeks into 2016, the USD/INR pair is being driven higher due to fear of foreign investment exodus. \f28 \uc2 \f2 DBS Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note Friday highlighted that foreign equity outflows have totalled $1.8 billion this year, versus 2015's $3.2 billion. The correlation between the Sensex - India's benchmark stock index - and the rupee is positive, as pointed out by DBS. Therefore as India stocks fall, so will the rupee. USD/INR is now 68.40 from its Thursday close of 68.29. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0527 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500470.BY) slips 4.3% to 215.35 rupees after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrades its credit ratings. The sharp fall in international steel prices due to excessive exports from China and other countries has triggered the rating downgrade, the steelmaker says in a exchange filing. Its long term corporate credit rating has been revised to Ba3 from Ba1. Last month, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  also downgraded the steel maker's corporate credit rating. The Indian government has already imposed a minimum import price on certain steel products to protect the domestic steel industry. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0516 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian outsourcing firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (WIT) is up 1.98% on Friday following news Thursday that it has agreed to acquire U.S. firm HealthPlan Services for $460 million. The company, which provides software to insurance firms, should help \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro\f28 \uc2 \f2  win business generated by the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act, commonly known as Obama Care, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a statement. Obama Care offers Americans the ability to shop for health coverage on new insurance exchanges, and the Tampa, Florida-based company offers software to health insurers to bid for business on such platforms. The deal is \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro\f28 \uc2 \f2 's second-biggest acquisition following its purchase of U.S.-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Infocrossing Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . for about $600 million in 2007 in what was one of the largest overseas purchases by an Indian software maker at the time. (newley.purnell@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @Newley) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0502 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's banking stocks are in the red for the fifth straight session, as investors worry that mounting nonperforming assets are weakening the financial system of Asia's third-largest economy. The S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bankex index, which tracks shares of banks, has lost 7% so far this week, as lenders reported falling profit for the third quarter. RBI Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's attempt to calm the market Thursday appears to have failed. He said only "a small minority of public-sector banks" might face a breach of the minimum capital requirement, but investors sent the Bankex down Friday morning by 1.05% in an overall up market. (gabriele.parussini@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0457 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The South Korea won is extending its losses to the U.S. dollar, led by a weakening of the Japanese yen amid speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be about to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Haruhiko Kuroda, after a meeting with Prime Minister Abe Friday, said that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is watching the yen closely. Though there was no mention of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the safe haven yen, which strengthened as much as 5.3% this week due to risk aversion, the news of a possible emergency BOJ meeting has stirred optimism for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action. If the BOJ unleashes its reserves to buy U.S. dollars, so as to weaken the yen, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the South Korea won, and even the Singapore dollar, will be dragged along. USD/KRW is now 1,210.5 from Thursday's close of 1,202.5, representing a 0.7% weakening of the won versus the dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Feb 2016 01:36 ET \par Nikkei Lost 11% This Week, Worst Since October -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0451 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  is partly responsible for the latest market gyrations, says Koji Toda, chief fund manager at Resona Bank's asset management division, which manages about 17 trillion yen ($151 billion). He says the BOJ's recent adoption of negative interest rates is raising concerns about the profitability of banks, triggering selling of their shares. "Banks are the cornerstone of the Japanese economy. It's hard to imagine the broader market indices going up on this." He says the BOJ policy moves, which tend to surprise markets, are a source of volatility, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't explained fully how the measure might help the economy. "They should explain. Because they haven't done so fully, immediate negatives are highlighted. The policy action is perhaps appropriate, but the BOJ should be blamed for its lack of full explanation. The BOJ carries great responsibility this time." The Nikkei is down 10% so far this week. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 12, 2016 01:36 ET (06:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160212ec2c000ie\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs